"use strict";(self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website=self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website||[]).push([["85469"],{93846(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"captcha-for-login-pages","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/captcha-for-login-pages","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","title":"Comparaison des fournisseurs de CAPTCHA pour les pages de connexion : reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest et Alibaba Cloud dans Casdoor","description":"Comparez reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha et le captcha int\xe9gr\xe9 pour les pages de connexion et d\'inscription, et utilisez-les dans Casdoor.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAPTCHA","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"reCAPTCHA","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/re-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"hCaptcha","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/h-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"Turnstile","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/turnstile"},{"inline":true,"label":"GeeTest","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/gee-test"},{"inline":true,"label":"Alibaba Cloud","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/alibaba-cloud"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":8.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"captcha-for-login-pages","title":"Comparaison des fournisseurs de CAPTCHA pour les pages de connexion : reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest et Alibaba Cloud dans Casdoor","description":"Comparez reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha et le captcha int\xe9gr\xe9 pour les pages de connexion et d\'inscription, et utilisez-les dans Casdoor.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CAPTCHA","reCAPTCHA","hCaptcha","Turnstile","GeeTest","Alibaba Cloud","Security"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","permalink":"/fr/blog/customize-login-page"}},"content":"Pour la plupart des pages de connexion et d\'inscription sur Internet mondial, Cloudflare Turnstile est le choix le plus simple : le plan gratuit n\'a pas de limite sur les d\xe9fis, et son mode par d\xe9faut n\'affiche une case \xe0 cocher qu\'aux visiteurs risqu\xe9s. Si beaucoup de vos utilisateurs sont en Chine continentale, utilisez plut\xf4t Alibaba Cloud Captcha ou GeeTest, car Cloudflare indique que Turnstile n\'est pas pris en charge l\xe0-bas. Si vous avez besoin que le captcha fonctionne sans aucun service externe, utilisez le captcha d\'image int\xe9gr\xe9 de Casdoor. Casdoor prend en charge tous ces fournisseurs, ainsi que Google reCAPTCHA (v2 et v3) et hCaptcha, en tant que [fournisseurs de captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/overview), et vous choisissez quand le captcha appara\xeet par application.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## La comparaison {#the-comparison}\\n\\nLes prix et la disponibilit\xe9 ci-dessous ont \xe9t\xe9 v\xe9rifi\xe9s sur les sites officiels de chaque fournisseur le 2026-11-02. Lorsqu\'un fournisseur ne publie pas de chiffre, le tableau l\'indique au lieu de faire des suppositions.\\n\\n| Fournisseur | Ce que voit l\'utilisateur | Mode invisible | Donn\xe9es et confidentialit\xe9 | Niveau gratuit et tarification | Chine continentale | Type Casdoor |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Casdoor par d\xe9faut | Saisit cinq chiffres depuis une image | Non | G\xe9n\xe9r\xe9 et v\xe9rifi\xe9 par votre serveur Casdoor ; aucun tiers | Gratuit, inclus dans Casdoor | Fonctionne, aucun appel externe | `Default` |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v2 | Case \xe0 cocher ; certains utilisateurs obtiennent un d\xe9fi d\'image | v2 a aussi une variante invisible qui ne d\xe9fie que le trafic suspect | Google ; d\xe9finit le cookie `_GRECAPTCHA` pour l\'analyse des risques | Tier Essentials gratuit jusqu\'\xe0 10 000 \xe9valuations par mois par organisation ; Premium est \xe0 8 $ fixe jusqu\'\xe0 100 000, puis 1 $ par 1 000 | Google dit d\'utiliser `www.recaptcha.net` lorsque `www.google.com` n\'est pas accessible | `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2` (case \xe0 cocher) |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v3 | Rien ; retourne un score | Oui | Identique \xe0 v2 | Identique \xe0 v2 | Identique \xe0 v2 | `reCAPTCHA v3` |\\n| hCaptcha | Case \xe0 cocher avec d\xe9fis d\'image sur le plan gratuit | Mode passif sur Pro (\\"99,9 % passif\\") et un mode sans-CAPTCHA sur Enterprise | Indique que le plan gratuit est conforme au RGPD, CCPA, LGPD et PIPL ; certifi\xe9 selon le cadre de protection des donn\xe9es UE-\xc9tats-Unis | Basic est gratuit ; Pro est \xe0 139 $ par mois (ou 99 $ par mois factur\xe9s annuellement) avec 100 000 \xe9valuations, puis 0,99 $ par 1 000 ; Enterprise via les ventes | Non document\xe9 par hCaptcha | `hCaptcha` |\\n| Cloudflare Turnstile | Le mode g\xe9r\xe9 affiche une case \xe0 cocher uniquement si n\xe9cessaire | Modes de widget non interactifs et invisibles | Cloudflare indique qu\'il ne traite que les donn\xe9es strictement n\xe9cessaires et ne lit pas les entr\xe9es de formulaire ; aucune proxy Cloudflare requis | Plan gratuit : d\xe9fis illimit\xe9s, jusqu\'\xe0 20 widgets ; Enterprise via les ventes | Cloudflare : \\"non pris en charge en Chine continentale\\" | `Cloudflare Turnstile` |\\n| GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 | Un clic, puzzle glissant, clic sur texte, jeu de correspondance | Non r\xe9pertori\xe9 sur la page produit v4 | Fournisseur chinois ; politique de confidentialit\xe9 sp\xe9cifique au produit | Pas de liste de prix publique ; essai gratuit de 30 jours, puis ventes | Fournisseur chinois | `GEETEST` |\\n| Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 | Un clic, curseur, puzzle, restauration d\'image | Mode sans trace dans les \xe9ditions Standard et Enterprise | Fonctionne sur Alibaba Cloud | Paiement \xe0 l\'usage : 0,005 CNY par appel en Chine continentale, 0,007 CNY ailleurs ; \xe9ditions par abonnement et forfaits de ressources \xe9galement disponibles | Oui ; Casdoor v\xe9rifie contre le point de terminaison de la r\xe9gion de Shanghai | `Aliyun Captcha` |\\n\\nDeux notes sur les lignes reCAPTCHA. Google a d\xe9plac\xe9 reCAPTCHA dans Google Cloud Fraud Defense avec les niveaux Essentials, Premium et Enterprise, et l\'allocation gratuite de 10 000 \xe9valuations est mutualis\xe9e sur toute l\'organisation Google Cloud. Casdoor communique avec reCAPTCHA via une cl\xe9 de site et une cl\xe9 secr\xe8te \xe0 travers le point de terminaison standard `siteverify`, et il charge le script depuis `recaptcha.net` plut\xf4t que `google.com`, \xe0 la fois pour le script du navigateur et la v\xe9rification c\xf4t\xe9 serveur.\\n\\n## Lequel choisir {#which-one-to-pick}\\n\\n### SaaS mondial {#global-saas}\\n\\nCommencez avec [Cloudflare Turnstile](/docs/provider/captcha/cloudflareTurnstile). Le plan gratuit ne mesure pas les d\xe9fis, le mode g\xe9r\xe9 \xe9vite \xe0 la plupart des utilisateurs de cliquer sur quoi que ce soit, et vous n\'avez pas besoin de router votre site via Cloudflare. Si vous fonctionnez d\xe9j\xe0 sur Google Cloud et souhaitez les signaux de risque de reCAPTCHA, [reCAPTCHA](/docs/provider/captcha/recaptcha) convient, mais surveillez les 10 000 \xe9valuations gratuites : une page de connexion occup\xe9e ou une attaque par envoi massif de SMS peut d\xe9passer ce quota en une journ\xe9e. [hCaptcha](/docs/provider/captcha/hcaptcha) est l\'autre choix courant ; son plan gratuit affiche toujours un d\xe9fi, et les modes passifs sont payants.\\n\\n### Une application avec des utilisateurs en Chine continentale {#an-app-with-users-in-mainland-china}\\n\\nTurnstile est exclu, selon la propre documentation de Cloudflare. Pour reCAPTCHA, Google documente `www.recaptcha.net` comme le domaine \xe0 utiliser lorsque `www.google.com` n\'est pas atteignable, et Casdoor l\'utilise d\xe9j\xe0, mais vous devriez tout de m\xeame tester depuis les r\xe9seaux de vos utilisateurs avant de compter dessus. hCaptcha ne publie aucune d\xe9claration concernant la Chine, donc le m\xeame conseil s\'applique.\\n\\nLes deux choix s\xfbrs sont [Alibaba Cloud Captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/aliyunCaptcha) et [GeeTest](/docs/provider/captcha/geetest). Alibaba Cloud publie des prix par appel et permet de commencer en paiement \xe0 l\'usage ; son mode sans trace (dans Casdoor, utilisez le sous-type `Popup` pour cela) se rapproche de l\'exp\xe9rience invisible de Turnstile. GeeTest vend via son \xe9quipe commerciale apr\xe8s un essai de 30 jours. Si vous servez \xe0 la fois la Chine et le reste du monde depuis un seul Casdoor, rappelez-vous qu\'une application a un seul fournisseur de captcha, donc s\xe9parez les audiences en deux applications si elles n\xe9cessitent des captchas diff\xe9rents.\\n\\n### Utilisateurs soucieux de la vie priv\xe9e dans l\'UE {#privacy-sensitive-users-in-the-eu}\\n\\nChaque captcha h\xe9berg\xe9 envoie des donn\xe9es sur le navigateur du visiteur au fournisseur. Si votre \xe9quipe juridique souhaite qu\'aucun tiers n\'intervienne dans le processus de connexion, le [captcha par d\xe9faut](/docs/provider/captcha/default) de Casdoor est la seule option du tableau qui maintient tout sur votre propre serveur. Parmi les solutions h\xe9berg\xe9es, Turnstile et hCaptcha consid\xe8rent tous deux la confidentialit\xe9 comme faisant partie int\xe9grante du produit (donn\xe9es limit\xe9es \xe0 ce dont le contr\xf4le a besoin, RGPD et cadre UE-\xc9tats-Unis pour la protection des donn\xe9es respectivement). reCAPTCHA d\xe9finit un cookie `_GRECAPTCHA` ; Google note que le service depuis `www.recaptcha.net` \xe9vite d\'autres cookies que `www.google.com` peut d\xe9finir. Quel que soit votre choix, mentionnez-le dans votre politique de confidentialit\xe9.\\n\\n### Auto-h\xe9bergement, intranet ou hors ligne {#self-hosted-intranet-or-offline}\\n\\nUtilisez le captcha par d\xe9faut. Il ne n\xe9cessite aucune cl\xe9 ni acc\xe8s r\xe9seau sortant, il fonctionne donc dans des installations isol\xe9es (air-gapped). Associez-le \xe0 la r\xe8gle `Internet-Only` (ci-dessous) afin que les personnes sur le r\xe9seau du bureau ne le voient jamais, tandis que les requ\xeates provenant d\'Internet public le d\xe9clenchent toujours.\\n\\nLe captcha par d\xe9faut est le plus faible du groupe face aux solveurs automatis\xe9s, car il s\'agit d\'une simple image de chiffres d\xe9form\xe9s. Pour une page de connexion publique \xe0 fort trafic, combinez-le avec l\'[authentification multi-facteurs](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mfa-methods-compared) ou faites migrer les utilisateurs vers une [connexion sans mot de passe](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/passwordless-authentication), ce qui supprime les tentatives de devinette de mot de passe que les captchas existent pour ralentir.\\n\\n## Comment fonctionne le CAPTCHA dans Casdoor {#how-captcha-works-in-casdoor}\\n\\nVous ajoutez un fournisseur avec **Category** d\xe9fini sur `Captcha` et l\'un de ces types : `Default`, `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2`, `reCAPTCHA v3`, `hCaptcha`, `Cloudflare Turnstile`, `GEETEST` ou `Aliyun Captcha`. Ensuite, vous l\'ajoutez \xe0 une application dans l\'onglet **Providers**. Une application ne peut contenir qu\'un seul fournisseur de captcha ; Casdoor refuse d\'en enregistrer un second. Les \xe9tapes de configuration pour chaque fournisseur sont disponibles dans la [documentation des fournisseurs captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/overview).\\n\\n### O\xf9 Casdoor le demande {#where-casdoor-asks-for-it}\\n\\nLe m\xeame fournisseur prot\xe8ge plusieurs actions, et Casdoor v\xe9rifie le jeton c\xf4t\xe9 serveur, pas seulement dans le navigateur :\\n\\n- Connexion par mot de passe.\\n- Soumission du formulaire d\'inscription.\\n- Envoi d\'un code de v\xe9rification par e-mail ou SMS, ce qui couvre l\'\xe9tape du code lors de l\'inscription, la page de r\xe9cup\xe9ration de mot de passe et la connexion par code. C\'est la protection contre les bots qui consomment votre budget SMS.\\n\\n### Quand Casdoor le demande {#when-casdoor-asks-for-it}\\n\\nLa **Rule** du fournisseur de captcha dans l\'application d\xe9cide quand il appara\xeet :\\n\\n| Rule         | Behavior                                                               |\\n|---|---|\\n| `None` | Jamais ; le captcha est d\xe9sactiv\xe9 |\\n| `Dynamic` | Seulement apr\xe8s que les \xe9checs de connexion de l\'utilisateur atteignent la limite **Failed signin limit** de l\'application |\\n| `Always` | \xc0 chaque fois |\\n| `Internet-Only` | Pour les requ\xeates provenant d\'adresses IP publiques ; pas pour les adresses priv\xe9es, de bouclage (loopback) ou autres non publiques |\\n\\n`Dynamic` est le param\xe8tre le plus doux pour la connexion : les utilisateurs honn\xeates ne voient jamais de d\xe9fi, et quelqu\'un qui devine des mots de passe en rencontre un apr\xe8s quelques \xe9checs. Cependant, cela ne s\'applique qu\'aux comptes existants. Une inscription, ou un code envoy\xe9 \xe0 une adresse qui n\'a pas encore de compte, ne le d\xe9clenche pas. Si l\'abus de SMS ou d\'e-mails lors de l\'inscription est votre pr\xe9occupation, utilisez `Always` ou `Internet-Only`.\\n\\n### O\xf9 il appara\xeet sur la page {#where-it-appears-on-the-page}\\n\\nDans les \xe9l\xe9ments de connexion de l\'application, l\'\xe9l\xe9ment **Captcha** a sa propre r\xe8gle : `Pop up` affiche le captcha dans une bo\xeete de dialogue lorsqu\'il est n\xe9cessaire, et `Inline` le place directement sur la page. Voir [Modifier le captcha](/docs/application/signin-items-table#captcha-rules). Alibaba Cloud Captcha poss\xe8de un champ suppl\xe9mentaire **Sub type** : `Embed` l\'affiche \xe0 l\'int\xe9rieur de la bo\xeete de dialogue Casdoor, et `Popup` donne le contr\xf4le \xe0 la fen\xeatre contextuelle propre d\'Alibaba Cloud, ce qui est n\xe9cessaire pour les sc\xe9narios sans trace (traceless scenes).\\n\\nUtilisez le bouton **Preview** sur la page du fournisseur pour v\xe9rifier les cl\xe9s avant d\'activer la r\xe8gle.\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- Versions de Google reCAPTCHA : https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/versions\\n- FAQ Google reCAPTCHA (`recaptcha.net`, cookie `_GRECAPTCHA`) : https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/faq\\n- Facturation reCAPTCHA dans Google Cloud Fraud Defense (v\xe9rifi\xe9 le 2026-11-02) : https://docs.cloud.google.com/recaptcha/docs/billing-information\\n- Tarifs hCaptcha (v\xe9rifi\xe9 le 2026-11-02) : https://www.hcaptcha.com/pricing\\n- Politique de confidentialit\xe9 hCaptcha : https://www.hcaptcha.com/privacy\\n- Vue d\'ensemble Cloudflare Turnstile : https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/\\n- Plans Cloudflare Turnstile (v\xe9rifi\xe9 le 2026-11-02) : https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/plans/\\n- FAQ Cloudflare China Network (Turnstile en Chine continentale) : https://developers.cloudflare.com/china-network/faq/\\n- GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 (v\xe9rifi\xe9 le 2026-11-02) : https://www.geetest.com/adaptive-captcha\\n- Vue d\'ensemble Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 : https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/what-is-alibaba-cloud-captcha-2\\n- \xc9ditions et prix Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 (v\xe9rifi\xe9 le 2026-11-02) : https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/version-description"},{"id":"customize-login-page","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/customize-login-page","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-customize-login-page.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-customize-login-page.mdx","title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","description":"Style Casdoor\'s hosted login and sign-up pages without forking: themes, CSS, HTML, logo, background, form position, sign-in fields and organization choice.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Login Page","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/login-page"},{"inline":true,"label":"UI Customization","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/ui-customization"},{"inline":true,"label":"CSS","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/css"},{"inline":true,"label":"Themes","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/themes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Branding","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/branding"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":9.28,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"customize-login-page","title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","description":"Style Casdoor\'s hosted login and sign-up pages without forking: themes, CSS, HTML, logo, background, form position, sign-in fields and organization choice.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Login Page","UI Customization","CSS","Themes","Branding","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Comparaison des fournisseurs de CAPTCHA pour les pages de connexion : reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest et Alibaba Cloud dans Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/captcha-for-login-pages"},"nextItem":{"title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","permalink":"/fr/blog/devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana"}},"content":"Yes, Casdoor supports custom styling of its authentication UI, and you don\'t need to fork the frontend to get it. Every application has a **UI Customization** tab where you set the logo, favicon, background image, custom CSS for desktop and mobile, the position of the form, a side panel, footer HTML and a theme. Two tables, **Signin items** and **Signup items**, decide which fields the login and sign-up pages show, in which order, with which labels and placeholders. All of it is stored per application in the database, so one Casdoor instance can serve many differently branded login pages, and organization admins can edit their own pages within safe limits.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What to change and where\\n\\n| What you want to change | Where in Casdoor | Docs |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Primary color, corner radius, dark or compact style | **Theme** of the organization, or of the application with **Follow organization theme** off | [Customize the theme](/docs/organization/customize-theme) |\\n| A complete ready-made look | **Identity \u2192 Marketplace**, sign-in themes | [Marketplace](/docs/marketplace/overview) |\\n| Logo, dark logo, favicon, page title | Application edit page | [Application settings](/docs/application/terminology) |\\n| Background image (desktop and phone) | **Background URL**, **Background URL Mobile** | [UI customization](/docs/application/ui-customization#1-background-image) |\\n| Look of the form | **Custom CSS**, **Custom CSS Mobile** | [UI customization](/docs/application/ui-customization#2-login-panel-style) |\\n| Form on the left, center or right, or next to a panel | **Form position**, **Side panel HTML** | [UI customization](/docs/application/ui-customization#3-panel-position) |\\n| Text below the form | **Footer HTML** | [Application settings](/docs/application/terminology#custom-html) |\\n| Which fields the login page shows, order, placeholders | **Signin items** | [Sign-in items table](/docs/application/signin-items-table) |\\n| Password, code, magic link, WebAuthn, LDAP and their order | **Signin methods** | [Sign-in methods](/docs/application/signin-methods) |\\n| Sign-up fields, required flags, labels, regex | **Signup items** | [Sign-up items table](/docs/application/signup-items-table) |\\n| Language selector | **Languages** sign-in item, **Languages** of the organization | [Sign-in items table](/docs/application/signin-items-table#language-selector-rules) |\\n| Let users pick or type their organization | **Org choice mode** | [Specify login organization](/docs/application/specify-login-organization) |\\n\\nIn the Chinese console the tab is called \u754C\u9762\u5B9A\u5236 and the sign-in table \u767B\u5F55\u9879, so if you are looking for how to change the \u767B\u5F55\u754C\u9762 of an application, start there.\\n\\n## Themes: organization, application and Marketplace\\n\\nThe quickest change is the theme. A theme in Casdoor is a small object with a style (Default, Dark, Document or Blossom in the editor), a primary color, a border radius and a compact switch. You can set it on the organization, where it applies to the console for its members and to the entry pages of every application that follows the organization theme, or on a single application by turning off **Follow organization theme**. The application edit page has a live preview, so you can see the result before you save.\\n\\nIf you want more than colors, the [Casdoor Marketplace](/docs/marketplace/overview) has sign-in themes. Installing one writes the theme data, form position, form CSS for desktop and mobile, side panel HTML, footer HTML and background URLs of one application. The casdoor-integrations repository currently holds 24 themes, from Midnight and Split to Terminal, Nord and Solarized. Uninstalling a theme restores what the application had before, so trying one costs nothing. Browse them at [marketplace.casdoor.com](https://marketplace.casdoor.com).\\n\\n## Branding: logo, favicon and background\\n\\nEach application has its own **Logo**, **Favicon** and **Title**, separate from the organization. One organization can run several products that share users but show different logos. There is also an optional **Logo dark**, shown when the page is in dark mode; without it, Casdoor keeps using the normal logo.\\n\\n**Background URL** sets the image behind the form. Set **Background URL Mobile** too if the desktop image crops badly on phones. The preview updates as soon as the URL loads.\\n\\n## Custom CSS and form layout\\n\\n**Custom CSS** takes plain CSS rules; Casdoor wraps them in a style element itself, so don\'t add one of your own. The main containers to target are `.login-panel` and `.login-form`:\\n\\n```css\\n.login-panel {\\n  border-radius: 12px;\\n  background-color: #ffffff;\\n  box-shadow: 0 0 30px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Custom CSS Mobile** holds rules for phones, so you don\'t need to write media queries for the common case.\\n\\n**Form position** places the form on the left, center or right. The fourth option, **Enable side panel**, centers the form and puts a panel next to it whose content comes from **Side panel HTML**. That field takes HTML, so a style element inside it is fine.\\n\\n**Footer HTML** is rendered below the sign-in and sign-up forms. If the application is the default application of its organization, its footer also replaces \\"Powered by Casdoor\\" in the admin console for that organization\'s users.\\n\\nWhen you are happy with a design on staging, **Export JSON** at the bottom of the application edit page copies the look (logo, favicon, backgrounds, CSS, form position, side panel, theme and the custom HTML fields) to the clipboard, and **Import JSON** on the same application on another instance applies it. The export contains no secrets or providers.\\n\\n## Fields, labels and order\\n\\nThe **Signin items** table lists every element of the login page: the logo, the username and password fields, the forgot-password link, the agreement checkbox, the language selector, the captcha, provider buttons and so on. For each row you can set **Visible**, a custom **Placeholder**, per-item **Custom CSS** and a rule, and move it up or down. You can also add custom items whose label is HTML.\\n\\nA few rules worth knowing:\\n\\n- Hiding **Forgot password?** turns off password reset in the API too, not just the link.\\n- The **Captcha** rule switches between a pop-up and an inline captcha. If you are choosing a captcha provider, see [CAPTCHA providers compared for login pages](/blog/captcha-for-login-pages).\\n- The **Languages** rule switches between a globe icon and a labeled selector. The list itself is limited to the **Languages** of the organization, so an organization that only serves English and Japanese users shows only those two.\\n\\nThe sign-in methods (password, verification code, magic link, WebAuthn, LDAP, Face ID and others) are a separate table, **Signin methods**, with its own order and display names.\\n\\nThe **Signup items** table does the same for registration, with **Required**, **Regex** validation, custom **Label** and rules such as \\"No verification\\" for email. `POST /api/signup` also clears every field whose item is hidden, so API clients can\'t set fields your form doesn\'t show. **Prompted** asks users for missing fields, such as a phone number, after they sign in through a provider that didn\'t supply one.\\n\\n## Specify the login organization\\n\\nIf several organizations share one Casdoor, users need a way to reach the right one. **Org choice mode** on the default application of an organization (or on `app-built-in`) adds an organization step to `/login`: **Input** shows a text field, **Select** shows a dropdown. After the user picks, Casdoor sends them to `/login/<organization>`, which shows that organization\'s own styled page. The browser remembers the last organization in `localStorage`, so a user whose console session expired lands on their own login page again.\\n\\n## What tenant admins can safely change\\n\\nLetting every customer style their own login page raises an obvious question: what stops an organization admin from adding a script that copies passwords, or a CSS selector that leaks each typed character through background image requests? Casdoor checks this on the server. When an admin who is not a global admin adds or saves an application, every custom HTML and CSS field that changed is validated, and the save is rejected if any of them breaks the rules:\\n\\n- HTML may only use layout and text elements such as `div`, `span`, `p`, headings, lists, links, images, tables, `style` and basic SVG shapes. `script`, `form`, `input` and `iframe` are refused, as are `on...` event handlers, `srcset`, `formaction`, `srcdoc` and similar attributes. `href` is allowed only on links with relative, `http`, `https`, `mailto` or `tel` URLs.\\n- CSS may not contain backslashes, `<`, `image(`, `image-set(` and a few other functions. Every `url()` must be relative, a `data:` URL, or start with an allowed prefix: Google Fonts, Fontsource on jsDelivr, the Casdoor CDNs, this Casdoor\'s own origin, the domains of the global storage providers, or an entry of `formCssAllowedUrls`.\\n- `@import` is allowed only from Google Fonts and Fontsource, because an imported stylesheet isn\'t checked itself.\\n\\nTo let tenants load images from your own host, add it to `conf/app.conf` (see [Configuration](/docs/basic/configuration#backend-options)):\\n\\n```ini\\nformCssAllowedUrls = \\"https://img.example.com/\\"\\n```\\n\\nOn top of the save-time check, the sign-in pages sanitize custom HTML before rendering it, unless the application belongs to the `built-in` organization or a global admin has turned on **Enable custom scripts** for it. Only a global admin can turn that switch on, and while it is on only a global admin can change that application\'s HTML. The same rules apply when a tenant installs a Marketplace theme: a theme that breaks them can only be installed by a global admin.\\n\\n**Signin HTML** and **Signup HTML** deserve a separate note: they replace the whole built-in page, form included. Since tenants can\'t add forms or scripts, these two fields are mainly for global admins; for branding, CSS, the side panel and the footer are the better tools.\\n\\n## Hosted login page or your own UI?\\n\\nYou can build your own login screen on Casdoor\'s API, but the hosted page is usually the better choice. With OAuth 2.0 or OpenID Connect, users type their password only on the Casdoor origin and your application receives an authorization code, never the credentials. Captcha, MFA, social and enterprise providers, forgot-password, sign-up validation and the organization picker keep working without code on your side, and they keep getting fixes when you upgrade Casdoor. A custom UI has to reimplement each of those flows and puts passwords through your own servers. Guides for wiring the hosted page into an app: [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc) and [Nginx SSO authentication](/blog/nginx-sso-authentication).\\n\\nOther hosted providers make the same trade. Idura Verify, for example, lets you add a stylesheet URL and custom HTML around its login box from its dashboard, under a strict Content Security Policy with an explicit list of style origins for fonts and images. Casdoor\'s `formCssAllowedUrls` plays the same role, with the difference that Casdoor is open source and self-hosted, so you control the list.\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- [Customize the look of the sign-in page](/docs/application/ui-customization)\\n- [Personnaliser le th\xe8me](/docs/organization/customize-theme)\\n- [Sign-in items table](/docs/application/signin-items-table) and [Sign-up items table](/docs/application/signup-items-table)\\n- [Specify login organization](/docs/application/specify-login-organization)\\n- [Marketplace](/docs/marketplace/overview) and [casdoor/casdoor-integrations](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-integrations)\\n- Casdoor source: [`object/application_util.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/application_util.go) and [`object/application_html.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/application_html.go)\\n- Idura Verify: [Custom styling](https://docs.idura.app/verify/guides/custom-styling/)"},{"id":"devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana.mdx","title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","description":"Use Casdoor as the OIDC provider for Gitea, Jenkins, Grafana, Argo CD, MinIO, Portainer, Harbor and GitLab: callback URLs, config keys and admin group mapping.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Jenkins","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/jenkins"},{"inline":true,"label":"Gitea","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/gitea"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grafana","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/grafana"},{"inline":true,"label":"Argo CD","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/argo-cd"},{"inline":true,"label":"MinIO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/min-io"},{"inline":true,"label":"DevOps","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/dev-ops"}],"readingTime":9.14,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana","title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","description":"Use Casdoor as the OIDC provider for Gitea, Jenkins, Grafana, Argo CD, MinIO, Portainer, Harbor and GitLab: callback URLs, config keys and admin group mapping.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","OIDC","SSO","Jenkins","Gitea","Grafana","Argo CD","MinIO","DevOps"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","permalink":"/fr/blog/customize-login-page"},"nextItem":{"title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/dingtalk-wecom-lark-login"}},"content":"Every tool in a typical self-hosted DevOps stack can act as an OpenID Connect client: Gitea and Argo CD speak OIDC natively, Jenkins does through the `oic-auth` plugin, Grafana and Portainer through generic OAuth 2.0, and MinIO, Harbor and GitLab through their OIDC settings. Point all of them at one Casdoor instance, give each tool its own Casdoor application, and put your people in Casdoor groups. Each tool then reads the `groups` claim and decides who is an administrator. This post lists the callback URL, the config keys and the group mapping for each tool, then covers the parts they share.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The stack at a glance\\n\\nAll paths below are relative to the tool\'s own public URL. Register them in **Redirect URLs** of the Casdoor application for that tool.\\n\\n| App | Protocol | Callback URL | Group/role to admin mapping | Casdoor doc |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Gitea | OIDC (OAuth2 auth source) | `/user/oauth2/<source-name>/callback` | Yes: admin group, restricted group, team map | [Gitea](/docs/integration/go/gitea) |\\n| Jenkins | OIDC (`oic-auth` plugin) | `/securityRealm/finishLogin`, logout `/OicLogout` | Yes: groups claim plus Matrix-based security | [Jenkins (OIDC)](/docs/integration/java/jenkins-oidc) |\\n| Grafana | Generic OAuth 2.0 | `/login/generic_oauth` | Yes: `role_attribute_path` | [Grafana](/docs/integration/go/grafana) |\\n| Argo CD | OIDC | `/auth/callback`, CLI `http://localhost:8085/auth/callback` | Yes: `argocd-rbac-cm` | [Argo CD](/docs/integration/go/argocd) |\\n| MinIO | OIDC | Console `/oauth_callback` | Policies from one claim (`claim_name`) | [MinIO](/docs/integration/go/minio) |\\n| Portainer | OAuth 2.0 (Custom) | The Portainer base URL | Business Edition only | [Portainer](/docs/integration/go/portainer) |\\n| Harbor | OIDC | `/c/oidc/callback` | Yes: OIDC Admin Group | none yet |\\n| GitLab | OIDC (OmniAuth) | `/users/auth/openid_connect/callback` | Premium and Ultimate: `admin_groups` | [GitLab](/docs/integration/ruby/gitlab) |\\n\\nThe discovery document every OIDC client needs is `https://<casdoor-host>/.well-known/openid-configuration`. If you have not connected an OIDC client to Casdoor before, [Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) explains the endpoints.\\n\\n## Prepare Casdoor once\\n\\nBefore touching the tools, set up three things in Casdoor.\\n\\n**Groups.** Create groups such as `devops-admins` and `devops-users` in the organization tree and add users to them. See [Groups](/docs/organization/organization-tree).\\n\\n**Token group format.** By default the `groups` claim names each group as `<organization>/<group>`. Most tools compare the claim with a plain string you type, so set **Token group format** on the **OIDC/OAuth** tab of each application to `Name (group)`. The claim then carries `devops-admins`. The options are described under [Group format](/docs/token/overview#token-group-format).\\n\\n**Token format.** Keep **Token format** at `JWT`, the default. In that format the ID token carries `groups`, `roles` and `tag`. `JWT-Empty` and `JWT-Standard` leave all three out of the token, and `JWT-Custom` carries them only if you select them in **Token fields**. The `/api/userinfo` response returns `groups` and the role names when the `profile` scope is granted, so tools that read userinfo still see them.\\n\\nRoles work too. In the `JWT` format the `roles` claim is an array of role objects, so match on `roles[*].name`. In `/api/userinfo` it is a plain array of names. See [User roles](/docs/user/roles).\\n\\n## Gitea\\n\\nIn **Site Administration**, add an authentication source of type **OAuth2** with provider **OpenID Connect**, the client ID and secret, and the Casdoor discovery URL. The name you give the source is part of the callback URL: a source named `casdoor` uses `https://gitea.example.com/user/oauth2/casdoor/callback`.\\n\\nFor group mapping, fill in **Claim name providing group names** with `groups`, then **Group Claim value for administrator users** with `devops-admins`. Gitea also has **Group Claim value for restricted users** and **Map claimed groups to Organization teams**. The same source from the CLI:\\n\\n```bash\\ngitea admin auth add-oauth \\\\\\n  --name casdoor \\\\\\n  --provider openidConnect \\\\\\n  --key <client-id> \\\\\\n  --secret <client-secret> \\\\\\n  --auto-discover-url https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration \\\\\\n  --group-claim-name groups \\\\\\n  --admin-group devops-admins\\n```\\n\\n## Jenkins\\n\\nJenkins has no OIDC support of its own. Install the [OpenId Connect Authentication](https://plugins.jenkins.io/oic-auth/) plugin, choose it as the security realm, and use the well-known configuration. The login callback is `/securityRealm/finishLogin` and the logout callback is `/OicLogout`; add both to Casdoor. In JCasC:\\n\\n```yaml\\njenkins:\\n  securityRealm:\\n    oic:\\n      serverConfiguration:\\n        wellKnown:\\n          wellKnownOpenIDConfigurationUrl: https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration\\n      clientId: <client-id>\\n      clientSecret: <client-secret>\\n      userNameField: name\\n      fullNameFieldName: displayName\\n      emailFieldName: email\\n      groupsFieldName: groups\\n      logoutFromOpenidProvider: true\\n      properties:\\n      - \\"pkce\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe plugin only passes the groups to Jenkins. Rights come from the authorization strategy: with Matrix-based security, each row of the table is a user or a group, so add a row for `devops-admins` and give it Overall/Administer. Choosing the plugin as the security realm turns off other login methods, so configure the plugin\'s `escapeHatch` property (a fallback username and secret) before you save. Casdoor also has its own Jenkins plugin, described in [Jenkins plugin](/docs/integration/java/jenkins-plugin), but the generic OIDC plugin is the one this post assumes.\\n\\n## Grafana\\n\\nGrafana uses generic OAuth. Its callback is `/login/generic_oauth`. Grafana\'s default `scopes` is `user:email`, so set the OIDC scopes explicitly:\\n\\n```ini\\n[auth.generic_oauth]\\nenabled = true\\nname = Casdoor\\nclient_id = <client-id>\\nclient_secret = <client-secret>\\nscopes = openid profile email\\nauth_url = https://door.example.com/login/oauth/authorize\\ntoken_url = https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token\\napi_url = https://door.example.com/api/userinfo\\nuse_pkce = true\\ngroups_attribute_path = groups\\nrole_attribute_path = contains(groups[*], \'devops-admins\') && \'Admin\' || contains(groups[*], \'devops-users\') && \'Editor\' || \'Viewer\'\\nrole_attribute_strict = true\\n```\\n\\nGrafana evaluates `role_attribute_path` against the ID token first, then the userinfo response, then the access token. Valid results are `None`, `Viewer`, `Editor`, `Admin` and `GrafanaAdmin`; the last one needs `allow_assign_grafana_admin = true`. `allowed_groups` limits login to listed groups and requires `groups_attribute_path`. Team sync is a Grafana Enterprise and Grafana Cloud feature.\\n\\n## Argo CD\\n\\nArgo CD talks to Casdoor directly, without its bundled Dex. The browser callback is `/auth/callback`. `argocd login --sso` starts a local server on port 8085 by default and uses `http://localhost:8085/auth/callback`, so register that as well.\\n\\nSet `url` and `oidc.config` (with `name`, `issuer`, `clientID`, `clientSecret`, `requestedScopes`) in `argocd-cm`. Argo CD\'s default `requestedScopes` adds a `groups` scope. Casdoor does not need it, because the `JWT` ID token always carries `groups`, so our guide requests `[\\"openid\\", \\"profile\\", \\"email\\"]`. Groups are mapped in `argocd-rbac-cm`, where `scopes` defaults to `[groups]`:\\n\\n```yaml\\ndata:\\n  policy.default: role:readonly\\n  policy.csv: |\\n    g, devops-admins, role:admin\\n```\\n\\nThe full walkthrough, including the client secret in `argocd-secret`, is in the [Argo CD](/docs/integration/go/argocd) guide.\\n\\n## MinIO\\n\\nMinIO reads policies from a single claim, set with `claim_name` (default `policy`). It does not map groups. MinIO\'s own Casdoor quickstart and our guide use Casdoor\'s `tag` claim: put a MinIO policy name such as `readwrite` in the user\'s **Tag**, then:\\n\\n```bash\\nmc admin config set myminio identity_openid \\\\\\n  config_url=\\"https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration\\" \\\\\\n  client_id=<client-id> client_secret=<client-secret> claim_name=\\"tag\\"\\n```\\n\\n`role_policy` is the alternative: every user who signs in through this provider gets the listed policies, and it cannot be combined with `claim_name`. The Console callback is `<console-url>/oauth_callback`. Behind a proxy, set `MINIO_BROWSER_REDIRECT_URL`; the old `redirect_uri` key is deprecated. Note that the `minio/minio` GitHub repository has been archived and its README says it is no longer maintained, so check which MinIO build you run before relying on these settings.\\n\\n## Portainer\\n\\nPortainer uses a Custom OAuth provider. Fill in **Authorization URL** (`/login/oauth/authorize`), **Access token URL** (`/api/login/oauth/access_token`) and **Resource URL** (`/api/userinfo`) on your Casdoor host, and set **Redirect URL** to the Portainer base URL. There is also a **Logout URL** field. In Portainer CE, **Automatic team membership** is marked as a Business Edition feature, and the setting that assigns admin rights to groups is only in Business Edition. On CE, users created by automatic provisioning get the standard user role and you promote admins by hand.\\n\\n## Harbor and GitLab\\n\\nHarbor shows its redirect URI at the bottom of the OIDC settings page: `<harbor-url>/c/oidc/callback`. Set **OIDC Provider Endpoint** to the Casdoor URL, **Group Claim Name** to `groups`, and **OIDC Admin Group** to `devops-admins`. Harbor reads the group claim from the ID token, which the `JWT` format provides. You can only switch Harbor from database to OIDC authentication before any local users exist.\\n\\nGitLab\'s OIDC docs have a Casdoor section and require Casdoor to be served over HTTPS. The callback is `/users/auth/openid_connect/callback`. Group-based roles (`groups_attribute`, `admin_groups`, `required_groups`, `external_groups`) need GitLab Premium or Ultimate.\\n\\n## Tools without OIDC\\n\\nFor internal dashboards with no login of their own, put them behind Apache APISIX\'s `openid-connect` plugin (`discovery`, `client_id`, `client_secret`, `redirect_uri`, `bearer_only`), as in the [APISIX guide](/docs/integration/lua/apisix), or behind nginx as described in [Nginx SSO authentication](/blog/nginx-sso-authentication).\\n\\n## One application per tool or a shared one\\n\\nA single Casdoor application with every tool\'s callback in its Redirect URLs works, but one application per tool is easier to run:\\n\\n- Each tool gets its own client secret, so a leaked Jenkins secret does not expose Grafana.\\n- **Token group format**, **Token format** and token expiry are per application, so you can give Argo CD `Name (group)` without changing another tool\'s claims.\\n- Casdoor checks `post_logout_redirect_uri` against the Redirect URLs of the application, so each list stays short and specific.\\n\\nUsers still sign in once: after the first login, Casdoor\'s session cookie lets the next tool\'s redirect complete without a password prompt.\\n\\n## D\xe9connexion {#logout}\\n\\nCasdoor\'s end-session endpoint is `/api/logout`, listed as `end_session_endpoint` in the discovery document. It accepts `id_token_hint`, `post_logout_redirect_uri`, `client_id` and `state`, and only redirects to a URL listed in the application\'s Redirect URLs. Signing out of Casdoor ends the Casdoor session and the tokens of that session, but each tool also keeps its own session cookie. Wire up logout in each tool:\\n\\n- Jenkins: `logoutFromOpenidProvider`, with `/OicLogout` registered.\\n- Grafana: `signout_redirect_url`.\\n- Argo CD: `logoutURL` in `oidc.config`, for example `https://door.example.com/api/logout?id_token_hint={{token}}&post_logout_redirect_uri={{logoutRedirectURL}}`.\\n- Harbor: **OIDC Session Logout**.\\n- Portainer: **Logout URL**.\\n\\nThe details of the endpoint are in [RP-initiated logout](/docs/session/rp-initiated-logout).\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- Gitea: [Authentication](https://docs.gitea.com/usage/authentication), [`admin auth add-oauth`](https://docs.gitea.com/administration/command-line)\\n- Jenkins: [OpenId Connect Authentication plugin](https://plugins.jenkins.io/oic-auth/), [configuration reference](https://github.com/jenkinsci/oic-auth-plugin/blob/master/docs/configuration/README.md), [Managing security](https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/security/managing-security/)\\n- Grafana: [Configure generic OAuth2 authentication](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-access/configure-authentication/generic-oauth/)\\n- Argo CD: [User management](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/user-management/), [RBAC](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/rbac/), [`argocd login`](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/commands/argocd_login/)\\n- MinIO: [Casdoor quickstart in the MinIO repository](https://github.com/minio/minio/blob/master/docs/sts/casdoor.md)\\n- Portainer: [OAuth authentication](https://docs.portainer.io/admin/settings/authentication/oauth)\\n- Harbor: [Configure OIDC provider authentication](https://goharbor.io/docs/main/administration/configure-authentication/oidc-auth/)\\n- GitLab: [Use OpenID Connect as an authentication provider](https://docs.gitlab.com/administration/auth/oidc/)\\n- Apache APISIX: [openid-connect plugin](https://apisix.apache.org/docs/apisix/plugins/openid-connect/)"},{"id":"dingtalk-wecom-lark-login","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/dingtalk-wecom-lark-login","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-dingtalk-wecom-lark-login.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-dingtalk-wecom-lark-login.mdx","title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","description":"Sign in with DingTalk, WeCom, Lark/Feishu, Baidu Infoflow and WeChat through Casdoor: app types, QR login, user IDs, email access, corporate tokens, sync.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"DingTalk","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/ding-talk"},{"inline":true,"label":"WeCom","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/we-com"},{"inline":true,"label":"Lark","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/lark"},{"inline":true,"label":"Feishu","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/feishu"},{"inline":true,"label":"WeChat","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/we-chat"},{"inline":true,"label":"Infoflow","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/infoflow"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":9.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"dingtalk-wecom-lark-login","title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","description":"Sign in with DingTalk, WeCom, Lark/Feishu, Baidu Infoflow and WeChat through Casdoor: app types, QR login, user IDs, email access, corporate tokens, sync.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","DingTalk","WeCom","Lark","Feishu","WeChat","Infoflow","OAuth","SSO"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","permalink":"/fr/blog/devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana"},"nextItem":{"title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","permalink":"/fr/blog/email-providers-for-verification-codes"}},"content":"Casdoor has built-in OAuth providers for DingTalk, WeCom (WeChat Work), Lark (Feishu), Baidu Infoflow and WeChat. You create an app in each vendor\'s developer console, paste its credentials into a Casdoor provider, and add the provider to your application. Casdoor runs the vendor flow (QR scan on a desktop, or sign-in inside the vendor\'s own client), fetches the corporate access token where the vendor needs one, and maps the vendor\'s user ID to a Casdoor user. Your application only talks OIDC to Casdoor and never sees a DingTalk header, a WeCom `user_ticket` or a WeChat `unionid`. For DingTalk, WeCom and Lark, a syncer can also import the whole org directory ahead of the first sign-in.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## En un coup d\'\u0153il {#at-a-glance}\\n\\n| Provider | App you create | Where users sign in | ID that links the Casdoor user | Email and phone | Directory syncer |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| DingTalk | Internal app (AppKey, AppSecret) | DingTalk sign-in page, `login.dingtalk.com` | `openId`; `unionid` becomes the username | From the profile, replaced by the corporate directory; treated as verified | Yes |\\n| WeCom | Self-built app (CorpID, Secret, AgentId), or third-party | Web login (QR, or quick login from WeCom desktop) or silent sign-in inside WeCom | `userid` (internal), `open_userid` (third-party) | Email only with `snsapi_privateinfo`; phone not read | Yes |\\n| Lark / Feishu | Custom app (App ID, App Secret) | Lark sign-in page, Feishu or Lark global | `open_id`; username is `user_id`, else `union_id`, else `open_id` | Yes, if the app has the contact permissions | Yes |\\n| Baidu Infoflow | Internal app (CorpID, Secret, AgentID) | Infoflow authorize page | `userid`, also the username | Email | No |\\n| WeChat | Website app on the Open Platform, plus an Official Account | QR scan on a PC; inside the WeChat browser through the Official Account | `unionid`, else `openid` | Neither | No |\\n\\nThe setup steps for each are in the docs: [DingTalk](/docs/provider/oauth/DingTalk), [WeCom](/docs/provider/oauth/weCom), [Lark](/docs/provider/oauth/lark), [Infoflow](/docs/provider/oauth/infoflow) and [WeChat](/docs/provider/oauth/Wechat). This post explains what happens behind each one.\\n\\n## What your application sees\\n\\nEvery provider in the table ends the same way. Casdoor exchanges the vendor\'s code, finds or creates the user in the organization, and then issues its own authorization code to your app. Your app redeems it at Casdoor\'s token endpoint and gets a Casdoor ID token and access token, signed with the application\'s certificate. The claims come from the Casdoor user record: name, display name, email, phone, avatar, groups.\\n\\nYour backend code is the same for all five. Any OIDC library works; see the [OAuth 2.0 and OIDC reference](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth) and, for a full walkthrough, [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc). If you do need the vendor\'s own API later, Casdoor stores the provider\'s access token on the user, as described in [OAuth providers](/docs/provider/oauth/overview#using-the-providers-access-token).\\n\\n## DingTalk\\n\\nCreate an internal app on the DingTalk Open Platform and copy its AppKey and AppSecret into the Client ID and Client secret of the provider. The sign-in page sends the user to `https://login.dingtalk.com/oauth2/auth` with `scope=openid` and `prompt=login consent`. After the user confirms, Casdoor makes four calls:\\n\\n1. `POST https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/oauth2/userAccessToken` turns the code into a user access token.\\n2. `GET https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/contact/users/me` returns the user\'s `openId`, `unionId`, nick, avatar, email and mobile. This needs the **Contact.User.Read** permission on the app.\\n3. `POST https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/oauth2/accessToken` with `appKey` and `appSecret` returns the corporate (app) access token.\\n4. With that token, `topapi/user/getbyunionid` maps the `unionid` to the corporate `userid`, and `topapi/v2/user/get` reads the directory record.\\n\\nIf the user is not a member of the organization that owns the app, step 4 fails with DingTalk error 60121 and the sign-in stops. If the directory lookup succeeds, the directory email and mobile replace the personal ones, `unionid` becomes the username, and the job title is kept as an extra claim.\\n\\n### The corporate access token and its header\\n\\nDingTalk has two API generations that take the same token in different places:\\n\\n```http\\nGET https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/contact/users/me\\nx-acs-dingtalk-access-token: <token>\\n```\\n\\n```http\\nPOST https://oapi.dingtalk.com/topapi/v2/user/get?access_token=<token>\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\"userid\\": \\"<userid>\\"}\\n```\\n\\nThe new API on `api.dingtalk.com` reads the token only from the `x-acs-dingtalk-access-token` header. Call it without that header (or with `Authorization: Bearer`) and it answers `AuthenticationFailed.MissingParameter`, naming the header. The older `oapi.dingtalk.com` endpoints still take an `access_token` query parameter. Casdoor uses both: the header for `users/me`, the query parameter for the two `topapi` calls, with the corporate token from `v1.0/oauth2/accessToken` in both places. DingTalk documents that token as valid for 7,200 seconds and asks callers to cache it per app. DingTalk also marks the old `gettoken` endpoint as legacy for new apps.\\n\\nCasdoor counts the email and phone that DingTalk returns as verified, so the default binding rule (email, then phone) can attach a DingTalk sign-in to an existing user. See [how Casdoor links accounts](/docs/provider/oauth/overview#automatic-account-linking).\\n\\n## WeCom (WeChat Work)\\n\\nA WeCom provider has a **Sub type** and a **Method**:\\n\\n- **Internal** with **Normal** sends the user to the WeCom web login, `login.work.weixin.qq.com/wwlogin/sso/login` with `login_type=CorpApp`. WeCom shows a QR code, or a one-click quick login when WeCom desktop is running and signed in.\\n- **Internal** with **Silent** uses `open.weixin.qq.com/connect/oauth2/authorize` with the app\'s `agentid`. This is the one to use for pages opened inside the WeCom client.\\n- **Third-party** uses `login_type=ServiceApp` and the service provider\'s secret (`get_provider_token`), then `get_login_info`, and links the user by `open_userid`.\\n\\nFor internal apps, Casdoor gets the corporate token from `cgi-bin/gettoken` with the CorpID and the app Secret, then exchanges the code for the member\'s `userid`. A visitor who is not a member comes back with an `openid` instead, and Casdoor rejects the sign-in. The token is a query parameter on every WeCom call; WeCom says it normally lasts 7,200 seconds and that `gettoken` is rate limited.\\n\\nThe email address is the part to plan for. WeCom stopped returning avatar, gender, mobile and email from the member read API (`user/get`) for self-built apps created after 20:00 on 2022-06-20. To get the email, set the provider\'s scope to `snsapi_privateinfo`. WeCom then returns a `user_ticket`, which Casdoor redeems at `getuserdetail`. WeCom requires the admin to pick the sensitive fields in the app settings and the member to consent, and it never gives these fields to third-party apps. Casdoor does not read the phone number from WeCom.\\n\\n## Lark and Feishu\\n\\nCreate a custom app on the [Feishu Open Platform](https://open.feishu.cn/) or the Lark global platform and copy the App ID and App Secret. Turn on **Use global endpoint** in the provider for Lark (`open.larksuite.com`); leave it off for Feishu (`open.feishu.cn`).\\n\\nCasdoor first gets a `tenant_access_token` from `auth/v3/tenant_access_token/internal`, then posts the code to `authen/v1/access_token` with that token in an `Authorization: Bearer` header. The response carries `open_id`, `union_id`, `user_id`, email, enterprise email and mobile. Feishu returns the sensitive ones only when the app holds the matching permission:\\n\\n| Field | Feishu permission |\\n|---|---|\\n| `user_id` | `contact:user.employee_id:readonly` |\\n| `email` | `contact:user.email:readonly` |\\n| `enterprise_email` | `contact:user.employee:readonly` |\\n| `mobile` | `contact:user.phone:readonly` |\\n\\nWithout them, sign-in still works: the username falls back from `user_id` to `union_id` to `open_id`. Casdoor uses `enterprise_email` when `email` is empty and treats both email and phone as verified. Feishu now lists the v1 `access_token` endpoint as a historical version, but it is still served.\\n\\n## Baidu Infoflow\\n\\nInfoflow (\u5982\u6D41) is Baidu\'s workplace app. Register an app in the Infoflow admin, give it address book permissions, and copy the CorpID, Secret and AgentID into the provider with the **Internal** sub type. The sign-in page goes to `https://xpc.im.baidu.com/oauth2/authorize`. Casdoor then calls `qy.im.baidu.com/api/gettoken` with the CorpID and Secret, exchanges the code and AgentID for the member\'s `userid` at `api/user/getuserinfo`, and reads name, avatar and email from `api/user/get`. The `userid` becomes both the linked ID and the username, and the email counts as verified. There is no Infoflow syncer, so users are created on first sign-in.\\n\\n## WeChat\\n\\nWeChat login has two halves, and you usually need both:\\n\\n- **Web** sub type: a website app on the WeChat Open Platform. The PC sign-in page shows a QR code from `open.weixin.qq.com/connect/qrconnect` with `scope=snsapi_login`; the user scans it with the WeChat app.\\n- **Mobile** sub type, or the second credential pair of the same provider: an Official Account. Inside the WeChat browser, a QR code is useless, so Casdoor switches to `connect/oauth2/authorize` with `snsapi_userinfo` and the Official Account\'s AppID. If no Official Account is configured, Casdoor tells the user to open the page in an external browser.\\n\\nWeChat returns no email or phone, only `openid`, `unionid`, nickname and avatar. Casdoor links the user by `unionid` when WeChat sends one, falling back to `openid`, and also stores the per-app `openid`. WeChat gives a user the same `unionid` across all apps and Official Accounts under one Open Platform account, so bind your website app and your Official Account to the same Open Platform account. Otherwise a user who scans on a PC and later signs in on a phone becomes two Casdoor users.\\n\\n## Importing the directory first\\n\\nFor DingTalk, WeCom and Lark, a [syncer](/docs/syncer/overview) can pull users and departments in on a schedule, so accounts exist before anyone signs in and departments become Casdoor groups. Each one uses a corporate token from the vendor\'s directory API: the [DingTalk syncer](/docs/syncer/DingTalk) walks departments recursively, the [WeCom syncer](/docs/syncer/WeCom) falls back to `user/list_id` and `user/get` for apps created after 2022-08-15, and the [Lark syncer](/docs/syncer/Lark) has a **Lark platform** setting to choose Feishu or Lark global.\\n\\nThe WeCom syncer writes the `userid` that the WeCom provider links by, so synced users sign in directly. The DingTalk and Lark syncers store the directory `userid` / `user_id`, while sign-in links by `openId` / `open_id`. Their first sign-in matches through the binding rule on email or phone instead, which works because both providers report those as verified.\\n\\n## QR login and account recovery\\n\\nQR login moves the password problem to the phone: if a user loses their WeChat or DingTalk account, they cannot scan. Because each of these identities is linked to a Casdoor user rather than being the user, the user can still sign in with a verification code or reset the password through the [forgot-password flow](/docs/application/signin-items-table#forgot-password-flow), as long as the application has an email or SMS provider and the user has an address on file. DingTalk and Lark fill that in from the directory; for WeChat, ask for a phone number at sign-up. To show more than one of these options on the page, see [sign-in methods](/docs/application/signin-methods).\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- DingTalk: get a user token (`userAccessToken`, `expireIn` 7200): https://open.dingtalk.com/document/isvapp-server/obtain-user-token\\n- DingTalk: get the access token of an internal app: https://open.dingtalk.com/document/development/obtain-orgapp-token\\n- DingTalk: obtain user personal information: https://open.dingtalk.com/document/orgapp-server/tutorial-obtaining-user-personal-information\\n- WeCom: get access_token: https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/91039\\n- WeCom: web login component (`wwlogin/sso/login`): https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/98152\\n- WeCom: build the OAuth link and scopes: https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/91022\\n- WeCom: get sensitive member info (`getuserdetail`): https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/95833\\n- WeCom: read member (2022-06-20 field change): https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/90196\\n- Feishu: get user_access_token (v1) and field permissions: https://open.feishu.cn/document/server-docs/authentication-management/access-token/create-2\\n- WeChat: website app login: https://developers.weixin.qq.com/doc/oplatform/Website_App/WeChat_Login/Wechat_Login.html\\n- WeChat: UnionID: https://developers.weixin.qq.com/doc/oplatform/Website_App/WeChat_Login/Authorized_Interface_Calling_UnionID.html\\n- Casdoor providers: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/idp"},{"id":"email-providers-for-verification-codes","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/email-providers-for-verification-codes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-email-providers-for-verification-codes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-email-providers-for-verification-codes.mdx","title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","description":"Pick an email provider for Casdoor sign-up codes, password resets and magic links: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, Azure ACS Email, Amazon SES and plain SMTP.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Email","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/email"},{"inline":true,"label":"SMTP","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/smtp"},{"inline":true,"label":"Brevo","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/brevo"},{"inline":true,"label":"Resend","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/resend"},{"inline":true,"label":"SendGrid","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/send-grid"},{"inline":true,"label":"Azure","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/azure"},{"inline":true,"label":"Deliverability","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/deliverability"}],"readingTime":9.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"email-providers-for-verification-codes","title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","description":"Pick an email provider for Casdoor sign-up codes, password resets and magic links: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, Azure ACS Email, Amazon SES and plain SMTP.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Email","SMTP","Brevo","Resend","SendGrid","Azure","Deliverability"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/dingtalk-wecom-lark-login"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","permalink":"/fr/blog/file-storage-and-cdn"}},"content":"Casdoor sends sign-up verification codes, password reset links and magic links through an Email provider. It has native API types for **Resend**, **SendGrid** and **Azure Communication Services (ACS) Email**, a `Default` SMTP type that works with any SMTP relay (your own server, Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, **Brevo**, **Amazon SES**), and a `Custom HTTP Email` type for any other HTTP API. If you want a free tier and a short setup, start with Resend or Brevo. If you already run on AWS or Azure, SES or ACS keeps billing in one place. Whichever you pick, the deliverability work is the same: a sender domain with SPF and DKIM that align with the From address.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Les options en un coup d\'\u0153il {#the-options-at-a-glance}\\n\\nFree tiers and prices were checked on 2026-11-02 on each vendor\'s own pricing page.\\n\\n| Provider | API or SMTP | Free tier (checked 2026-11-02) | Sender domain setup | Casdoor type |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Your own mail server | SMTP | You run it | You publish SPF, DKIM and DMARC, and need forward and reverse DNS for the IP | `Default` |\\n| Google Workspace (`smtp.gmail.com`) | SMTP with an app password | Part of the Workspace subscription, 2,000 messages per day | Your Workspace domain | `Default` |\\n| Microsoft 365 (`smtp.office365.com`) | SMTP AUTH | Part of the subscription | Your Microsoft 365 domain | `Default` |\\n| Brevo | SMTP relay (`smtp-relay.brevo.com`) | 300 emails per day | Brevo code (TXT), DKIM and DMARC records | `Default` |\\n| Resend | API | 3,000 emails per month, 100 per day | SPF and DKIM records generated by Resend, then DMARC | `Resend` |\\n| SendGrid | API | 60-day trial, 100 emails per day | Domain authentication: CNAME records for SPF and DKIM, TXT for DMARC | `SendGrid` |\\n| Azure Communication Services Email | API | None listed; $0.00025 per email plus $0.00012 per MB | Azure managed subdomain, or a custom domain with SPF and DKIM | `Azure ACS` |\\n| Amazon SES | SMTP interface | No SES-only quota; new AWS accounts get Free Tier credits that SES can use | Verified identity for every From address | `Default` |\\n| Any other HTTP API | HTTP | Depends on the service | Depends on the service | `Custom HTTP Email` |\\n\\nThe [Email providers overview](/docs/provider/email/overview) in the docs lists the same types and the fields each one uses.\\n\\n## SMTP: your own server, Google Workspace, Microsoft 365\\n\\nThe `Default` type takes a host, port, username and password. **SSL mode** `Auto` uses implicit TLS on port 465 and STARTTLS on other ports, which covers almost every relay below.\\n\\nWith your own server, SPF, DKIM signing, reverse DNS and IP reputation are all yours. Google requires valid forward and reverse DNS (PTR) records for the sending IP.\\n\\nGoogle Workspace documents `smtp.gmail.com` on port 465 or 587, signed in with the full Workspace address and an app password, with a limit of 2,000 messages per day.\\n\\nMicrosoft 365 is the one to avoid for new setups. Casdoor\'s SMTP provider signs in with a username and password, which is Basic authentication for SMTP AUTH. Microsoft has announced plans to retire Basic authentication for SMTP AUTH in Exchange Online, and has already turned SMTP AUTH off in tenants that weren\'t using it. Check the latest timeline in the announcement below before you build on it.\\n\\n## Brevo SMTP\\n\\nBrevo sends transactional mail through a standard SMTP relay, so it uses the `Default` type. Most setup problems come from the Brevo SMTP login:\\n\\n- The server is `smtp-relay.brevo.com`, on port 587, 465 or 2525. Port 465 needs SSL, which `Auto` handles.\\n- The SMTP login is not always your account email. Depending on when the account was created, it is either your login email or a generated address like `[ID]@smtp-brevo.com`, and it is shown on the **SMTP** page in Brevo. Changing your account email doesn\'t change it.\\n- The password is an **SMTP key**, not an API key.\\n\\nIn Casdoor, set **Type** to `Default` and put those values in **Host**, **Port**, **Username** and **Password**. The [Brevo guide](/docs/provider/email/brevo) has screenshots. For the domain, Brevo asks for its own TXT verification record, DKIM and DMARC; SPF is only needed with a dedicated IP.\\n\\n## Resend\\n\\nThe `Resend` type needs only an API key in **Secret key**, plus a **From address** on a verified domain. Leave **Host**, **Port** and **Username** empty. Casdoor formats the sender as `Name <address>` from **From name**, or from the organization\'s display name when **From name** is empty.\\n\\nTwo things to know before you test. First, the shared `onboarding@resend.dev` sender can only send to the email address of your own Resend account; any other recipient gets a 403. Second, verifying your own domain means adding the SPF and DKIM records Resend generates, and Resend recommends sending from a subdomain to keep its reputation separate. The free plan covers 3,000 emails a month with a cap of 100 per day; the Pro plan starts at $20 a month for 50,000. See the [Resend guide](/docs/provider/email/resend).\\n\\n## SendGrid\\n\\nThe `SendGrid` type uses an API key. Leave **Host** and **Endpoint** empty and Casdoor uses SendGrid\'s standard send API; fill both only if you need a different endpoint. Casdoor treats anything but `202 Accepted` as a failure and shows the error messages SendGrid returns, which helps when a sender isn\'t verified yet.\\n\\nSendGrid\'s pricing page lists a 60-day free trial at 100 emails a day, then Essentials from $19.95 a month. Domain authentication with automated security adds a CNAME for SPF and DKIM and a TXT record for DMARC. See the [SendGrid guide](/docs/provider/email/sendgrid).\\n\\n## ACS Email (Azure Communication Services)\\n\\nThe `Azure ACS` type takes the resource endpoint as **Host** and the access key as **Secret key**; Casdoor signs each request with that key. Azure gives you a one-click managed subdomain under `azurecomm.net` with authentication already set up, and Microsoft suggests using it for development and switching to a custom domain, with its SPF and DKIM records, for launch. There is no free tier on the pricing page: $0.00025 per email plus $0.00012 per MB transferred. See the [Azure ACS guide](/docs/provider/email/azureACS).\\n\\n## Amazon SES\\n\\nCasdoor has no SES API type, so use the SES SMTP interface with the `Default` type. The host is the Region\'s SMTP endpoint, such as `email-smtp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com`; SES accepts STARTTLS on 25, 587 or 2587 and implicit TLS on 465 or 2465. The SMTP username and password are SMTP credentials you generate in SES. They are not your AWS access keys, and they are different in each Region.\\n\\nNew accounts start in the SES sandbox: mail goes only to verified addresses, at most 200 messages per 24 hours and one per second. Request production access before real users sign up. SES charges $0.10 per 1,000 outbound emails at its \xe0 la carte rate, with no minimum fee.\\n\\n## Custom HTTP Email\\n\\nFor a service without a native type, `Custom HTTP Email` sends `fromAddress`, `fromName`, `toAddress` (or `toAddresses` for several recipients), `subject` and `content` as a form or JSON body, with headers you define. You can rename each field to match the target API. Casdoor counts only HTTP 200 as success, so an API that answers `202 Accepted` shows up as an error; a small adapter in front fixes that. See [Send email through your own HTTP API](/docs/provider/email/customHttp).\\n\\n## Deliverability basics for auth mail\\n\\nVerification codes are useless if they land in spam. The rules that matter:\\n\\n- **SPF and DKIM.** Google requires every sender to Gmail to use SPF or DKIM. Above 5,000 messages a day it requires both, plus DMARC (a `p=none` policy is accepted). Set up all three from the start.\\n- **Alignment.** The domain in the From header must match the SPF domain or the DKIM domain, or DMARC fails. This is why each provider makes you verify the domain you send from.\\n- **A separate subdomain.** Yahoo recommends separating transactional mail from marketing mail by IP or DKIM domain, and Resend recommends subdomains. Something like `auth.example.com` keeps a bad newsletter from hurting your password resets.\\n- **TLS and spam rate.** Google requires TLS and asks you to keep the reported spam rate below 0.3%, ideally below 0.1%.\\n- **No unsubscribe header needed.** Google\'s FAQ says one-click unsubscribe applies to marketing mail, and names password reset messages as an example of transactional mail that is excluded.\\n- **Simple content.** Casdoor sends the template as one HTML body, without a separate plain-text part, for every built-in type. Keep the HTML light, put the code in plain text, and show the full link as text next to the button, as Casdoor\'s built-in magic link email does. Use your Casdoor origin directly rather than a link shortener, so the link domain matches your brand.\\n\\n## Configure and test the provider in Casdoor\\n\\n1. Go to **Identity** > **Providers**, add a provider, set **Category** to `Email` and choose the **Type** from the table above.\\n2. Set **From address** and, optionally, **From name**.\\n3. Write **Email title** and **Email content**. The content can use `%s` for the code, `%{user.friendlyName}` for the user\'s name, and `%link` inside a `<reset-link>` block for the password reset link. Magic link emails use `%link` and `%expireTime`; if your content has no `%link`, Casdoor sends its own magic link email instead. The [templates section](/docs/provider/email/overview#email-content-and-placeholders) has examples. One thing to edit: the default template says the code is valid for 5 minutes, but codes and magic links expire after `verificationCodeTimeout` in `conf/app.conf`, which defaults to 10 minutes.\\n4. Click **Test SMTP Connection** to open a connection without sending (SMTP types only), then enter an address in **Test Email** and click **Send Testing Email**. The test uses the values in the form, saved or not, with `%s` replaced by `123456` and the reset link block removed.\\n5. Add the provider to the application with a rule such as `Signup`, `Forget Password`, `Magic link` or `All`. You can use different providers for different rules. See [Add providers to an application](/docs/application/providers#provider-rules).\\n6. Set `origin` in `conf/app.conf`. Without it, unless the request comes in on a loopback host such as `localhost`, Casdoor leaves the reset link out of password reset emails and refuses to send magic links. See [Sign in with a magic link](/docs/application/signin-methods#magic-link).\\n\\nFor local development, point a `Default` provider at a fake SMTP server instead of a real account; [MailHog vs Mailpit](/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit) covers that setup.\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- Casdoor source: [`email/provider.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/email/provider.go), [`object/email.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/email.go)\\n- [Resend pricing](https://resend.com/pricing) and [the resend.dev 403 error](https://resend.com/docs/knowledge-base/403-error-resend-dev-domain)\\n- [Twilio SendGrid Email API pricing](https://www.twilio.com/en-us/products/email-api/pricing) and [domain authentication](https://www.twilio.com/docs/sendgrid/ui/account-and-settings/how-to-set-up-domain-authentication)\\n- [Brevo pricing](https://www.brevo.com/pricing/) and [Send transactional emails using Brevo SMTP](https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/7924908994450)\\n- [Azure Communication Services pricing](https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/details/communication-services/) and [email domains and sender authentication](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/communication-services/concepts/email/email-domain-and-sender-authentication)\\n- [Amazon SES pricing](https://aws.amazon.com/ses/pricing/), [SES SMTP endpoints](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/smtp-connect.html) and [moving out of the sandbox](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/request-production-access.html)\\n- [Google email sender guidelines](https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126) and [FAQ](https://support.google.com/a/answer/14229414)\\n- [Yahoo sender best practices](https://senders.yahooinc.com/best-practices/)\\n- [Google Workspace: send email from a printer, scanner, or app](https://support.google.com/a/answer/176600)\\n- [Updated Exchange Online SMTP AUTH Basic authentication deprecation timeline](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/exchange/updated-exchange-online-smtp-auth-basic-authentication-deprecation-timeline/4489835)"},{"id":"file-storage-and-cdn","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/file-storage-and-cdn","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-file-storage-and-cdn-for-casdoor.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-file-storage-and-cdn-for-casdoor.mdx","title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","description":"Where Casdoor keeps avatars and uploaded files (local disk, Amazon S3, MinIO, Azure Blob, GCS, OSS, COS, Synology) and how to serve them through a CDN.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/storage"},{"inline":true,"label":"CDN","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/cdn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Amazon S3","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/amazon-s-3"},{"inline":true,"label":"MinIO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/min-io"},{"inline":true,"label":"Azure Blob","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/azure-blob"},{"inline":true,"label":"Object Storage","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/object-storage"}],"readingTime":9.79,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"file-storage-and-cdn","title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","description":"Where Casdoor keeps avatars and uploaded files (local disk, Amazon S3, MinIO, Azure Blob, GCS, OSS, COS, Synology) and how to serve them through a CDN.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Storage","CDN","Amazon S3","MinIO","Azure Blob","Object Storage"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","permalink":"/fr/blog/email-providers-for-verification-codes"},"nextItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit : Test des e-mails de v\xe9rification et de r\xe9initialisation de mot de passe avec Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"}},"content":"To host static files on a CDN, put them in object storage (Amazon S3, MinIO, Azure Blob Storage, Google Cloud Storage, Alibaba Cloud OSS or Tencent Cloud COS), make the CDN pull from that bucket as its origin, and hand out URLs on the CDN\'s domain. In Casdoor this is one setting: create a storage provider for the bucket and put the CDN address in its **Domain** field. Casdoor uploads avatars and other files through the provider\'s API and saves URLs that start with that domain. Casdoor\'s own frontend can come from a CDN as well, through `frontendCdnUrl` and `staticBaseUrl` in `app.conf`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Storage providers at a glance\\n\\nA storage provider decides where Casdoor writes uploaded files: user avatars, ID card images, terms-of-use pages, and anything uploaded on the **Resources** page. You add the provider to an application, and uploads made in that application go to it. The [storage provider overview](/docs/provider/storage/overview) lists all the types. This table covers the ones people usually ask about:\\n\\n| Storage | Casdoor provider type | Does Casdoor use the S3 API? | Self-hostable | How the public URL is set |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Local file system | `Local File System` | No, plain files on disk | Yes, it is the Casdoor server | Served by Casdoor under `/files/`. **Domain** is read-only |\\n| Amazon S3 | `AWS S3` | Yes | No | **Domain**: the bucket URL or a CloudFront domain. **Endpoint (Intranet)**: a VPC endpoint |\\n| MinIO | `MinIO` | Yes, with path-style requests | Yes | **Domain**: the MinIO URL followed by the bucket name, or a CDN domain |\\n| Alibaba Cloud OSS | `Aliyun OSS` | No, the OSS SDK | No | **Domain**: a CDN domain. Left empty, it becomes `bucket.endpoint`. **Endpoint (Intranet)**: the internal endpoint |\\n| Tencent Cloud COS | `Tencent Cloud COS` | No, the COS API | No | **Domain**: a CDN domain |\\n| Azure Blob Storage | `Azure Blob` | No, the Blob API | No | **Domain**: a custom or Front Door domain. Casdoor adds the container name to the path |\\n| Google Cloud Storage | `Google Cloud Storage` | No, Google\'s Go client | No | **Domain**: left empty, it becomes `https://storage.googleapis.com`. Casdoor adds the bucket name to the path |\\n| Synology NAS | `Synology` | No, the DSM File Station API | Yes | No **Domain** field. The URL is based on **Endpoint** |\\n\\nCasdoor also supports `Qiniu Cloud Kodo`, `CUCloud OSS`, and `Casdoor`, which stores files in another Casdoor instance. The per-provider guides for [Amazon S3](/docs/provider/storage/amazon-s3), [MinIO](/docs/provider/storage/minio), [Azure Blob](/docs/provider/storage/azure), [Google Cloud Storage](/docs/provider/storage/google-cloudstorage), [Alibaba Cloud OSS](/docs/provider/storage/aliyun-oss), [Tencent Cloud COS](/docs/provider/storage/tencentCloudCOS), [Synology NAS](/docs/provider/storage/synology-nas) and the [local file system](/docs/provider/storage/localFileSystem) show which value goes in which field.\\n\\n## Endpoint vs. Domain\\n\\nThese two fields do different jobs, and most CDN problems come from mixing them up.\\n\\n- **Endpoint** is the address Casdoor uses to talk to the storage API: upload, delete, list. The CDN is not involved.\\n- **Endpoint (Intranet)** replaces Endpoint when `isCloudIntranet = true` is set in `app.conf`. That keeps upload traffic on the provider\'s internal network when Casdoor runs in the same cloud. The field appears for AWS S3, Aliyun OSS, Azure Blob and CUCloud OSS.\\n- **Domain** is the base of the URL that Casdoor saves and that browsers request. If you have a CDN, its domain goes here.\\n- **Path prefix** is put in front of every object key. With a prefix of `casdoor`, an avatar ends up under `casdoor/avatar/...`.\\n\\nCasdoor builds the file URL from the scheme and host of Domain, then the path of Domain, then the path prefix, then the file path. A Domain without a scheme gets `https://`. The path part of Domain also becomes part of the object key, so `https://cdn.example.com/casdoor/` stores files under `casdoor/` in the bucket. For Azure Blob and Google Cloud Storage, Casdoor adds the container or bucket name as the first path segment after the host. Your CDN needs to serve that path.\\n\\nIf Domain is empty, Casdoor fills it in from the provider\'s endpoint the first time it uses the provider, and saves it. Sometimes the result works, as with OSS and GCS. Sometimes it doesn\'t: for an S3 endpoint without the bucket name, or for MinIO, where path-style requests put the bucket name in the path, the generated URL misses the bucket. Set Domain yourself, upload one test avatar, and open the URL Casdoor returns before anyone else uses it.\\n\\nThe provider that Casdoor uses for avatars it copies from social logins comes from `defaultStorageProvider` in `app.conf`. It is the name of a provider owned by `admin` (see the [configuration reference](/docs/basic/configuration)):\\n\\n```ini\\ndefaultStorageProvider = provider_storage_s3\\nisCloudIntranet = false\\n```\\n\\n## Public buckets, private buckets and signed URLs\\n\\nCasdoor saves one plain, permanent URL in the user\'s `avatar` field and in the resource record. It doesn\'t create signed or expiring URLs for these files. So whatever is at Domain has to answer an anonymous `GET`. There are two ways to do that.\\n\\n**Make the objects public.** With `AWS S3` and `MinIO`, Casdoor uploads every object with the `public-read` canned ACL. With `Aliyun OSS`, the client library does the same by default. On S3 this conflicts with the defaults for new buckets: according to AWS, new buckets use the **Bucket owner enforced** setting, which disables ACLs, and an upload that sets an ACL such as `public-read` fails with `AccessControlListNotSupported`. That is why the [Amazon S3 guide](/docs/provider/storage/amazon-s3) has you turn on ACLs and turn off **Block all public access**. On OSS, an object ACL takes precedence over the bucket ACL, so a `public-read` object can be read even in a private bucket. MinIO doesn\'t use the ACL for anonymous reads. It uses a bucket policy:\\n\\n```bash\\nmc anonymous set download myminio/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nAzure needs anonymous access allowed on the storage account and the container set to the **Blob** access level. Google Cloud Storage needs the Storage Object Viewer role (`roles/storage.objectViewer`) granted to `allUsers` on the bucket.\\n\\n**Keep the bucket private and let only the CDN read it.** The CDN signs its requests to the origin, and viewers go through the CDN without a signature. Each vendor has its own version:\\n\\n- CloudFront has origin access control (OAC). AWS requires **Bucket owner enforced** for OAC, or **Bucket owner preferred** if you need ACLs.\\n- Alibaba Cloud CDN can fetch from a private OSS bucket after you authorize it.\\n- Tencent Cloud CDN has origin-pull authentication for private COS buckets.\\n- Azure Front Door Premium can reach a storage account over Private Link.\\n- Google\'s Cloud CDN guide covers private bucket access too, where the Storage Object Viewer role goes to a service account instead of `allUsers`.\\n\\nThis hides the bucket\'s own URL, but the files are still public through the CDN. With the `AWS S3` and `Aliyun OSS` types, the objects also stay readable at their bucket URLs, because of the `public-read` ACL that Casdoor sets.\\n\\nIf avatars must not be public at all, a CDN in front of object storage is the wrong tool. Keep those files on a storage type you control, and limit who can reach that host.\\n\\n## Cache headers and the avatar trap\\n\\nOn S3 and MinIO, Casdoor sets `Content-Type` from the file extension when it uploads. It doesn\'t set `Cache-Control` on any provider. That means the CDN\'s default TTL applies. On CloudFront, objects without `Cache-Control` are cached for the default TTL, which is 24 hours when you don\'t use a cache policy. To control this per object, add `Cache-Control` metadata in the bucket. For a whole path, use a cache rule on the CDN.\\n\\nAvatars need a short TTL. When a user changes their picture, Casdoor writes the new file to the same key, `avatar/<organization>/<username>.<extension>`, and saves the same URL. A long TTL means people see the old picture until it expires or until you purge or invalidate that path. Other uploads get a numbered suffix (`-1`, `-2`, ...) when the name is already taken, so a new upload gets a new URL and can be cached for a long time.\\n\\nA layout that works:\\n\\n| Path | Suggested caching |\\n|---|---|\\n| `avatar/*` | Short TTL (minutes), or purge on change |\\n| `resource/*` and other uploads | Long TTL |\\n| Hashed frontend assets (`assets/*`) | Very long TTL. The file name changes on every build |\\n\\n## Casdoor\'s own frontend on a CDN\\n\\nSeparate from user uploads, there are three ways to move Casdoor\'s own static files off the Casdoor server.\\n\\n**`frontendCdnUrl`.** Use this if you install the frontend from the `casdoor-web` npm package. Point it at an npm CDN, for example:\\n\\n```ini\\nfrontendCdnUrl = \\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/{name}@{version}\\"\\n```\\n\\nCasdoor reads the package name and version from the `package.json` in the build folder. It rewrites the script and stylesheet links in `index.html` to that CDN and keeps the local paths as a fallback. If a file fails to load, the browser loads it from Casdoor, and a cookie keeps that browser on local files for the rest of the session. A frontend you build yourself has no such `package.json`, so the setting has no effect on it.\\n\\n**The deployment script.** For a frontend you build from source, [Host the frontend files on a CDN](/docs/deployment/deploy-cdn) explains `deployment/deploy_test.go`. It uploads `web/build/assets/` through one of your storage providers and points `index.html` at the provider\'s Domain, which must end with `/`. Vite hashes these file names, so they suit the long TTL in the table above.\\n\\n**`staticBaseUrl`.** This sets where default images such as logos, provider icons and the default avatar come from. The default is `https://cdn.casbin.org`, which serves the [casbin/static](https://github.com/casbin/static) repository. On a network without internet access, serve a copy of that repository yourself and point `staticBaseUrl` at it, as described in [Host static files on an intranet](/docs/deployment/deploy-intranet).\\n\\n## Picking one\\n\\n- **One server, small user base:** `Local File System`. Mount the `files` directory on a volume if Casdoor runs in a container. Casdoor sends `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and a sandboxing `Content-Security-Policy` with these files, so an uploaded HTML or SVG file can\'t run script on your Casdoor domain.\\n- **Self-hosted and S3-shaped:** `MinIO`. It is also the type the docs suggest for other S3-compatible stores.\\n- **Already on a cloud:** use that cloud\'s object storage and CDN, set **Endpoint (Intranet)** where Casdoor runs in the same cloud, and set Domain to the CDN.\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- [Casdoor storage providers](/docs/provider/storage/overview) and the source: [`object/storage.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/storage.go), [`storage/`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/storage), [`routers/frontend_cdn.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/routers/frontend_cdn.go)\\n- AWS: [Controlling ownership of objects and disabling ACLs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/about-object-ownership.html), [Restrict access to an Amazon S3 origin](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-s3.html), [Manage how long content stays in the cache](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/Expiration.html)\\n- MinIO: [`mc anonymous set`](https://docs.min.io/community/minio-object-store/reference/minio-mc/mc-anonymous-set.html)\\n- Microsoft: [Configure anonymous read access for containers and blobs](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/storage/blobs/anonymous-read-access-configure), [Integrate an Azure Storage account with Azure Front Door](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/frontdoor/integrate-storage-account)\\n- Google Cloud: [Making data public](https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/making-data-public), [Set up Cloud CDN with a backend bucket](https://cloud.google.com/cdn/docs/setting-up-cdn-with-bucket)\\n- Alibaba Cloud: [Object ACL](https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/oss/user-guide/object-acl), [Origin fetch from private OSS buckets](https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/cdn/user-guide/grant-alibaba-cloud-cdn-access-permissions-on-private-oss-buckets)\\n- Tencent Cloud: [Setting CDN Acceleration for COS](https://www.tencentcloud.com/document/product/436/18670)"},{"id":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit : Test des e-mails de v\xe9rification et de r\xe9initialisation de mot de passe avec Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit : les deux \xe9coutent sur le port SMTP 1025 et le port d\'interface web 8025. Comparez la maintenance, les images Docker et les API de test, puis configurez Casdoor pour utiliser l\'un ou l\'autre.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Email","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/email"},{"inline":true,"label":"MailHog","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/mail-hog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mailpit","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/mailpit"},{"inline":true,"label":"Testing","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/testing"},{"inline":true,"label":"SMTP","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/smtp"},{"inline":true,"label":"CI","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/ci"}],"readingTime":9.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit : Test des e-mails de v\xe9rification et de r\xe9initialisation de mot de passe avec Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit : les deux \xe9coutent sur le port SMTP 1025 et le port d\'interface web 8025. Comparez la maintenance, les images Docker et les API de test, puis configurez Casdoor pour utiliser l\'un ou l\'autre.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Email","MailHog","Mailpit","Testing","SMTP","CI"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","permalink":"/fr/blog/file-storage-and-cdn"},"nextItem":{"title":"URIs de redirection OAuth MCP pour Cursor, Claude et ChatGPT : Enregistrement des clients MCP dans Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"}},"content":"MailHog et Mailpit sont tous deux de faux serveurs SMTP pour le d\xe9veloppement : ils acceptent chaque message, le conservent et l\'affichent dans une interface web au lieu de le livrer. Les deux utilisent les m\xeames ports par d\xe9faut, **SMTP sur 1025 et l\'interface web et l\'API sur 8025**, donc l\'adresse SMTP est `smtp://127.0.0.1:1025` et la bo\xeete de r\xe9ception est \xe0 `http://127.0.0.1:8025` pour l\'un ou l\'autre. La diff\xe9rence r\xe9side dans la maintenance : la derni\xe8re version de MailHog, v1.0.1, est sortie en ao\xfbt 2020, tandis que Mailpit a publi\xe9 la v1.33.0 le 31 octobre 2026. Pour un nouveau projet, utilisez Mailpit. Casdoor envoie aux deux via un fournisseur SMTP simple, vous pouvez donc tester les codes d\'inscription, les e-mails de r\xe9initialisation de mot de passe et les liens magiques sans un vrai compte de messagerie.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## MailHog vs Mailpit en un coup d\'\u0153il {#mailhog-vs-mailpit-at-a-glance}\\n\\n| | MailHog | Mailpit |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Derni\xe8re version | v1.0.1, 2020-08-11 | v1.33.0, 2026-10-31 |\\n| Dernier commit sur la branche par d\xe9faut | 2022-08-02 | 2026-10-31 |\\n| License | MIT | MIT |\\n| Default SMTP port | 1025 | 1025 |\\n| Default web UI and API port | 8025 | 8025 |\\n| Docker image | `mailhog/mailhog`, amd64 only | `axllent/mailpit`, amd64, arm64 and 386 |\\n| HTTP API | v1 and v2: list, get, delete, search by `from`, `to` or `containing` | v1: list, get, delete, search with filters such as `to:` and `subject:`, `latest` message shortcut |\\n| Default storage | In memory | Temporary SQLite database, deleted on exit |\\n| Persistent storage | MongoDB or Maildir | SQLite file (`MP_DATABASE`) or rqlite |\\n| Message limit | None documented | Keeps the newest 500 by default (`MP_MAX_MESSAGES`) |\\n| HTML check, link check, spam check | No | Yes |\\n| Webhook on new mail | No | Yes (`MP_WEBHOOK_URL`) |\\n| SMTP TLS | Not offered | Optional STARTTLS or SSL/TLS |\\n| Failure injection | Jim | Chaos |\\n| Release a message to a real SMTP server | Yes | Yes |\\n| POP3 server | No | Yes, optional |\\n| Basic auth for the web UI | Yes | Yes |\\n\\nLes statistiques de Docker Hub montrent que beaucoup de personnes t\xe9l\xe9chargent encore MailHog. Cela refl\xe8te les anciens tutoriels et fichiers CI, pas une activit\xe9 r\xe9elle : un ticket ouvert sur le d\xe9p\xf4t MailHog intitul\xe9 \\"THIS PROJECT IS DEAD!\\" contient une r\xe9ponse de l\'auteur en 2023 indiquant qu\'il n\'a plus le temps de maintenir le projet. Le README de Mailpit se d\xe9crit comme inspir\xe9 par MailHog et pointe vers ce m\xeame commentaire.\\n\\nL\'architecture de l\'image est plus importante qu\'elle ne le para\xeet. Sur un ordinateur portable Apple Silicon ou un runner CI ARM, `mailhog/mailhog` fonctionne uniquement en \xe9mulation, tandis que `axllent/mailpit` dispose d\'une image native arm64.\\n\\n## Ex\xe9cutez l\'un ou l\'autre avec Docker {#run-either-one-with-docker}\\n\\nMailHog:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailhog -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 mailhog/mailhog\\n```\\n\\nMailpit:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nMailpit conserve les e-mails dans une base de donn\xe9es temporaire qui dispara\xeet lorsque le conteneur s\'arr\xeate. Pour la conserver entre les red\xe9marrages, montez un volume et d\xe9finissez `MP_DATABASE`:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit \\\\\\n  -v \\"$PWD/mailpit-data:/data\\" \\\\\\n  -e MP_DATABASE=/data/mailpit.db \\\\\\n  -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 \\\\\\n  axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nSans Docker, `brew install mailhog` et `brew install mailpit` fonctionnent tous deux sous macOS, et Mailpit fournit des binaires statiques pour Windows, Linux et macOS sur sa page de versions.\\n\\nDans GitHub Actions, ex\xe9cutez-le comme conteneur de service \xe0 c\xf4t\xe9 de votre job de test:\\n\\n```yaml\\nservices:\\n  mailpit:\\n    image: axllent/mailpit\\n    ports:\\n      - 1025:1025\\n      - 8025:8025\\n```\\n\\n## Configurer Casdoor avec MailHog ou Mailpit {#point-casdoor-at-mailhog-or-mailpit}\\n\\nCasdoor ne poss\xe8de pas de type de fournisseur distinct pour MailHog ou Mailpit, et il n\'en a pas besoin. Le type d\'e-mail `Default` envoie via SMTP, et ces deux outils sont des serveurs SMTP.\\n\\n1. En tant qu\'administrateur global, ajoutez un fournisseur dans l\'organisation `admin` ou `built-in`.\\n1. D\xe9finissez **Category** sur `Email` et **Type** sur `Default`.\\n1. D\xe9finissez **Host** sur l\'endroit o\xf9 l\'outil s\'ex\xe9cute et **Port** sur `1025`.\\n1. Laissez **Username** et **Password** vides.\\n1. Cliquez sur **Test SMTP Connection**, puis sur **Send Testing Email**, et v\xe9rifiez l\'interface web sur le port 8025.\\n\\nLes pages \xe9tape par \xe9tape avec captures d\'\xe9cran sont [Test email with MailHog](/docs/provider/email/mailhog) et [Test email with Mailpit](/docs/provider/email/mailpit).\\n\\nQuelques d\xe9tails d\xe9terminent si cela fonctionne du premier coup:\\n\\n**Laissez le mode SSL sur Auto, ou d\xe9finissez-le sur Disable.** Dans le mode `Auto`, Casdoor utilise le TLS implicite uniquement sur le port 465 et effectue une mise \xe0 niveau avec STARTTLS uniquement si le serveur le propose. Ni MailHog ni Mailpit par d\xe9faut ne proposent STARTTLS sur le port 1025, donc la connexion reste en clair. Ne choisissez pas `Enable` : cela force le TLS implicite, et la poign\xe9e de main \xe9choue contre un serveur en texte brut. Le [tableau des modes SSL](/docs/provider/email/overview#ssl-mode) d\xe9taille le comportement complet.\\n\\n**Laissez les identifiants vides.** Casdoor se connecte au serveur SMTP uniquement lorsque **Username** est d\xe9fini. Si vous le remplissez, l\'authentification `PLAIN` de Go refuse d\'envoyer le mot de passe sur une connexion non chiffr\xe9e sauf si l\'h\xf4te est localhost, ce qui fait qu\'un MailHog situ\xe9 sur `192.168.x.x` \xe9choue avec une erreur d\'authentification bien que MailHog accepte n\'importe quel login. Mailpit par d\xe9faut ne demande aucune authentification.\\n\\n**Utilisez le bon h\xf4te.** `127.0.0.1` fonctionne uniquement lorsque Casdoor et le collecteur d\'e-mails tournent sur la m\xeame machine. Si les deux tournent dans Docker Compose, utilisez le nom du service, tel que `mailpit`, comme h\xf4te.\\n\\n**Utilisez un fournisseur global.** Un fournisseur appartenant \xe0 une organisation ordinaire ne peut atteindre que des adresses Internet, il ne peut donc pas se connecter \xe0 `127.0.0.1` ou \xe0 une IP priv\xe9e. Les fournisseurs dans `admin` ou `built-in` ne sont pas restreints. Voir [Quelles adresses un fournisseur peut-il atteindre](/docs/provider/overview#which-addresses-a-provider-can-reach).\\n\\n**D\xe9finissez `origin` pour les liens.** Les codes de v\xe9rification fonctionnent imm\xe9diatement. Les liens de r\xe9initialisation de mot de passe et les liens magiques n\xe9cessitent que Casdoor connaisse son URL publique, via `origin` dans `conf/app.conf` ou une requ\xeate sur un h\xf4te loopback. Sinon, le lien de r\xe9initialisation est omis de l\'e-mail et les liens magiques \xe9chouent. Voir [Mod\xe8les](/docs/provider/email/overview#email-content-and-placeholders).\\n\\nEnfin, ajoutez le fournisseur \xe0 votre application dans [Add providers to an application](/docs/application/providers) afin que l\'inscription et la r\xe9initialisation de mot de passe l\'utilisent r\xe9ellement.\\n\\n## Lire le code de v\xe9rification dans un test automatis\xe9 {#read-the-verification-code-in-an-automated-test}\\n\\nLes codes de v\xe9rification de Casdoor comportent six chiffres, et le mod\xe8le par d\xe9faut indique \\"Here is your code: \\" suivi du code. Un test de bout en bout inscrit un utilisateur, demande \xe0 Casdoor d\'envoyer le code, puis le lit depuis l\'API du collecteur d\'e-mails.\\n\\nAvec Mailpit, recherchez par destinataire et ouvrez la correspondance la plus r\xe9cente. `GET /api/v1/search` renvoie les correspondances de la plus r\xe9cente \xe0 la plus ancienne, et `GET /api/v1/message/{ID}` renvoie les corps `Text` et `HTML` analys\xe9s:\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { request } from \\"@playwright/test\\";\\n\\nconst MAILPIT = process.env.MAILPIT_URL ?? \\"http://127.0.0.1:8025\\";\\n\\nexport async function latestCode(to: string): Promise<string> {\\n  const api = await request.newContext({ baseURL: MAILPIT });\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {\\n    const res = await api.get(\\"/api/v1/search\\", {\\n      params: { query: `to:\\"${to}\\"`, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n    });\\n    const { messages } = await res.json();\\n    if (messages.length > 0) {\\n      const msg = await (await api.get(`/api/v1/message/${messages[0].ID}`)).json();\\n      const match = (msg.Text || msg.HTML).match(/code: (\\\\d{6})/);\\n      if (match) return match[1];\\n    }\\n    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 500));\\n  }\\n  throw new Error(`no verification email for ${to}`);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSi un seul test envoie des e-mails \xe0 la fois, `GET /api/v1/message/latest` ignore l\'\xe9tape de recherche. Avant chaque test, `DELETE /api/v1/messages` sans corps vide la bo\xeete de r\xe9ception.\\n\\nAvec MailHog, l\'appel le plus proche est `GET /api/v2/search?kind=to&query=<address>`. Les messages sont renvoy\xe9s du plus r\xe9cent au plus ancien dans `items`, et le corps se trouve dans `items[0].Content.Body` :\\n\\n```ts\\nconst res = await api.get(\\"/api/v2/search\\", {\\n  params: { kind: \\"to\\", query: to, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n});\\nconst { items } = await res.json();\\nconst body: string = items[0]?.Content.Body ?? \\"\\";\\n```\\n\\n`Content.Body` est le corps MIME brut. Casdoor utilise l\'encodage quoted-printable, donc `=` devient `=3D` et les lignes longues sont coup\xe9es par des sauts de ligne souples termin\xe9s par `=`. D\xe9codez-le d\'abord, ou gardez le code pr\xe8s du d\xe9but d\'un mod\xe8le court. Videz MailHog avec `DELETE /api/v1/messages`.\\n\\n## Migration de MailHog vers Mailpit {#migrating-from-mailhog-to-mailpit}\\n\\nComme les ports correspondent, la plupart des migrations n\xe9cessitent une modification d\'une seule ligne dans `docker-compose.yml` : remplacez `mailhog/mailhog` par `axllent/mailpit`. Le fournisseur de Casdoor conserve le m\xeame h\xf4te et le m\xeame port. Ce qui change :\\n\\n- **Param\xe8tres.** MailHog lit les variables `MH_*` ; Mailpit lit les variables `MP_*`. Si vous utilisiez `MH_STORAGE=maildir` ou MongoDB, passez \xe0 `MP_DATABASE`.\\n- **Helpers de test.** Mailpit n\'a pas d\'endpoints `/api/v2`. R\xe9\xe9crivez les appels \xe0 `/api/v2/messages` et `/api/v2/search` pour utiliser `/api/v1/messages` et `/api/v1/search`, et lisez `messages[].ID` ainsi que `Text` ou `HTML` au lieu de `items[].Content.Body`. Le d\xe9codage quoted-printable dispara\xeet.\\n- **Syntaxe de recherche.** `kind=to&query=a@example.com` devient `query=to:\\"a@example.com\\"`.\\n- **Injection de pannes.** Jim n\'a pas de drapeaux (flags) dans Mailpit ; utilisez Chaos.\\n- **Authentification.** Si une application exige une connexion via SMTP en clair, d\xe9marrez Mailpit avec `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ACCEPT_ANY=1` et `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE=1`. Pour Casdoor, il est plus simple de laisser les identifiants vides.\\n- **R\xe9tention.** Par d\xe9faut, Mailpit ne conserve que les 500 messages les plus r\xe9cents. Augmentez `MP_MAX_MESSAGES` si une longue s\xe9rie de tests n\xe9cessite davantage de stockage.\\n\\n## Passage en production {#going-to-production}\\n\\nMailHog et Mailpit ne livrent jamais d\'e-mails aux vraies bo\xeetes de r\xe9ception sauf si vous lib\xe9rez manuellement les messages, la production n\xe9cessite donc un vrai fournisseur. Dans Casdoor, vous \xe9changez le fournisseur, pas les mod\xe8les. Les mod\xe8les d\'e-mail, le placeholder de code `%s` et le bloc `<reset-link>` restent identiques :\\n\\n- Tout serveur SMTP de type `Default`, d\xe9crit dans [Fournisseurs d\'e-mail](/docs/provider/email/overview)\\n- [SendGrid](/docs/provider/email/sendgrid) et [Resend](/docs/provider/email/resend) via leurs API\\n- [Brevo](/docs/provider/email/brevo) via son relais SMTP\\n- [Azure Communication Services](/docs/provider/email/azureACS)\\n- Votre propre endpoint HTTP avec [Custom HTTP Email](/docs/provider/email/customHttp)\\n\\nUne configuration courante consiste \xe0 garder Mailpit en d\xe9veloppement et CI, et le vrai fournisseur en production. Chaque environnement pointe son application vers un fournisseur diff\xe9rent, afin que le flux d\'inscription que vous testez soit celui qui sera d\xe9ploy\xe9.\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- D\xe9p\xf4t et README de MailHog : https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog\\n- Configuration de MailHog et documentation API v1/v2 : https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/tree/master/docs\\n- Commentaire sur la maintenance de MailHog : https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/issues/442#issuecomment-1493415258\\n- D\xe9p\xf4t et README de Mailpit : https://github.com/axllent/mailpit\\n- Documentation de Mailpit (Docker, stockage, SMTP, webhook, filtres de recherche) : https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/\\n- R\xe9f\xe9rence API v1 de Mailpit : https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/api-v1/\\n- Code du fournisseur d\'e-mail de Casdoor : https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/email"},{"id":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","title":"URIs de redirection OAuth MCP pour Cursor, Claude et ChatGPT : Enregistrement des clients MCP dans Casdoor","description":"Les URL de rappel OAuth utilis\xe9es par Cursor, Claude et ChatGPT pour les serveurs MCP, l\'emplacement de claude_desktop_config.json, et comment enregistrer chaque client dans Casdoor.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cursor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/cursor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Claude","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/claude"},{"inline":true,"label":"ChatGPT","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/chat-gpt"},{"inline":true,"label":"Dynamic Client Registration","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/dynamic-client-registration"}],"readingTime":8.34,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","title":"URIs de redirection OAuth MCP pour Cursor, Claude et ChatGPT : Enregistrement des clients MCP dans Casdoor","description":"Les URL de rappel OAuth utilis\xe9es par Cursor, Claude et ChatGPT pour les serveurs MCP, l\'emplacement de claude_desktop_config.json, et comment enregistrer chaque client dans Casdoor.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","MCP","OAuth","Cursor","Claude","ChatGPT","Dynamic Client Registration"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit : Test des e-mails de v\xe9rification et de r\xe9initialisation de mot de passe avec Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"},"nextItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO : Protection de toute application derri\xe8re Nginx avec Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"}},"content":"Chaque client MCP envoie sa propre URI de redirection lors de la connexion \xe0 un serveur MCP prot\xe9g\xe9 par OAuth. Cursor desktop utilise `http://localhost:8787/callback` (les versions ant\xe9rieures \xe0 mi-2026 utilisaient `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback`), Cursor web et Cursor Cloud Agents utilisent `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback`, claude.ai, les connecteurs Claude Desktop, Claude mobile et Cowork utilisent `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, et ChatGPT utilise `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect` ou un `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>` par connexion. Si vous activez l\'enregistrement dynamique des clients dans Casdoor, vous n\'avez pas besoin de saisir ces valeurs : les clients s\'enregistrent eux-m\xeames ou fournissent \xe0 Casdoor un document de m\xe9tadonn\xe9es d\'ID client. Vous les ajoutez aux **Redirect URLs** uniquement lorsque vous cr\xe9ez manuellement une application Casdoor pour un client.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## URIs de redirection par client {#redirect-uris-by-client}\\n\\n| Client | URI de redirection | Comment il obtient un ID client de Casdoor | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Cursor desktop (actuel) | `http://localhost:8787/callback` | DCR, ou `auth.CLIENT_ID` dans `mcp.json` | Port fixe 8787 |\\n| Cursor web et Cloud Agents | `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, ou `auth.CLIENT_ID` | Avec DCR, l\'application desktop enregistre cette URI et celle de localhost |\\n| Cursor desktop (versions anciennes) | `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, ou `auth.CLIENT_ID` | Cursor a rendu l\'URI loopback par d\xe9faut en juillet 2026 |\\n| claude.ai, connecteurs Claude Desktop, Claude mobile, Cowork | `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback` | CIMD, puis DCR, ou un ID client saisi dans la fen\xeatre de dialogue du connecteur personnalis\xe9 | Les serveurs d\'Anthropic effectuent les requ\xeates |\\n| Claude Code | `http://localhost:<port>/callback` et `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback` | CIMD | Le port change \xe0 chaque session |\\n| Claude Desktop via `mcp-remote` | `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback` | DCR | Le port est d\xe9riv\xe9 de l\'URL du serveur |\\n| ChatGPT | `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect`, ou `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>` | CIMD, DCR, ou un client pr\xe9d\xe9fini | Copiez la valeur exacte depuis ChatGPT |\\n\\nCes valeurs proviennent de la documentation des fournisseurs au 2 novembre 2026 (voir [R\xe9f\xe9rences](#references)). Les clients peuvent les modifier. En cas d\'\xe9chec de connexion, lisez le param\xe8tre `redirect_uri` de la demande d\'autorisation dans la barre d\'adresse du navigateur et comparez-le avec ce que Casdoor poss\xe8de.\\n\\nLe tableau s\'applique \xe0 deux configurations : le [serveur MCP int\xe9gr\xe9 \xe0 Casdoor](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/overview) sur `/api/mcp`, et votre propre serveur MCP qui utilise [Casdoor comme serveur d\'autorisation](/docs/mcp-auth/overview). Dans les deux cas, le client d\xe9couvre Casdoor via les m\xe9tadonn\xe9es de ressource prot\xe9g\xe9e ([RFC 9728](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9728)) et obtient son ID client aupr\xe8s de Casdoor.\\n\\n## Option 1 : laisser les clients s\'enregistrer eux-m\xeames {#option-1-let-clients-register-themselves}\\n\\nCasdoor prend en charge l\'enregistrement dynamique des clients ([RFC 7591](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7591)) sur `/api/oauth/register`, ainsi que les documents de m\xe9tadonn\xe9es d\'ID client (CIMD), o\xf9 le `client_id` est une URL HTTPS vers un document JSON listant les URI de redirection du client. Un seul interrupteur contr\xf4le les deux : **Enable dynamic client registration** au niveau de l\'organisation. Il est d\xe9sactiv\xe9 par d\xe9faut pour toutes les organisations, y compris `built-in`.\\n\\nAvec cet interrupteur activ\xe9 pour `built-in`, le document de d\xe9couverte affiche ce que les clients recherchent :\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -s https://your-casdoor.com/.well-known/openid-configuration | jq \'{\\n  registration_endpoint,\\n  client_id_metadata_document_supported,\\n  code_challenge_methods_supported,\\n  token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported,\\n  authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\n}\'\\n```\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"registration_endpoint\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/oauth/register\\",\\n  \\"client_id_metadata_document_supported\\": true,\\n  \\"code_challenge_methods_supported\\": [\\"S256\\"],\\n  \\"token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported\\": [\\"client_secret_basic\\", \\"client_secret_post\\", \\"private_key_jwt\\", \\"none\\"],\\n  \\"authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\": true\\n}\\n```\\n\\nChaque client choisit ensuite sa m\xe9thode :\\n\\n- **Claude** utilise CIMD uniquement si les m\xe9tadonn\xe9es contiennent \xe0 la fois `client_id_metadata_document_supported: true` et `none` dans `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported`. Casdoor propose les deux d\xe8s que l\'interrupteur est activ\xe9, donc Claude s\'identifie avec une URL de document et Casdoor cr\xe9e une application nomm\xe9e `cimd_...`. Sinon, Claude revient \xe0 DCR.\\n- **ChatGPT** pr\xe9f\xe8re CIMD, avec le `client_id` `https://chatgpt.com/oauth/client.json` (ou une variante sp\xe9cifique au callback), et utilise DCR lorsque CIMD n\'est pas disponible.\\n- **Cursor** s\'enregistre via DCR en tant que client public sans secret. La documentation de Cursor ne mentionne pas CIMD.\\n- **`mcp-remote`** s\'enregistre via DCR.\\n\\nLes URI de redirection proviennent de la demande d\'enregistrement ou du document de m\xe9tadonn\xe9es, il n\'y a donc rien \xe0 saisir. Les clients enregistr\xe9s fonctionnent sous un r\xf4le restreint et leurs jetons ne peuvent pas appeler l\'API de gestion. Consultez [Enregistrement dynamique des clients](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration) pour conna\xeetre les r\xe8gles compl\xe8tes et l\'organisation dans laquelle atterrit le client.\\n\\n## Option 2 : cr\xe9er une application pour le client {#option-2-create-an-application-for-the-client}\\n\\nSi vous gardez l\'enregistrement d\xe9sactiv\xe9, cr\xe9ez une application par client afin de pouvoir les r\xe9voquer s\xe9par\xe9ment :\\n\\n1. Dans la console d\'administration Casdoor, allez dans **Identity** > **Applications** et ajoutez une application, par exemple `cursor-mcp`.\\n1. Ajoutez les URI de redirection du client issus du tableau dans **Redirect URLs**. Pour Cursor, ajoutez les trois, car diff\xe9rentes installations de Cursor envoient des URI diff\xe9rents.\\n1. D\xe9finissez **Category** sur `Agent`. Consultez [Configurer Casdoor pour votre serveur MCP](/docs/mcp-auth/setup) pour les scopes et les types d\'autorisation.\\n1. Fournissez le **Client ID** au client.\\n\\nUn secret client est optionnel pour ces clients. Casdoor accepte un \xe9change de code d\'autorisation sans secret lorsque la demande utilisait PKCE, et Cursor, Claude et ChatGPT utilisent tous PKCE avec `S256`. Si un client envoie tout de m\xeame un secret, il doit \xeatre correct.\\n\\nO\xf9 placer l\'ID client :\\n\\n- **Cursor** : un objet `auth` dans `~/.cursor/mcp.json` ou `.cursor/mcp.json`. Avec un `CLIENT_ID` statique, l\'application de bureau Cursor envoie uniquement son URI de redirection localhost.\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"url\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\",\\n        \\"auth\\": {\\n          \\"CLIENT_ID\\": \\"${env:CASDOOR_MCP_CLIENT_ID}\\",\\n          \\"scopes\\": [\\"application:read\\", \\"user:read\\"]\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\n- **Claude** : le champ d\'ID client OAuth dans la bo\xeete de dialogue du connecteur personnalis\xe9. Enregistrez `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`.\\n- **ChatGPT** : ChatGPT fonctionne \xe9galement avec des clients OAuth pr\xe9d\xe9finis. Copiez l\'URI de redirection depuis la page de gestion du serveur MCP dans ChatGPT, car la forme utilis\xe9e d\xe9pend du serveur d\'autorisation.\\n\\nGuides \xe9tape par \xe9tape : [Cursor](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-cursor), [Claude Desktop](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-claude-desktop), [ChatGPT](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-chatgpt).\\n\\n## Comment Casdoor fait correspondre les URI de redirection {#how-casdoor-matches-redirect-uris}\\n\\nCasdoor compare le `redirect_uri` d\'une demande avec chaque entr\xe9e dans **Redirect URLs** :\\n\\n- Le sch\xe9ma, l\'h\xf4te et le chemin doivent correspondre. La cha\xeene de requ\xeate n\'est pas compar\xe9e.\\n- Le port doit correspondre, sauf pour les h\xf4tes boucle locale (loopback). Lorsque les deux URI utilisent le m\xeame h\xf4te boucle locale (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1` ou `::1`), Casdoor ignore le port, conform\xe9ment \xe0 la [RFC 8252](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8252#section-7.3) pour les applications natives. `localhost` et `127.0.0.1` sont des h\xf4tes diff\xe9rents, donc Claude Code n\xe9cessite \xe0 la fois `http://localhost/callback` et `http://127.0.0.1/callback`.\\n- Un URI de redirection sur un sous-domaine d\'un h\xf4te enregistr\xe9 correspond \xe9galement, donc `https://example.com/cb` accepte `https://app.example.com/cb`. Enregistrez l\'h\xf4te le plus sp\xe9cifique possible.\\n- Les sch\xe9mas personnalis\xe9s tels que `cursor://` correspondent selon les m\xeames r\xe8gles. Les URI `javascript:`, `data:` et `vbscript:` sont toujours rejet\xe9s.\\n\\nComme le port boucle locale est ignor\xe9, un enregistrement `mcp-remote` ou Claude Code continue de fonctionner lorsque le port local change entre les sessions.\\n\\n## Emplacement de claude_desktop_config.json {#where-claude_desktop_configjson-lives}\\n\\nLa [documentation MCP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers) fournit ces chemins. Dans Claude Desktop, **Settings** > **Developer** > **Edit Config** ouvre le fichier et le cr\xe9e s\'il est manquant.\\n\\n| Operating system | Path                                                              |\\n|---|---|\\n| macOS            | `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` |\\n| Windows          | `%APPDATA%\\\\Claude\\\\claude_desktop_config.json`                   |\\n| Linux            | `~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`                     |\\n\\nClaude Desktop acc\xe8de \xe0 un serveur MCP distant de deux mani\xe8res, qui utilisent des URI de redirection diff\xe9rents :\\n\\n- **En tant que connecteur**, ajout\xe9 dans les param\xe8tres de connecteur de Claude. Les serveurs d\'Anthropic ex\xe9cutent le flux OAuth, l\'URI de redirection est `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, et Casdoor doit \xeatre accessible depuis Internet.\\n- **Via `claude_desktop_config.json`**, avec `mcp-remote` comme pont local. Le flux OAuth s\'ex\xe9cute sur votre ordinateur, et l\'URI de redirection est `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback` :\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"command\\": \\"npx\\",\\n        \\"args\\": [\\"mcp-remote\\", \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\"]\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\nQuitter compl\xe8tement Claude Desktop et le red\xe9marrer apr\xe8s avoir modifi\xe9 le fichier.\\n\\n## Les clients h\xe9berg\xe9s n\xe9cessitent un Casdoor public {#hosted-clients-need-a-public-casdoor}\\n\\nLes connecteurs ChatGPT et Claude appellent votre serveur MCP et Casdoor depuis les serveurs de leurs \xe9diteurs, et non depuis votre ordinateur. Casdoor doit \xeatre accessible via HTTPS public avec un certificat valide. Les requ\xeates d\'Anthropic proviennent de `160.79.104.0/21`, si vous filtrez par IP. Cursor desktop, Claude Code et `mcp-remote` ex\xe9cutent le flux localement, donc un Casdoor sur votre r\xe9seau fonctionne pour eux.\\n\\nDeux autres points v\xe9rifi\xe9s par les clients h\xe9berg\xe9s, que Casdoor g\xe8re d\xe9j\xe0 :\\n\\n- **\xc9metteur dans la r\xe9ponse** ([RFC 9207](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9207)) : ChatGPT rejette une r\xe9ponse d\'autorisation sans le champ `iss` correct, et utilise son URI de redirection stable uniquement avec les serveurs d\'autorisation qui respectent ses exigences RFC 9207. Casdoor annonce cette prise en charge dans ses m\xe9tadonn\xe9es et ajoute `iss` \xe0 la redirection.\\n- **Indicateurs de ressources** ([RFC 8707](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8707)) : ChatGPT envoie `resource` avec l\'URL du serveur MCP lors des demandes d\'autorisation et de jeton. Casdoor place cette valeur dans la revendication `aud` du jeton d\'acc\xe8s, et votre serveur MCP doit la v\xe9rifier. Consultez [Authentification MCP](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/authentication) pour le flux de d\xe9couverte.\\n\\nLectures connexes sur www.casdoor.com : [OAuth pour les agents IA](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-for-ai-agents) et [serveurs d\'autorisation pour MCP](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mcp-authorization-servers).\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- Cursor : [Model Context Protocol (MCP)](https://cursor.com/docs/mcp), section \\"Static OAuth for remote servers\\"\\n- Forum Cursor : [OAuth redirect URI changed from cursor:// to http://localhost](https://forum.cursor.com/t/oauth-redirect-uri-changed-from-cursor-to-http-localhost-for-streamable-http-mcp/165019)\\n- Anthropic : [Authentication for connectors](https://claude.com/docs/connectors/building/authentication)\\n- Model Context Protocol : [Connect to local MCP servers](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers)\\n- OpenAI : [Authenticate users](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/auth)\\n- `mcp-remote` : [README](https://github.com/geelen/mcp-remote)\\n- Casdoor : [Inscription dynamique des clients](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration), [Configurer Casdoor pour votre serveur MCP](/docs/mcp-auth/setup)"},{"id":"nginx-sso-authentication","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/nginx-sso-authentication","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO : Protection de toute application derri\xe8re Nginx avec Casdoor","description":"Ajoutez une connexion CAS, OIDC ou auth_request devant toute application derri\xe8re nginx avec Casdoor : comparaison entre forward auth, OAuth2 Proxy, un module nginx, les clients CAS et MFA.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"nginx","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/nginx"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAS","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/cas"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"MFA","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/mfa"},{"inline":true,"label":"forward auth","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/forward-auth"}],"readingTime":11.44,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"nginx-sso-authentication","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO : Protection de toute application derri\xe8re Nginx avec Casdoor","description":"Ajoutez une connexion CAS, OIDC ou auth_request devant toute application derri\xe8re nginx avec Casdoor : comparaison entre forward auth, OAuth2 Proxy, un module nginx, les clients CAS et MFA.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","nginx","CAS","SSO","OIDC","MFA","forward auth"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"URIs de redirection OAuth MCP pour Cursor, Claude et ChatGPT : Enregistrement des clients MCP dans Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"},"nextItem":{"title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","permalink":"/fr/blog/oidc-rp-initiated-logout"}},"content":"Nginx open-source ne dispose pas de module CAS, donc \xab authentification CAS pour nginx \xbb signifie en pratique l\'une des deux configurations suivantes. Soit nginx interroge un service d\'authentification pour chaque requ\xeate via son module `auth_request`, et Casdoor r\xe9pond (directement sur `/api/forward-auth`, ou via casdoor-forward-auth ou OAuth2 Proxy), soit l\'application derri\xe8re nginx est elle-m\xeame un client CAS et communique avec les endpoints du serveur CAS de Casdoor. Si vous pouvez recompiler nginx, le module nginx de Casdoor ex\xe9cute la connexion \xe0 l\'int\xe9rieur de nginx, et si vous pr\xe9f\xe9rez ne pas utiliser nginx, le proxy de site de Casdoor peut se placer devant l\'application. Dans tous les cas, la page de connexion est celle de Casdoor, de sorte qu\'une seule session SSO, un seul jeu d\'autorisations et une seule politique MFA couvrent toutes les applications.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Les options en un coup d\'\u0153il {#the-options-at-a-glance}\\n\\n| Option | O\xf9 s\'ex\xe9cute la logique de connexion | Ce dont nginx a besoin | Protocole vers Casdoor | Qui est autoris\xe9 | Choisissez-la quand |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Casdoor forward auth | Casdoor, `/api/forward-auth` | `auth_request` | Cookie de session Casdoor sur le domaine de l\'app | Autorisations de l\'application, v\xe9rifi\xe9es au moins une fois par minute | nginx est d\xe9j\xe0 en place et vous ne voulez aucun service suppl\xe9mentaire |\\n| casdoor-forward-auth | Un petit service Go | `auth_request` | Code d\'autorisation OIDC | R\xf4les ou groupes Casdoor, par emplacement | Un cookie de session partag\xe9 par plusieurs sous-domaines |\\n| OAuth2 Proxy | OAuth2 Proxy | `auth_request` | OIDC | `allowed_groups`, domaines d\'e-mail | Vous utilisez d\xe9j\xe0 OAuth2 Proxy |\\n| casdoor-nginx-module | nginx lui-m\xeame | Un module compil\xe9 | OIDC avec PKCE, jetons v\xe9rifi\xe9s localement | Tout utilisateur connect\xe9 de l\'app | Aucun processus suppl\xe9mentaire, plus des jetons bearer pour les API |\\n| Client CAS dans l\'app | L\'application | Rien, elle ne fait que proxifier | CAS 1.0, 2.0, 3.0 | Ce que l\'app fait avec les attributs | L\'app prend d\xe9j\xe0 en charge CAS |\\n| Proxy de site Casdoor | Casdoor sur les ports 80 et 443 | Pas de nginx | Session Casdoor | Autorisations de l\'application | Rien d\'autre n\'est plac\xe9 devant l\'app |\\n\\nLes trois premi\xe8res options diff\xe8rent uniquement par le service qui r\xe9pond \xe0 la sous-requ\xeate de nginx.\\n\\n## Pourquoi il n\'y a pas de \xab module nginx CAS \xbb {#why-there-is-no-nginx-cas-module}\\n\\nCAS est une conversation entre une application et le serveur CAS : l\'application redirige le navigateur vers le `/login` du serveur avec une URL `service`, re\xe7oit un ticket de service en retour sur cette URL, et valide le ticket par un appel de serveur \xe0 serveur. Apache httpd dispose du module [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas) d\'Apereo pour effectuer cela au niveau du serveur web, mais nginx ne propose aucun module CAS parmi ses modules officiels. Pour nginx, le composant standard est [`ngx_http_auth_request_module`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html) : avant de servir une requ\xeate, nginx envoie une sous-requ\xeate vers une URL choisie. Une r\xe9ponse 2xx autorise la requ\xeate, 401 ou 403 la refuse avec ce code, et tout autre code est une erreur.\\n\\nPour l\'utilisateur, une connexion CAS et une connexion OIDC semblent identiques : une redirection vers la page de connexion Casdoor puis retour. Ainsi, pour une application sans prise en charge CAS, la solution pratique consiste \xe0 utiliser OIDC au niveau de la couche nginx. Informations g\xe9n\xe9rales sur le protocole : [Qu\'est-ce que CAS](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-cas).\\n\\nLe module auth_request n\'est pas construit par d\xe9faut : ajoutez `--with-http_auth_request_module` lors de la compilation, ou v\xe9rifiez `nginx -V` sur une version packag\xe9e de nginx.\\n\\n## Option 1 : Forward auth de Casdoor {#option-1-casdoors-own-forward-auth}\\n\\nCasdoor peut r\xe9pondre directement \xe0 la sous-requ\xeate de nginx. Vous ajoutez un **Site** dans Casdoor dont le **Domain** correspond au domaine de l\'application et dont l\'**Casdoor app** est l\'application dans laquelle les utilisateurs se connectent, et vous ajoutez `https://app.example.com/caswaf-handler` aux **Redirect URLs** de cette application. Le host et le port peuvent rester vides, car le trafic ne passe pas par Casdoor.\\n\\nComme `auth_request` ne peut pas suivre les redirections, Casdoor r\xe9pond \xe0 une sous-requ\xeate non authentifi\xe9e par `401` et place l\'URL de connexion dans `Location`. nginx transforme cela en une r\xe9ponse 302 :\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    proxy_buffer_size 16k;\\n    proxy_buffers 8 16k;\\n\\n    location = /caswaf-handler {\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $http_host;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Uri $request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location = /casdoor-auth {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n        proxy_set_header X-Original-URL $scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Method $request_method;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /casdoor-auth;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_redirect $upstream_http_location;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_email $upstream_http_x_forwarded_email;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_groups $upstream_http_x_forwarded_groups;\\n        error_page 401 =302 $casdoor_redirect;\\n\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Email $casdoor_email;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Groups $casdoor_groups;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:3000;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nLe backend re\xe7oit l\'utilisateur dans `X-Forwarded-User`, `X-Forwarded-Email` et `X-Forwarded-Groups`. L\'acc\xe8s suit une [permission](/docs/permission/permission-configuration) sur l\'application, v\xe9rifi\xe9e au moins une fois par minute, afin qu\'un utilisateur d\xe9sactiv\xe9 perde rapidement l\'acc\xe8s. Les **Public paths** du site (webhooks, contr\xf4les de sant\xe9) passent sans connexion.\\n\\nConsultez [Utiliser votre propre reverse proxy](/docs/site/overview#using-your-own-reverse-proxy-forward-auth) pour la r\xe9f\xe9rence et [Ajouter une connexion \xe0 une application sans OIDC](/docs/site/protect-app) pour un guide pas \xe0 pas avec Grafana. L\'application ne doit \xeatre accessible que via nginx, sinon n\'importe qui peut envoyer l\'en-t\xeate `X-Forwarded-User` lui-m\xeame.\\n\\n## Option 2 : casdoor-forward-auth ou OAuth2 Proxy {#option-2-casdoor-forward-auth-or-oauth2-proxy}\\n\\nSi vous pr\xe9f\xe9rez ne pas cr\xe9er de site Casdoor pour chaque application, ex\xe9cutez un petit service qui effectue le flux OIDC contre Casdoor et r\xe9pond aux sous-requ\xeates.\\n\\n[casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth) est le service autonome de Casdoor con\xe7u pour cela (il a remplac\xe9 traefik-casdoor-auth). Il conserve l\'utilisateur dans un cookie sign\xe9, sans \xe9tat c\xf4t\xe9 serveur. Pour nginx, il expose `/verify`, qui r\xe9pond par un code 200 avec les en-t\xeates d\'identit\xe9 ou un code 401, et nginx redirige vers son `/login` en cas de 401 :\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    location = /_casdoor_verify {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor-forward-auth:9999/verify?groups=built-in/dev;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n    }\\n\\n    location @casdoor_login {\\n        return 302 https://auth.example.com/login?rd=$scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /_casdoor_verify;\\n        error_page 401 = @casdoor_login;\\n\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_roles $upstream_http_x_forwarded_roles;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Roles $casdoor_roles;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:8080;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nLes param\xe8tres de requ\xeate `roles` et `groups` sur l\'URL de v\xe9rification restreignent chaque application aux d\xe9tenteurs d\'un r\xf4le Casdoor ou aux membres d\'un groupe ; les utilisateurs connect\xe9s ne poss\xe9dant pas ces attributs re\xe7oivent un code 403. Les r\xf4les et groupes sont lus lors de la connexion et conserv\xe9s pour la session, donc les modifications prennent effet apr\xe8s une nouvelle connexion de l\'utilisateur. D\xe9finir `COOKIE_DOMAIN=example.com` permet \xe0 une seule connexion de couvrir tous les sous-domaines. Le [guide Traefik](/docs/integration/go/traefik) couvre ce m\xeame service pour Traefik.\\n\\n[OAuth2 Proxy](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx) fonctionne de la m\xeame mani\xe8re avec `provider = \\"oidc\\"` et `oidc_issuer_url` d\xe9fini sur votre URL Casdoor. Son point de terminaison `/oauth2/auth` renvoie 202 ou 401, les en-t\xeates utilisateur et email n\xe9cessitent `--set-xauthrequest`, et la gestion des en-t\xeates transf\xe9r\xe9s dans son exemple nginx n\xe9cessite `--reverse-proxy`. Deux param\xe8tres Casdoor sont importants : utilisez le format de jeton `JWT-Standard`, sinon les utilisateurs ayant une email non v\xe9rifi\xe9e seront rejet\xe9s, et conservez la port\xe9e `profile` afin que les groupes et r\xf4les soient renvoy\xe9s. Consultez [OAuth2 Proxy avec Casdoor](/docs/integration/go/oauth2-proxy) et le [guide de la version communautaire nginx](/docs/integration/C++/NginxCommunityVersion).\\n\\n## Option 3 : le module nginx de Casdoor {#option-3-the-casdoor-nginx-module}\\n\\n[casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module) g\xe8re la connexion directement dans nginx, sans processus s\xe9par\xe9. Les navigateurs sans session sont redirig\xe9s vers la page de connexion de Casdoor ; le module \xe9change le code avec PKCE, v\xe9rifie la signature du jeton d\'acc\xe8s ainsi que les champs `exp`, `aud` et le type par rapport au certificat de Casdoor, et conserve l\'utilisateur dans un cookie sign\xe9. Les clients API peuvent envoyer `Authorization: Bearer` avec un jeton d\'acc\xe8s Casdoor, v\xe9rifi\xe9 localement sans appel \xe0 Casdoor.\\n\\nIl n\xe9cessite nginx 1.22 ou plus r\xe9cent et OpenSSL 1.1.1 ou plus r\xe9cent. Vous pouvez le compiler en tant que module dynamique par rapport au nginx que vous ex\xe9cutez d\xe9j\xe0 :\\n\\n```bash\\n./configure --with-compat --with-http_ssl_module --add-dynamic-module=/path/to/casdoor-nginx-module\\nmake modules\\ncp objs/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so /etc/nginx/modules/\\n```\\n\\n```nginx\\nload_module modules/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so;\\n\\nevents {}\\n\\nhttp {\\n    server {\\n        listen 443 ssl;\\n        server_name app.example.com;\\n        ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/app.crt;\\n        ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/app.key;\\n\\n        casdoor_endpoint        https://door.example.com;\\n        casdoor_client_id       <client-id>;\\n        casdoor_client_secret   <client-secret>;\\n        casdoor_certificate     /etc/nginx/casdoor.pem;\\n        casdoor_redirect_uri    https://app.example.com/casdoor/callback;\\n        casdoor_token_location  /_casdoor/token;\\n\\n        location = /_casdoor/token {\\n            internal;\\n            proxy_pass https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token;\\n            proxy_ssl_server_name on;\\n        }\\n\\n        location = /casdoor/callback {\\n            casdoor_callback;\\n        }\\n\\n        location / {\\n            casdoor on;\\n            proxy_set_header X-User $casdoor_user;\\n            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nRemplacez les espaces r\xe9serv\xe9s par les valeurs de l\'application. La session n\'est pas rev\xe9rifi\xe9e aupr\xe8s de Casdoor avant son expiration, donc la d\xe9sactivation d\'un utilisateur prend effet lorsque la session se termine ; `casdoor_session_timeout` raccourcit cette fen\xeatre. Le module n\'effectue aucune v\xe9rification de r\xf4le ou de groupe. Sur NGINX Plus, consultez plut\xf4t [le guide NGINX Plus](/docs/integration/C++/Nginx).\\n\\n## Option 4 : l\'application est un client CAS {#option-4-the-app-is-a-cas-client}\\n\\nSi l\'application parle d\xe9j\xe0 CAS, comme une application Java avec le client Apereo CAS, nginx agit uniquement comme proxy et Casdoor est le serveur CAS. Chaque application Casdoor poss\xe8de des points de terminaison CAS sous `/cas/<organization>/<application>` : `/login` et `/logout`, `/validate` pour CAS 1.0, `/serviceValidate`, `/proxyValidate` et `/proxy` pour 2.0, `/p3/serviceValidate` et `/p3/proxyValidate` pour 3.0, et `/samlValidate` pour la validation SAML 1.1.\\n\\nL\'application envoie le navigateur vers `/login?service=...`, Casdoor redirige en retour avec `ticket=ST-...`, et l\'application valide ce ticket :\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl \\"https://door.example.com/cas/my-org/my-app/p3/serviceValidate?ticket=ST-1234567890&service=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example.com%2F\\"\\n```\\n\\nUne r\xe9ponse r\xe9ussie est un \xe9l\xe9ment `cas:serviceResponse` contenant `cas:user` et des attributs tels que l\'email, le nom affich\xe9 et `memberOf` ; ajoutez `format=json` pour obtenir du JSON. Voici les pi\xe8ges courants :\\n\\n- Chaque URL `service` doit \xeatre list\xe9e dans les **Redirect URLs** de l\'application, et le param\xe8tre `service` utilis\xe9 lors de la validation doit correspondre exactement \xe0 celui pour lequel le ticket a \xe9t\xe9 \xe9mis.\\n- Les tickets ne fonctionnent qu\'une seule fois et expirent apr\xe8s 5 minutes.\\n- Les tickets sont conserv\xe9s en m\xe9moire, donc avec plusieurs r\xe9pliques de Casdoor, la validation doit atteindre la r\xe9plique qui a \xe9mis le ticket (sessions collantes).\\n- Derri\xe8re nginx, transmettez `Host` et `X-Forwarded-Proto` afin que l\'application construise la m\xeame URL `service` que celle vue par le navigateur.\\n\\nLa configuration, y compris les filtres du client Java pour chaque version de CAS, est d\xe9crite dans [Connecter un client CAS](/docs/how-to-connect/cas).\\n\\n## Option 5 : laissez Casdoor agir comme reverse proxy {#option-5-let-casdoor-be-the-reverse-proxy}\\n\\nLorsque rien d\'autre ne se trouve devant l\'application, Casdoor peut la servir directement : son proxy de site \xe9coute sur `gatewayHttpPort` et `gatewayHttpsPort` (80 et 443 par d\xe9faut) d\xe8s qu\'un site existe. D\xe9finissez le champ **Host** du site sur le backend, choisissez un certificat, d\xe9finissez **Casdoor app**, et pointez le DNS vers Casdoor. La connexion, les permissions et les en-t\xeates se comportent comme dans l\'option 1. Consultez [Sites](/docs/site/overview).\\n\\n## Ajout de MFA {#adding-mfa}\\n\\nMFA ne n\xe9cessite aucun changement dans nginx ou dans l\'application. Casdoor demande le second facteur sur sa propre page de connexion avant d\'\xe9mettre un code d\'autorisation OAuth ou un ticket de service CAS, donc toutes les options ci-dessus en b\xe9n\xe9ficient. Les utilisateurs peuvent utiliser une application TOTP, SMS, email ou RADIUS, avec des codes de r\xe9cup\xe9ration, et les administrateurs peuvent exiger MFA pour une organisation via les [\xe9l\xe9ments MFA](/docs/organization/mfa-items). Cela r\xe9pond \xe9galement \xe0 \xab CAS 2FA \xbb : avec Casdoor comme serveur CAS, le second facteur fait partie de la connexion CAS.\\n\\nMFA est appliqu\xe9 \xe0 la connexion, pas par requ\xeate : une session d\xe9j\xe0 d\xe9tenue par nginx ou un service forward-auth dure jusqu\'\xe0 son expiration, et le champ **MFA remember time** de l\'organisation permet aux utilisateurs d\'ignorer le second facteur sur un appareil de confiance pendant un certain temps. Consultez le [guide MFA](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication) et [Qu\'est-ce que MFA](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-mfa).\\n\\nUne fois connect\xe9 \xe0 Casdoor, les utilisateurs acc\xe8dent \xe0 d\'autres applications qui les renvoient vers Casdoor sans nouvelle demande de mot de passe ; avec **Auto signin**, Casdoor ignore \xe9galement le s\xe9lecteur de compte. Consultez [single sign-on](/docs/session/single-sign-on) et [Single logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/single-logout).\\n\\n## Lequel choisir {#which-one-to-pick}\\n\\n- Pas de connexion dans l\'application et nginx d\xe9j\xe0 en place : forward auth de Casdoor.\\n- Une connexion unique sur de nombreux sous-domaines, ou des v\xe9rifications de r\xf4le par emplacement : casdoor-forward-auth (ou OAuth2 Proxy si vous l\'utilisez d\xe9j\xe0).\\n- Vous configurez nginx vous-m\xeame et prot\xe9gez \xe9galement les API avec des jetons bearer : casdoor-nginx-module.\\n- L\'application prend en charge CAS : les endpoints CAS de Casdoor, nginx comme proxy simple.\\n- Pas encore de proxy : le site proxy de Casdoor.\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- nginx : [ngx_http_auth_request_module](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html)\\n- OAuth2 Proxy : [int\xe9gration nginx](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx)\\n- Apereo : [Sp\xe9cification du protocole CAS](https://apereo.github.io/cas/7.1.x/protocol/CAS-Protocol-Specification.html), [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas)\\n- Casdoor : [casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth), [casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module)\\n- Docs Casdoor : [Sites](/docs/site/overview), [Connecter un client CAS](/docs/how-to-connect/cas), [MFA](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication)"},{"id":"oidc-rp-initiated-logout","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/oidc-rp-initiated-logout","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-oidc-rp-initiated-logout.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-oidc-rp-initiated-logout.mdx","title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","description":"How OIDC RP-initiated logout, back-channel logout and token revocation work in Casdoor, what each one clears, and how single sign-out spans several apps.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Logout","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/logout"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Session","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/session"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/o-auth"}],"readingTime":10.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"oidc-rp-initiated-logout","title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","description":"How OIDC RP-initiated logout, back-channel logout and token revocation work in Casdoor, what each one clears, and how single sign-out spans several apps.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","OIDC","Logout","SSO","Session","OAuth"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO : Protection de toute application derri\xe8re Nginx avec Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"},"nextItem":{"title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","permalink":"/fr/blog/radius-mfa-vpn-wifi"}},"content":"Logging a user out of an OpenID Connect app takes more than deleting your own session cookie. The user is still signed in at the identity provider, so the next \\"Sign in\\" click goes straight through without a password, and the other apps that share that identity provider session stay signed in too. A proper logout has three parts: clear your app\'s local session, send the browser to the provider\'s `end_session_endpoint` (RP-initiated logout) so the provider session ends, and let the provider tell the other apps through back-channel logout. With Casdoor, the end-session endpoint is `/api/logout`, back-channel logout is configured per application, and refresh tokens can be revoked at `/api/login/oauth/revoke`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Logout mechanisms at a glance\\n\\n| Mechanism | Who triggers it | What it clears | Casdoor support |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Local app logout | Your app | Your app\'s session cookie and stored tokens only | Your code |\\n| RP-initiated logout | Your app, by redirecting the browser | The Casdoor browser session and the token of the `id_token_hint` | Yes, `GET` or `POST /api/logout`, announced as `end_session_endpoint` |\\n| Back-channel logout | Casdoor, server to server | The sessions of other apps, each app ends its own | Yes, per application **Backchannel logout URL** |\\n| Front-channel logout | The provider, through hidden iframes | The sessions of other apps, through the browser | No, Casdoor doesn\'t announce `frontchannel_logout_supported` |\\n| Token revocation (RFC 7009) | Your app, server or client | One access or refresh token, and the tokens that share its refresh token | Yes, `POST /api/login/oauth/revoke`, announced as `revocation_endpoint` |\\n| Casdoor SSO logout | Your app, with the user\'s token or cookie | All sessions and tokens of the user, or the current session only | Yes, `/api/sso-logout`, Casdoor-specific |\\n| SAML single logout | Casdoor or a SAML service provider | Sessions in SAML apps | Yes, signed `LogoutRequest` |\\n\\n## Local session vs. identity provider session\\n\\nThere are at least two sessions involved in every OIDC sign-in, and often three:\\n\\n1. **Your app\'s session.** Usually a cookie that maps to the user and the tokens you got from the token endpoint.\\n2. **The Casdoor session.** A cookie on the Casdoor domain. As long as it is valid, Casdoor signs the user in to any app of the organization without a password, after a one-click account picker or silently with automatic sign-in. That is what makes [single sign-on](/docs/session/single-sign-on) work.\\n3. **An upstream identity provider session**, when the user signed in to Casdoor through Google, GitHub, an enterprise IdP and so on.\\n\\nAuth0 describes the same three layers in its logout documentation (application, Auth0 and identity provider session layers), and the model applies to any OIDC provider.\\n\\n\\"Localized application session termination\\" ends only the first one: your app forgets the user, the Casdoor session lives on. That is what happens when you just clear a cookie. \\"Centralized single sign-out\\" ends the Casdoor session and tells every app of the user to end its own. Most users who click \\"Sign out\\" expect the second, especially on a shared computer.\\n\\nFor the third layer, Casdoor calls the logout URL of a Custom OAuth provider when the application signs users in through one and the provider has a logout URL set. It doesn\'t sign the user out of Google or GitHub.\\n\\n## RP-initiated logout with the end-session endpoint\\n\\n[OpenID Connect RP-Initiated Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-rpinitiated-1_0.html) defines how a relying party (your app) asks the provider to end its session. The provider publishes the endpoint as `end_session_endpoint` in its discovery document, and the request carries these parameters:\\n\\n- `id_token_hint`: the ID token the provider issued to your app. The spec marks it RECOMMENDED. Casdoor uses it to find and expire that exact token.\\n- `post_logout_redirect_uri`: where the browser goes afterwards. The spec requires it to be registered in advance. Casdoor checks it against the application\'s **Redirect URLs** and refuses to redirect to anything else.\\n- `state`: an opaque value that Casdoor appends to the redirect, so your app can tell its own logout round trip from a forged one.\\n- `client_id`: identifies the app when you can\'t send an ID token.\\n\\nThe spec also defines `logout_hint` and `ui_locales`. Casdoor doesn\'t read them.\\n\\nCasdoor\'s discovery document at `/.well-known/openid-configuration` announces `end_session_endpoint` as `https://<your-casdoor>/api/logout`, so OIDC libraries that support RP-initiated logout pick it up without configuration. The endpoint accepts `GET` and `POST`, as the spec requires.\\n\\nWhat Casdoor does with the request, briefly (the [RP-initiated logout reference](/docs/session/rp-initiated-logout) has every branch):\\n\\n- With `id_token_hint`, Casdoor looks up the token, sends back-channel logout tokens to the user\'s applications, expires the token (its refresh token stops working too), clears the browser\'s Casdoor session and redirects to `post_logout_redirect_uri` with `state`.\\n- Without `id_token_hint`, Casdoor signs out the current browser session and expires the tokens issued in it. If the browser has no session left, it still redirects when it knows the app, from the session or from `client_id`.\\n- Without `post_logout_redirect_uri`, the endpoint returns a JSON response instead of a redirect.\\n\\n### Example: sign-out button in a browser app\\n\\nA single-page app that signed in with the authorization code flow and PKCE keeps the ID token after the token exchange. The sign-out handler clears local state first, then hands the browser to Casdoor:\\n\\n```js\\nconst ISSUER = \\"https://door.example.com\\";\\nconst CLIENT_ID = \\"your-client-id\\";\\n\\nasync function signOut() {\\n  const idToken = sessionStorage.getItem(\\"id_token\\");\\n\\n  const discovery = await fetch(`${ISSUER}/.well-known/openid-configuration`)\\n    .then((r) => r.json());\\n\\n  // 1. Forget the user locally, whatever happens next\\n  sessionStorage.clear();\\n\\n  // 2. Remember a state value to check when Casdoor sends the browser back\\n  const state = crypto.randomUUID();\\n  sessionStorage.setItem(\\"logout_state\\", state);\\n\\n  // 3. End the Casdoor session\\n  const url = new URL(discovery.end_session_endpoint);\\n  if (idToken) {\\n    url.searchParams.set(\\"id_token_hint\\", idToken);\\n  } else {\\n    url.searchParams.set(\\"client_id\\", CLIENT_ID);\\n  }\\n  url.searchParams.set(\\"post_logout_redirect_uri\\", \\"https://app.example.com/signed-out\\");\\n  url.searchParams.set(\\"state\\", state);\\n  window.location.assign(url.toString());\\n}\\n\\n// On https://app.example.com/signed-out\\nconst returned = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get(\\"state\\");\\nif (returned !== sessionStorage.getItem(\\"logout_state\\")) {\\n  console.warn(\\"Unexpected logout redirect\\");\\n}\\nsessionStorage.removeItem(\\"logout_state\\");\\n```\\n\\nAdd `https://app.example.com/signed-out` to the application\'s **Redirect URLs** in Casdoor, or Casdoor returns an error instead of redirecting. A server-rendered app does the same from its logout route: destroy the server session, then answer with a 302 to the end-session URL. For Spring Security, see the [Spring Boot SSO post](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc).\\n\\n## Back-channel logout: telling the other apps\\n\\nEnding the Casdoor session stops new silent sign-ins, but app B still has its own session cookie from an hour ago. Something has to tell app B. [OpenID Connect Back-Channel Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-backchannel-1_0.html) does this server to server: the provider sends a `POST` with a form parameter `logout_token`, a signed JWT that names the user, to each app\'s registered back-channel URI.\\n\\nIn Casdoor, set **Backchannel logout URL** on the application\'s edit page. When a user signs out through `/api/logout`, Casdoor sends a logout token to every application that holds an active token for that user and has this URL set. The token is signed with the application\'s certificate, the same key as its ID tokens, and contains:\\n\\n- `iss` and `aud`: the same issuer and client ID as the app\'s ID tokens\\n- `sub`: the user\'s ID, the same value as `sub` in the ID token\\n- `iat` and `jti`\\n- `events` with the member `http://schemas.openid.net/event/backchannel-logout`\\n- `sid` only for some logouts, such as [SSO logout](/docs/session/single-sign-out), and not for `/api/logout`\\n\\nCasdoor\'s ID tokens don\'t carry a `sid` claim, so key your local sessions by `sub` and end all of them for that user. Casdoor\'s logout token also has no `exp` claim, which the final spec lists as required, so check freshness with `iat` and don\'t make `exp` mandatory in your validator. Casdoor sends each token once, with a 30-second timeout, and doesn\'t retry. For applications outside the `built-in` organization, it refuses to call loopback and link-local addresses, so `http://localhost` won\'t receive logout tokens during development.\\n\\n### Example: a back-channel logout receiver\\n\\nThis Express handler follows the validation steps of the spec, using the [`jose`](https://github.com/panva/jose) library:\\n\\n```js\\nimport express from \\"express\\";\\nimport { createRemoteJWKSet, jwtVerify } from \\"jose\\";\\n\\n// Take both values from the discovery document of your Casdoor\\nconst ISSUER = \\"https://door.example.com\\";\\nconst JWKS = createRemoteJWKSet(new URL(`${ISSUER}/.well-known/jwks`));\\nconst CLIENT_ID = process.env.CASDOOR_CLIENT_ID;\\nconst EVENT = \\"http://schemas.openid.net/event/backchannel-logout\\";\\n\\nconst seenJti = new Map();\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.post(\\n  \\"/oidc/backchannel-logout\\",\\n  express.urlencoded({ extended: false }),\\n  async (req, res) => {\\n    res.set(\\"Cache-Control\\", \\"no-store\\");\\n    try {\\n      const { payload } = await jwtVerify(req.body.logout_token, JWKS, {\\n        issuer: ISSUER,\\n        audience: CLIENT_ID,\\n        maxTokenAge: \\"2m\\",\\n      });\\n\\n      if (!payload.events || typeof payload.events[EVENT] !== \\"object\\") {\\n        throw new Error(\\"not a logout token\\");\\n      }\\n      if (!payload.sub && !payload.sid) {\\n        throw new Error(\\"no sub or sid\\");\\n      }\\n      if (\\"nonce\\" in payload) {\\n        throw new Error(\\"nonce is not allowed\\");\\n      }\\n      if (seenJti.has(payload.jti)) {\\n        throw new Error(\\"replayed token\\");\\n      }\\n      seenJti.set(payload.jti, Date.now());\\n\\n      // Your own helper: delete every session row that belongs to this user\\n      await destroySessionsForUser(payload.sub);\\n      res.sendStatus(200);\\n    } catch (err) {\\n      res.status(400).json({ error: \\"invalid_request\\", error_description: err.message });\\n    }\\n  }\\n);\\n```\\n\\n`jwtVerify` checks the signature, `iss`, `aud`, and with `maxTokenAge` the `iat` claim. To find sessions by user, store `sub` from the ID token in each session record at sign-in. Prune `seenJti` periodically, or keep it in Redis.\\n\\n## Revoking refresh tokens\\n\\nA refresh token outlives the session that created it. If your app keeps one, a logout that never touches it leaves a credential that can mint new access tokens for days.\\n\\nThe end-session call with `id_token_hint` already expires that token, refresh token included. Revoke explicitly when the logout doesn\'t go through the browser, for example in a CLI, a desktop or mobile app, or when the user leaves only your app and keeps the Casdoor session. Casdoor implements [RFC 7009](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7009) at the `revocation_endpoint` of its discovery document:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -X POST https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/revoke \\\\\\n  -u \\"$CLIENT_ID:$CLIENT_SECRET\\" \\\\\\n  -d token=\\"$REFRESH_TOKEN\\" \\\\\\n  -d token_type_hint=refresh_token\\n```\\n\\nA public client that used PKCE can send `client_id` in the body without a secret. Revoking a refresh token also expires the access tokens issued with it. After that, the refresh endpoint answers `invalid_grant` and introspection returns `\\"active\\": false`; [When Casdoor revokes tokens](/docs/token/overview#when-casdoor-revokes-tokens) lists every event that does this.\\n\\nOne limit applies to every provider that issues JWT access tokens: an API that only checks the signature and `exp` keeps accepting a revoked access token until it expires. Keep access tokens short-lived, or have the API call [token introspection](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth#how-to-verify-access-token) when it must notice a logout at once.\\n\\n## Single sign-out across several apps\\n\\nWith the pieces above, single sign-out works like this when the user clicks \\"Sign out\\" in app A:\\n\\n1. App A clears its session and redirects to `/api/logout` with `id_token_hint`.\\n2. Casdoor sends logout tokens to the back-channel URLs of the user\'s other apps, expires the token, and ends the browser\'s Casdoor session.\\n3. Apps B and C receive the logout token and delete their sessions for that `sub`.\\n4. The browser lands on app A\'s signed-out page. Opening app B now shows the Casdoor sign-in page.\\n\\nAn app without a back-channel URL stays signed in until its own session or access token expires. If you can\'t add a receiver to an app, keep its sessions short or make it validate the token on each request.\\n\\nFor \\"sign me out everywhere\\" after a lost laptop, use Casdoor\'s [SSO logout endpoint](/docs/session/single-sign-out) with `logoutAll=true`: it expires every token and deletes every session of the user, and also sends signed notifications through notification providers to apps that don\'t speak OIDC. Administrators can end single sessions on the [Sessions page](/docs/session/management). For SAML apps, see [SAML single logout](/docs/how-to-connect/saml/overview#single-logout). Background reading: [single logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/single-logout) and [SAML single logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/saml-single-logout) on www.casdoor.com.\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- [OpenID Connect RP-Initiated Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-rpinitiated-1_0.html)\\n- [OpenID Connect Back-Channel Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-backchannel-1_0.html)\\n- [OpenID Connect Front-Channel Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-frontchannel-1_0.html)\\n- [RFC 7009: OAuth 2.0 Token Revocation](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7009)\\n- [Auth0: Logout](https://auth0.com/docs/authenticate/login/logout)\\n- [Casdoor: RP-initiated logout](/docs/session/rp-initiated-logout)\\n- [Casdoor: Sign users out of all applications](/docs/session/single-sign-out)\\n- [Casdoor: Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client)"},{"id":"radius-mfa-vpn-wifi","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/radius-mfa-vpn-wifi","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-radius-mfa-vpn-wifi.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-radius-mfa-vpn-wifi.mdx","title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","description":"Add TOTP 2FA to VPN logins over RADIUS with Casdoor: why only PAP works, how Access-Challenge asks for the code, which VPN clients support it, and Wi-Fi limits.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"RADIUS","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/radius"},{"inline":true,"label":"MFA","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/mfa"},{"inline":true,"label":"2FA","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/2-fa"},{"inline":true,"label":"TOTP","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/totp"},{"inline":true,"label":"VPN","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/vpn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Wi-Fi","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/wi-fi"},{"inline":true,"label":"802.1X","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/802-1-x"}],"readingTime":10.04,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"radius-mfa-vpn-wifi","title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","description":"Add TOTP 2FA to VPN logins over RADIUS with Casdoor: why only PAP works, how Access-Challenge asks for the code, which VPN clients support it, and Wi-Fi limits.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","RADIUS","MFA","2FA","TOTP","VPN","Wi-Fi","802.1X"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","permalink":"/fr/blog/oidc-rp-initiated-logout"},"nextItem":{"title":"SSO Spring Boot avec OpenID Connect : Spring Security, Resource Server et Spring Cloud Gateway avec Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"}},"content":"To put multi-factor authentication in front of a VPN with RADIUS, the VPN gateway sends the username and password to the RADIUS server with PAP, the server checks the password and answers with an `Access-Challenge`, the client shows a prompt, and the user types the 2FA code from an authenticator app into a second request. Casdoor ships a RADIUS server that does exactly this for users who have TOTP set up, so a gateway that can show an `Access-Challenge` prompt (FortiGate SSL VPN is one whose vendor documents it) gets a password plus a 2FA code without any extra software. Wi-Fi with WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise is different: 802.1X runs over EAP, which has no place for a typed code, so Casdoor has a switch to skip the code for those clients.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nIf you want the background on RADIUS itself and how it compares with LDAP for network devices, read [RADIUS vs LDAP](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/radius-vs-ldap) first. This post is about the MFA part: what goes over the wire, which clients can carry a second factor, and how to turn it on in Casdoor.\\n\\n## Which clients can do RADIUS MFA with Casdoor\\n\\nThe table lists what each vendor documents. \\"Challenge support\\" means the client shows the `Access-Challenge` prompt to the user and sends the answer back. Where the vendor page we checked doesn\'t say, the table says so instead of guessing.\\n\\n| Client | RADIUS auth method to use with Casdoor | Access-Challenge support | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| FortiGate SSL VPN | PAP (set the method to PAP, or leave Default, which tries PAP first) | Yes, per Fortinet | Fortinet says SSL VPN forwards the challenge to the client and waits for the answer |\\n| FortiGate IPsec VPN (including FortiClient) | PAP | Not forwarded by default | Fortinet\'s fix requires MS-CHAP on the RADIUS server, which Casdoor doesn\'t speak; use SSL VPN or skip MFA |\\n| Cisco ASA (AnyConnect / Secure Client) | PAP, the ASA default for all connection types | Not stated in the ASA RADIUS configuration guide | Don\'t enable `password-management` on the tunnel group: it switches the ASA to MS-CHAPv2 |\\n| OpenVPN Access Server | PAP (it also offers CHAP and MS-CHAPv2) | Not stated on the RADIUS settings page | Accounting is a separate toggle, default port 1813 |\\n| pfSense (RADIUS auth server, used by its OpenVPN) | PAP (also MD5-CHAP, MS-CHAPv1, MS-CHAPv2) | Not stated in the Netgate docs | Netgate\'s OTP examples put the code inside the password field, which Casdoor doesn\'t accept |\\n| WireGuard | None | Not applicable | Peers authenticate with public keys only, so there is no login for RADIUS to check |\\n| Wi-Fi, WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise (802.1X) | EAP (PEAP, EAP-TTLS and so on) | No typed-code prompt | Casdoor doesn\'t implement EAP; see the Wi-Fi section |\\n\\nWhere a cell says \\"not stated\\", test with the real VPN client before you roll it out, and check that the prompt appears and the code goes through.\\n\\n## Why only PAP can carry the password Casdoor checks\\n\\nRADIUS has several ways to send a password, and they decide what the server is able to verify.\\n\\nWith **PAP**, the client puts the password into the `User-Password` attribute, hidden with an MD5 hash of the shared secret and the request authenticator (RFC 2865, section 5.2). The server reverses that with the same secret, gets the cleartext password, and can check it against a stored hash or an LDAP bind. Casdoor reads `User-Password` and runs the same password check as its sign-in page.\\n\\nWith **CHAP**, the client never sends the password. It sends a hash of the password and a random challenge. RFC 2865 says plainly that CHAP needs the user\'s password in cleartext on the server to recompute that hash. **MS-CHAPv2** has the same shape with an NT hash. An identity provider that stores passwords as one-way hashes (Casdoor\'s built-in organization uses bcrypt) can\'t verify either one. The Casdoor RADIUS server only reads `User-Password`, so a CHAP or MS-CHAPv2 request reaches it with no password and is rejected.\\n\\nBecause the PAP hiding is only MD5 with the shared secret, use a long random secret, keep RADIUS on a private network or a VPN between the gateway and Casdoor, and turn on the `Message-Authenticator` attribute on the client. Casdoor verifies `Message-Authenticator` when a request carries it and signs its reply in that case, which is the mitigation for Blast-RADIUS (CVE-2024-3596). The details are in the [RADIUS server docs](/docs/radius/overview#message-authenticator).\\n\\n## Access-Challenge vs appending the code to the password\\n\\nThere are two common ways to get a 2FA code through RADIUS.\\n\\n1. **Access-Challenge.** The server checks the password, then replies with `Access-Challenge`, a `State` attribute and a `Reply-Message`. The client shows the message, the user types the code, and the client sends a new `Access-Request` with the code in `User-Password` and the same `State`. RFC 2865 section 4.4 also says what happens on a client without challenge support: it MUST treat the `Access-Challenge` as an `Access-Reject`.\\n2. **Code appended to the password.** The user types `password123456` into the one password field, and the server splits off the last six digits. This works with any client, which is why products built for older gateways offer it.\\n\\nCasdoor implements only the first. Here is the flow in its RADIUS server (`radius/server.go`):\\n\\n- The first `Access-Request` must carry the correct password. A user with MFA turned on gets `Access-Challenge`, a random `State`, and the `Reply-Message` `please enter OTP`.\\n- The `State` is valid for 120 seconds and can be used once. It is tied to the organization and username of the first request.\\n- The second `Access-Request` must carry that `State` and the six-digit TOTP code from the user\'s authenticator app in `User-Password`. Casdoor checks the code against the user\'s TOTP secret and answers `Access-Accept` or `Access-Reject`.\\n- Wrong codes count toward the failed sign-in limit of the user\'s application (5 attempts and a 15-minute freeze by default), separately from wrong passwords.\\n\\nTwo consequences follow. A client that can only do \\"password plus code in one field\\" will fail, because Casdoor checks the whole string as the password. And only TOTP works over RADIUS: SMS and email codes need Casdoor to send a message after the password step, which the RADIUS server doesn\'t do. A user whose only MFA method is SMS or email gets the challenge and then a reject, so have RADIUS users set up an authenticator app.\\n\\n## Wi-Fi: 802.1X and WPA-Enterprise\\n\\nOn Wi-Fi with WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise, the access point doesn\'t collect a password itself. It relays an EAP conversation between the device (the supplicant) and the RADIUS server, and RADIUS carries the EAP packets in `EAP-Message` attributes. In that setup `Access-Challenge` is already in use: every round trip of the EAP exchange is an `Access-Request` and `Access-Challenge` pair (RFC 3579). RFC 3579 also says a message carrying `EAP-Message` must not include `Reply-Message`, so there is no field for \\"please enter OTP\\" and no dialog on the phone or laptop to type a code into.\\n\\nTwo practical points for Casdoor:\\n\\n- Casdoor\'s RADIUS server doesn\'t implement EAP. An access point can\'t talk to it directly for WPA-Enterprise. You need an EAP server in front that terminates the TLS tunnel and forwards a plain PAP request. FreeRADIUS can do this with EAP-TTLS and PAP as the inner method, proxying the inner request to another RADIUS server, and the devices must be set to TTLS with PAP inside. PEAP with MS-CHAPv2, the default on many devices, won\'t work against Casdoor for the reason in the PAP section.\\n- For those users, turn on **Skip MFA for RADIUS** on the organization. Casdoor then accepts users who have MFA after the password check alone. MFA on the web sign-in page is not affected.\\n\\n## Ports, shared secret and accounting\\n\\nRFC 2865 assigns UDP port 1812 to RADIUS authentication, and RFC 2866 assigns 1813 to accounting. Casdoor listens on one UDP port, `radiusServerPort`, and handles both `Access-Request` and `Accounting-Request` there. So point the gateway\'s accounting at the same port as authentication (1812 by default), not at 1813, or accounting packets will go nowhere. If Casdoor runs in a container, publish the port as UDP, for example `-p 1812:1812/udp`.\\n\\nThe shared secret is one value for all clients, `radiusSecret`. Every gateway that talks to Casdoor uses it.\\n\\nFor accounting, Casdoor records `Start`, `Interim-Update` and `Stop` messages in its database (table `radius_accounting`), with the session ID, session time, NAS address, framed IP and byte and packet counters. It only does that for organizations with RADIUS turned on.\\n\\n## Configurer Casdoor {#configure-casdoor}\\n\\n**1. Set the RADIUS options.** In `conf/app.conf`, or as environment variables with the same names:\\n\\n```ini\\nradiusServerPort = 1812\\nradiusDefaultOrganization = \\"my-company\\"\\nradiusSecret = \\"a-long-random-string-shared-with-the-gateway\\"\\n```\\n\\n`radiusDefaultOrganization` is the organization Casdoor uses when a request has no `Class` attribute. If your gateway can\'t add a `Class` attribute to its requests, this setting decides which organization\'s users sign in over RADIUS. All keys are listed in the [configuration reference](/docs/basic/configuration).\\n\\n**2. Turn on RADIUS for the organization.** On the organization edit page, turn on **Enable RADIUS**. It is off by default and only global administrators can change it. While it is off, Casdoor rejects every `Access-Request` for that organization and ignores its accounting.\\n\\n**3. Decide on MFA for RADIUS.** Leave **Skip MFA for RADIUS** off for VPN gateways that support `Access-Challenge`. Turn it on for an organization whose RADIUS clients can\'t answer a challenge, such as WPA-Enterprise Wi-Fi.\\n\\n**4. Get users onto TOTP.** Users set up an authenticator app from their profile page, as described in [multi-factor authentication](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication). To make it mandatory, add the TOTP item with the **Required** rule under the organization\'s [MFA items](/docs/organization/mfa-items). Note the order: a user who must still set up required MFA, or must change the password first, is rejected over RADIUS until they finish that on the Casdoor sign-in page.\\n\\n**5. Test from the command line.** FreeRADIUS\'s `radclient` sends PAP requests and prints the reply:\\n\\n```bash\\necho \'User-Name = \\"alice\\", User-Password = \\"her-password\\"\' \\\\\\n  | radclient -x casdoor.example.com:1812 auth a-long-random-string-shared-with-the-gateway\\n```\\n\\nFor a user with TOTP you should see `Access-Challenge` with a `State` and the `Reply-Message`. Send the code back with that `State` within 120 seconds:\\n\\n```bash\\necho \'User-Name = \\"alice\\", User-Password = \\"123456\\", State = 0x...\' \\\\\\n  | radclient -x casdoor.example.com:1812 auth a-long-random-string-shared-with-the-gateway\\n```\\n\\nPaste the `State` value from the first reply in place of `0x...`. An `Access-Accept` means the setup is right, and the gateway is next.\\n\\nIf you also want Casdoor to use an outside RADIUS server as a second factor for its own web sign-in, that is the opposite direction and is covered by the [RADIUS MFA provider](/docs/provider/mfa/radius).\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- [RFC 2865: Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS)](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2865)\\n- [RFC 2866: RADIUS Accounting](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2866)\\n- [RFC 3579: RADIUS Support for EAP](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3579)\\n- [Fortinet: Difference in RADIUS Access-Challenge handling between SSL VPN and IPsec VPN](https://community.fortinet.com/t5/FortiGate/Technical-Tip-Difference-in-RADIUS-Access-Challenge-for/ta-p/406069)\\n- [FortiOS 7.6.5: Configuring a RADIUS server](https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.6.5/administration-guide/759080/configuring-a-radius-server)\\n- [Cisco ASA 9.22: RADIUS servers for AAA](https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/security/asa/asa922/configuration/general/asa-922-general-config/aaa-radius.html)\\n- [OpenVPN Access Server: Authentication](https://openvpn.net/as-docs/authentication-78640/)\\n- [pfSense: RADIUS authentication servers](https://docs.netgate.com/pfsense/en/latest/usermanager/radius.html)\\n- [WireGuard](https://www.wireguard.com/)\\n- [FreeRADIUS `inner-tunnel` virtual server](https://github.com/FreeRADIUS/freeradius-server/blob/v3.2.x/raddb/sites-available/inner-tunnel)\\n- [Casdoor RADIUS server](/docs/radius/overview)"},{"id":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","title":"SSO Spring Boot avec OpenID Connect : Spring Security, Resource Server et Spring Cloud Gateway avec Casdoor","description":"Ajouter l\'authentification unique \xe0 Spring Boot avec OpenID Connect et Casdoor : applications web oauth2Login, serveurs de ressources JWT et Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, avec YAML.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Boot","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/spring-boot"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Security","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/spring-security"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Cloud Gateway","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/spring-cloud-gateway"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth 2.0","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"JWT","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/jwt"}],"readingTime":12.62,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","title":"SSO Spring Boot avec OpenID Connect : Spring Security, Resource Server et Spring Cloud Gateway avec Casdoor","description":"Ajouter l\'authentification unique \xe0 Spring Boot avec OpenID Connect et Casdoor : applications web oauth2Login, serveurs de ressources JWT et Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, avec YAML.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Spring Boot","Spring Security","Spring Cloud Gateway","OIDC","OAuth 2.0","SSO","JWT"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","permalink":"/fr/blog/radius-mfa-vpn-wifi"},"nextItem":{"title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/sso-for-apache-data-platforms"}},"content":"Pour ajouter l\'authentification unique (SSO) \xe0 Spring Boot, enregistrez chaque application en tant qu\'application dans un fournisseur OpenID Connect tel que Casdoor, pointez Spring Security vers l\'URL d\'\xe9metteur du fournisseur, et choisissez le module Spring correspondant \xe0 l\'application : `oauth2Login()` pour une application web rendue c\xf4t\xe9 serveur, `oauth2ResourceServer().jwt()` pour une API REST qui re\xe7oit des jetons bearer, et Spring Cloud Gateway avec le filtre `TokenRelay` lorsqu\'une passerelle authentifie les utilisateurs pour un ensemble de microservices. Les utilisateurs b\xe9n\xe9ficient du SSO car toutes les applications les redirigent vers le m\xeame Casdoor, qui m\xe9morise la session : la deuxi\xe8me application effectue une redirection, Casdoor conna\xeet d\xe9j\xe0 l\'utilisateur, et celui-ci revient directement sans formulaire de connexion. Cet article met en place ces trois configurations contre un seul Casdoor, chacune avec un fichier `application.yml` fonctionnel et un `SecurityFilterChain` personnalis\xe9.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Les trois configurations en un coup d\'\u0153il {#the-three-shapes-at-a-glance}\\n\\n| Configuration | D\xe9pendance Spring | Qui d\xe9tient la connexion | Endpoints Casdoor utilis\xe9s | Cha\xeene de filtres |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Application web | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | L\'application (session HTTP) | discovery, authorize, token, userinfo, JWKS, logout | `SecurityFilterChain` avec `oauth2Login()` |\\n| API REST | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server` | Personne, chaque requ\xeate porte un jeton bearer | JWKS (et discovery si vous utilisez `issuer-uri`) | `SecurityFilterChain` avec `oauth2ResourceServer()` |\\n| Passerelle + microservices | `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` + `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | La passerelle (session WebFlux) | M\xeame chose que pour l\'application web ; les services derri\xe8re utilisent JWKS | `SecurityWebFilterChain` avec `oauth2Login()`, `TokenRelay` sur les routes |\\n\\nHypoth\xe8ses de version : Spring Boot 3.5 avec Spring Security 6.5, Java 17, et Spring Cloud 2025.0 (Spring Cloud Gateway 4.3). Sur Spring Boot 4, les deux starters Security sont nomm\xe9s `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-client` et `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-resource-server`. Gateway 4.3 et 5.0 utilisent tous deux le pr\xe9fixe de propri\xe9t\xe9 `spring.cloud.gateway.server.webflux.*` utilis\xe9 ci-dessous ; le pr\xe9fixe plus ancien `spring.cloud.gateway.*` et l\'artefact `spring-cloud-starter-gateway` sont d\xe9pr\xe9ci\xe9s depuis Spring Cloud 2025.0.\\n\\n## Ce que Casdoor fournit \xe0 Spring {#what-casdoor-gives-spring}\\n\\nDans la console d\'administration Casdoor, cr\xe9ez une application (ou r\xe9utilisez-en une) et copiez son **Client ID** et son **Client secret**. Ajoutez tous les callbacks que les applications Spring utiliseront aux **Redirect URLs**. La valeur par d\xe9faut de Spring Security est `{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}`, donc avec l\'ID d\'enregistrement `casdoor` et une application sur le port 8080, l\'URL est `http://localhost:8080/login/oauth2/code/casdoor`. Si vous utilisez la d\xe9connexion OIDC, ajoutez \xe9galement la page o\xf9 les utilisateurs atterrissent ensuite, par exemple `http://localhost:8080/` ; Casdoor refuse un `post_logout_redirect_uri` qui ne figure pas dans la liste.\\n\\nCasdoor publie un document de d\xe9couverte standard \xe0 `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, donc Spring n\'a besoin que de l\'\xe9metteur. Voici les endpoints qu\'il annonce, tir\xe9s du code source de Casdoor :\\n\\n| Champ de m\xe9tadonn\xe9es | Chemin Casdoor |\\n|---|---|\\n| `issuer` | L\'origine du backend, e.g. `https://door.example.com` (sans barre oblique finale) |\\n| `authorization_endpoint` | `/login/oauth/authorize` |\\n| `token_endpoint` | `/api/login/oauth/access_token` |\\n| `userinfo_endpoint` | `/api/userinfo` |\\n| `jwks_uri` | `/.well-known/jwks` |\\n| `end_session_endpoint` | `/api/logout` |\\n\\nLes scopes pris en charge sont `openid`, `email`, `profile`, `address`, `phone` et `offline_access`, ainsi que tout scope personnalis\xe9 d\xe9fini sur l\'application. Les jetons sont sign\xe9s avec RS256 par d\xe9faut et portent le nom du certificat comme `kid`. Chaque application a \xe9galement son propre document de d\xe9couverte \xe0 `/.well-known/<application>/openid-configuration` ; voir [Connecter un client OIDC standard](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) pour les d\xe9tails et la liste compl\xe8te des [endpoints OAuth 2.0](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth).\\n\\nDeux \xe9l\xe9ments dans les jetons de Casdoor comptent pour Spring :\\n\\n- **L\'\xe9metteur doit correspondre exactement.** Spring compare l\'`issuer` dans le document de d\xe9couverte avec l\'`issuer-uri` que vous configurez, puis avec la revendication `iss` de chaque jeton. Casdoor d\xe9rive l\'\xe9metteur du param\xe8tre `origin` dans `app.conf`, ou de l\'en-t\xeate Host lorsque `origin` est vide. Si vos applications Spring acc\xe8dent \xe0 Casdoor via un nom d\'h\xf4te interne (par exemple `http://casdoor:8000` dans Docker) tandis que les navigateurs utilisent un nom public, d\xe9finissez `origin` sur l\'URL publique afin que chaque jeton obtienne le m\xeame `iss`. Voir [Configuration](/docs/basic/configuration).\\n- **Les r\xf4les se pr\xe9sentent sous deux formes.** `/api/userinfo` renvoie `roles` sous forme de liste de noms de r\xf4les lorsque le scope `profile` a \xe9t\xe9 accord\xe9. Le jeton d\'acc\xe8s dans le format de jeton `JWT` [par d\xe9faut](/docs/token/overview) contient `roles` sous forme de liste d\'objets de r\xf4le, chacun ayant un champ `name`. Le code ci-dessous g\xe8re les deux cas. Les jetons d\'acc\xe8s de Casdoor portent \xe9galement `scope` sous forme de cha\xeene s\xe9par\xe9e par des espaces, que Spring transforme en autorit\xe9s `SCOPE_...` par d\xe9faut.\\n\\nSi vous souhaitez des informations g\xe9n\xe9rales sur le protocole, consultez [Qu\'est-ce qu\'OpenID Connect](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-openid-connect) et [ID token vs access token](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/id-token-vs-access-token).\\n\\n## 1. Application web Spring Boot avec oauth2Login() {#1-spring-boot-web-app-with-oauth2login}\\n\\nAjoutez `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` et `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client`. Configurez ensuite le fournisseur via l\'\xe9metteur (issuer) :\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n```\\n\\nAu d\xe9marrage, Spring Boot r\xe9cup\xe8re `https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration` et remplit les URI d\'autorisation, de jeton, d\'informations utilisateur et JWKS. Spring valide ensuite la signature du ID token, l\'\xe9metteur, l\'audience et le nonce, appelle `/api/userinfo` car `profile` et `email` sont dans la port\xe9e (scope), et fusionne les claims. `preferred_username` est le nom d\'utilisateur Casdoor ; `name` dans les infos utilisateur est le nom affich\xe9, et le `sub` par d\xe9faut est l\'UUID de l\'utilisateur.\\n\\nSans configuration personnalis\xe9e, Spring Boot exige une connexion pour chaque URL. Le `SecurityFilterChain` personnalis\xe9 ci-dessous ouvre la page d\'accueil, mappe les r\xf4les Casdoor vers des autorit\xe9s `ROLE_`, et d\xe9connecte \xe0 la fois Casdoor et l\'application :\\n\\n```java title=\\"SecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class SecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http,\\n            ClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) throws Exception {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/public/**\\", \\"/error\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(login -> login\\n                .userInfoEndpoint(userInfo -> userInfo\\n                    .userAuthoritiesMapper(casdoorRoles())))\\n            .logout(logout -> logout\\n                .logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private GrantedAuthoritiesMapper casdoorRoles() {\\n        return authorities -> {\\n            Set<GrantedAuthority> mapped = new HashSet<>(authorities);\\n            for (GrantedAuthority authority : authorities) {\\n                if (authority instanceof OidcUserAuthority oidc && oidc.getUserInfo() != null) {\\n                    List<String> roles = oidc.getUserInfo().getClaimAsStringList(\\"roles\\");\\n                    if (roles != null) {\\n                        roles.forEach(role -> mapped.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + role)));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return mapped;\\n        };\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`hasRole(\\"admin\\")` correspond d\xe9sormais aux utilisateurs ayant le r\xf4le Casdoor `admin`. `OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler` lit `end_session_endpoint` depuis le document de d\xe9couverte et redirige vers `/api/logout` de Casdoor avec `id_token_hint` et `post_logout_redirect_uri`, ce qui termine \xe9galement la session Casdoor. Sans cela, `/logout` ne supprime que la session Spring et la prochaine connexion sera silencieuse.\\n\\nDans un contr\xf4leur, l\'utilisateur connect\xe9 est un `OidcUser` :\\n\\n```java\\n@GetMapping(\\"/me\\")\\nMap<String, Object> me(@AuthenticationPrincipal OidcUser user) {\\n    return Map.of(\\"name\\", user.getName(), \\"email\\", user.getEmail(),\\n            \\"authorities\\", user.getAuthorities().toString());\\n}\\n```\\n\\nLa page \xe9tape par \xe9tape plus ancienne pour cette configuration est [Spring Security OAuth](/docs/integration/java/spring-security/spring-security-oauth).\\n\\n## 2. API Spring Boot comme serveur de ressources OAuth2 {#2-spring-boot-api-as-an-oauth2-resource-server}\\n\\nUne API REST ne doit rediriger personne. Elle accepte `Authorization: Bearer <access token>`, v\xe9rifie la signature JWT contre le JWKS de Casdoor et contr\xf4le les claims. Ajoutez `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` et `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server` :\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 9000\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      resourceserver:\\n        jwt:\\n          issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n          jwk-set-uri: https://door.example.com/.well-known/jwks\\n          audiences: <client-id>\\n```\\n\\nAvec seulement ce fichier, Spring Boot construit un `JwtDecoder` qui v\xe9rifie la signature, l\'expiration, `iss` et `aud`. D\xe9finir `jwk-set-uri` \xe0 c\xf4t\xe9 de `issuer-uri` signifie que les cl\xe9s sont r\xe9cup\xe9r\xe9es directement au lieu de passer par la d\xe9couverte, et l\'\xe9metteur est toujours valid\xe9. L\'audience est l\'ID client de l\'application Casdoor qui a \xe9mis le jeton : si une application monopage ou la passerelle de la section 3 obtient le jeton, utilisez l\'ID client de cette application.\\n\\nIl y a une v\xe9rification sp\xe9cifique \xe0 Casdoor qui vaut la peine d\'\xeatre ajout\xe9e. Casdoor signe les ID tokens et les access tokens avec la m\xeame cl\xe9 et donne les deux l\'ID client comme audience, donc les validateurs par d\xe9faut accepteraient aussi un ID token envoy\xe9 comme bearer token. Chaque jeton Casdoor a un claim `tokenType` (`access-token`, `id-token` ou `refresh-token`), ce qui rend la v\xe9rification simple. D\xe9finir un bean `JwtDecoder` remplace celui de Boot, donc le bean r\xe9ajoute les v\xe9rifications de l\'\xe9metteur et de l\'audience :\\n\\n```java title=\\"ApiSecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class ApiSecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    JwtDecoder jwtDecoder(\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri}\\") String issuer,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.jwk-set-uri}\\") String jwkSetUri,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.audiences}\\") String clientId) {\\n        NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(jwkSetUri).build();\\n        decoder.setJwtValidator(new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>(\\n                JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer(issuer),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<List<String>>(JwtClaimNames.AUD,\\n                        aud -> aud != null && aud.contains(clientId)),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<String>(\\"tokenType\\", \\"access-token\\"::equals)));\\n        return decoder;\\n    }\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain apiFilterChain(HttpSecurity http, JwtDecoder jwtDecoder) throws Exception {\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/api/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(server -> server\\n                .jwt(jwt -> jwt\\n                    .decoder(jwtDecoder)\\n                    .jwtAuthenticationConverter(casdoorJwtConverter())))\\n            .sessionManagement(session -> session\\n                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))\\n            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable());\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private JwtAuthenticationConverter casdoorJwtConverter() {\\n        JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter scopes = new JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter();\\n        JwtAuthenticationConverter converter = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();\\n        converter.setPrincipalClaimName(\\"name\\");\\n        converter.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(jwt -> {\\n            Collection<GrantedAuthority> authorities = new ArrayList<>(scopes.convert(jwt));\\n            if (jwt.getClaims().get(\\"roles\\") instanceof Collection<?> roles) {\\n                for (Object role : roles) {\\n                    Object name = role instanceof Map<?, ?> map ? map.get(\\"name\\") : role;\\n                    if (name instanceof String roleName && !roleName.isEmpty()) {\\n                        authorities.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + roleName));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return authorities;\\n        });\\n        return converter;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nLe convertisseur conserve les autorit\xe9s `SCOPE_`, ajoute des autorit\xe9s `ROLE_` depuis le claim `roles` qu\'il contienne des noms ou des objets, et utilise le claim `name` (le nom d\'utilisateur Casdoor) comme principal. Si l\'application utilise le format de jeton `JWT-Standard`, utilisez `preferred_username` comme claim principal \xe0 la place. D\xe9sactiver CSRF et les sessions est correct ici car l\'API ne voit jamais de cookie navigateur ; ne copiez pas ces deux lignes dans l\'application web.\\n\\nPour essayer sans navigateur, obtenez un jeton machine avec le grant client credentials et appelez l\'API :\\n\\n```shell\\nTOKEN=$(curl -s -u \'<client-id>:<client-secret>\' \\\\\\n  -d grant_type=client_credentials \\\\\\n  https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token | jq -r .access_token)\\n\\ncurl -H \\"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\\" http://localhost:9000/api/hello\\n```\\n\\nUn jeton client credentials repr\xe9sente l\'application, pas un utilisateur, donc il n\'a pas de r\xf4les. Pour plus d\'informations sur quels claims v\xe9rifier et pourquoi, consultez [Validation JWT](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/jwt-validation) et [Port\xe9es OAuth](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-scopes).\\n\\n## 3. Spring Cloud Gateway avec TokenRelay {#3-spring-cloud-gateway-with-tokenrelay}\\n\\nAvec les microservices, une architecture courante consiste \xe0 laisser la passerelle g\xe9rer la connexion et \xe0 garder les services comme serveurs de ressources. Le navigateur ne communique qu\'avec la passerelle et d\xe9tient un cookie de session ; la passerelle stocke le jeton d\'acc\xe8s de l\'utilisateur et le filtre `TokenRelay` l\'ajoute en tant que `Authorization: Bearer ...` \xe0 chaque requ\xeate proxifi\xe9e. Les services de la section 2 le valident normalement.\\n\\nAjoutez `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` et `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client`, avec le BOM Spring Cloud 2025.0 :\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  application:\\n    name: gateway\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n  cloud:\\n    gateway:\\n      server:\\n        webflux:\\n          routes:\\n            - id: orders\\n              uri: http://localhost:9000\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/orders/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n            - id: users\\n              uri: http://localhost:9001\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/users/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n```\\n\\n`TokenRelay=` sans argument transmet le jeton de l\'utilisateur connect\xe9. La fabrique de filtres `TokenRelay` n\'est cr\xe9\xe9e que lorsque `spring.security.oauth2.client.*` est configur\xe9, et le magasin de clients autoris\xe9s par d\xe9faut est en m\xe9moire, donc une passerelle avec plusieurs r\xe9pliques n\xe9cessite un magasin partag\xe9 ou des sessions collantes (sticky sessions). Les services derri\xe8re elle d\xe9finissent `audiences` sur l\'ID client de la passerelle.\\n\\nSpring Cloud Gateway fonctionne sur WebFlux, donc sa configuration de s\xe9curit\xe9 est une `SecurityWebFilterChain` construite \xe0 partir de `ServerHttpSecurity`, et non la `SecurityFilterChain` servlet :\\n\\n```java title=\\"GatewaySecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebFluxSecurity\\npublic class GatewaySecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityWebFilterChain gatewayFilterChain(ServerHttpSecurity http,\\n            ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeExchange(exchanges -> exchanges\\n                .pathMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .anyExchange().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(Customizer.withDefaults())\\n            .logout(logout -> logout.logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nGardez les d\xe9cisions d\'autorisation dans les services, o\xf9 les r\xf4les sont dans le jeton ; la passerelle s\'assure simplement qu\'il y a un utilisateur connect\xe9. La protection CSRF reste activ\xe9e dans la passerelle car elle est bas\xe9e sur des cookies.\\n\\nLes pages existantes [Spring Cloud Gateway](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud-gateway) et [Spring Cloud](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud) montrent une conception diff\xe9rente, o\xf9 un `GlobalFilter` personnalis\xe9 v\xe9rifie un utilisateur Casdoor stock\xe9 dans la session de la passerelle.\\n\\n## Alternative : le starter Casdoor Spring Boot {#alternative-the-casdoor-spring-boot-starter}\\n\\nSi vous pr\xe9f\xe9rez ne pas \xe9crire le YAML, [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter) 2.x (Spring Boot 3 et 4, Java 17+) prend `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.client-id`, `casdoor.client-secret`, `casdoor.organization-name` et `casdoor.application-name`, et d\xe9finit les propri\xe9t\xe9s `spring.security.oauth2.*` ci-dessus selon le starter Security pr\xe9sent sur le classpath. Il enregistre le client sous le nom `casdoor`, mappe les r\xf4les Casdoor vers des autorit\xe9s `ROLE_` pour la connexion OAuth2 et le serveur de ressources servlet, et expose les services du [Casdoor Java SDK](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-java-sdk) en tant que beans. Ses propri\xe9t\xe9s ont la priorit\xe9 la plus basse, donc vous pouvez toujours les remplacer. Voir [Spring Boot](/docs/integration/java/spring-boot) pour la configuration.\\n\\n## Erreurs courantes {#common-errors}\\n\\n- **\\"The Issuer ... provided in the configuration metadata did not match the requested issuer\\"** : l\'`issuer-uri` diff\xe8re de celui rapport\xe9 par Casdoor. Supprimez toute barre oblique finale et d\xe9finissez `origin` dans Casdoor si les noms d\'h\xf4te sont diff\xe9rents.\\n- **`redirect_uri` rejet\xe9 par Casdoor** : le callback ne figure pas dans les **Redirect URLs** de l\'application. V\xe9rifiez le sch\xe9ma et le port, surtout derri\xe8re un reverse proxy.\\n- **\\"Missing attribute \'preferred_username\'\\"** : la port\xe9e `profile` n\'a pas \xe9t\xe9 demand\xe9e, ou les champs de token de l\'application omettent `Name`.\\n- **Erreur 401 avec une erreur `invalid_token` sur l\'API** : le token provient d\'une autre application (`aud` incorrect), il a expir\xe9, ou c\'est un ID token.\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- Document de d\xe9couverte et endpoints de Casdoor : [`object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go)\\n- Claims du token Casdoor : [`object/token_jwt.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/token_jwt.go)\\n- [R\xe9f\xe9rence Spring Boot 3.5 : Spring Security, client OAuth2 et serveur de ressources](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/3.5/reference/web/spring-security.html)\\n- [Spring Security : d\xe9connexion initi\xe9e par le client OIDC](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/login/logout.html)\\n- [Spring Security : JWT pour le serveur de ressources OAuth 2.0](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/resource-server/jwt.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway : factory TokenRelay GatewayFilter](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/gatewayfilter-factories/tokenrelay-factory.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway : starter `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux`](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/starter.html)\\n- [Notes de version Spring Cloud 2025.0](https://github.com/spring-cloud/spring-cloud-release/wiki/Spring-Cloud-2025.0-Release-Notes)\\n- [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter)"},{"id":"sso-for-apache-data-platforms","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/sso-for-apache-data-platforms","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-sso-for-apache-data-platforms.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-sso-for-apache-data-platforms.mdx","title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","description":"Which Apache data projects support Casdoor SSO, since which version and with which config keys: DolphinScheduler, IoTDB, ShenYu, Pulsar Manager, ElasticJob UI.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"DolphinScheduler","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/dolphin-scheduler"},{"inline":true,"label":"IoTDB","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/io-tdb"},{"inline":true,"label":"ShenYu","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/shen-yu"},{"inline":true,"label":"Pulsar","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/pulsar"},{"inline":true,"label":"ShardingSphere","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sharding-sphere"},{"inline":true,"label":"RuoYi","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/ruo-yi"}],"readingTime":8.91,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"sso-for-apache-data-platforms","title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","description":"Which Apache data projects support Casdoor SSO, since which version and with which config keys: DolphinScheduler, IoTDB, ShenYu, Pulsar Manager, ElasticJob UI.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","OIDC","DolphinScheduler","IoTDB","ShenYu","Pulsar","ShardingSphere","RuoYi"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"SSO Spring Boot avec OpenID Connect : Spring Security, Resource Server et Spring Cloud Gateway avec Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"},"nextItem":{"title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","permalink":"/fr/blog/steam-telegram-login"}},"content":"Several Apache data and middleware projects ship a Casdoor login of their own: Apache DolphinScheduler has an authentication type called `CASDOOR_SSO` (since 3.2.0) and a generic OIDC mode (since 3.4.0), Apache ShenYu has a Casdoor plugin (since 2.5.1), Apache Pulsar Manager has a \\"Log in with Casdoor\\" button (since 0.3.0), and Apache APISIX has the `authz-casdoor` plugin (since 2.14.0). Apache IoTDB is the exception: the database itself authenticates with its own users and passwords, and only the now archived IoTDB Web Workbench talks to Casdoor. The table below lists every project, the version, and where the settings live; the second half of the post sets up user authentication in IoTDB step by step.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Which Apache projects support Casdoor\\n\\nAll facts in this table come from each project\'s source tree and release tags, checked on 2026-11-02.\\n\\n| Project | How it connects to Casdoor | First release | Settings |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Apache DolphinScheduler | Built-in `CASDOOR_SSO` authentication type (Casdoor Java SDK, OAuth 2.0 code flow) | 3.2.0 | `security.authentication.type`, `casdoor.*` in `dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/resources/application.yaml` |\\n| Apache DolphinScheduler | Generic OIDC login (Spring Security) | 3.4.0 | `security.authentication.oidc.*` in the same file |\\n| Apache IoTDB Web Workbench | Casdoor login in the web console | no tagged release, repository archived | `casdoor.*` in `backend/src/main/resources/application.properties`, `VUE_APP_CASDOOR` in `frontend/.env` |\\n| Apache IoTDB (server) | Native users and roles; an `OpenIdAuthorizer` existed up to 2.0.8 and was removed | n/a | see the IoTDB section below |\\n| Apache ShenYu | Casdoor plugin on the gateway | 2.5.1 | plugin config `endpoint`, `client_id`, `client_secrect`, `certificate`, `organization-name`, `application-name` |\\n| Apache Pulsar Manager | \\"Log in with Casdoor\\" button next to the password form | 0.3.0 | `casdoor.*` in `src/main/resources/application.properties`; front-end config in `front-end/src/main.js` since 0.4.0 |\\n| Apache ShardingSphere ElasticJob UI | \\"Login with Casdoor\\" button | master only, after 3.0.2 | `casdoor.*` in the backend `application.properties` |\\n| Apache APISIX | `authz-casdoor` plugin, or `openid-connect` | 2.14.0 | `endpoint_addr`, `client_id`, `client_secret`, `callback_url` |\\n| RuoYi-Cloud (not an Apache project) | Code changes with the Casdoor Spring Boot SDK | n/a | your own login controller |\\n\\nEvery SDK-based integration in the table follows the same pattern. You create an application in Casdoor, copy its client ID, client secret and the public certificate of its token cert, and paste them into the project\'s config together with the organization and application names. The project redirects the browser to Casdoor, receives an authorization code, exchanges it for a JWT, and verifies the JWT with the certificate. The certificate is the one under **Certs** in the Casdoor admin console; see [certificates](/docs/cert/overview) and the **Token cert** field in the [application settings reference](/docs/application/terminology).\\n\\n## Apache DolphinScheduler\\n\\nDolphinScheduler gives you two ways in.\\n\\n**`CASDOOR_SSO`** has been there since 3.2.0. Set the type and fill the top-level `casdoor` block of `dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/resources/application.yaml`:\\n\\n```yaml\\nsecurity:\\n  authentication:\\n    type: CASDOOR_SSO\\n    casdoor:\\n      user:\\n        admin: \\"alice\\"   # this Casdoor user becomes a DolphinScheduler admin\\ncasdoor:\\n  endpoint: https://door.example.com\\n  client-id: <client id>\\n  client-secret: <client secret>\\n  certificate: |-\\n    -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\\n    ...\\n    -----END CERTIFICATE-----\\n  organization-name: my-org\\n  application-name: app-dolphinscheduler\\n  redirect-url: http://localhost:5173/login\\n```\\n\\n`redirect-url` is the DolphinScheduler UI login page, and it must also be in the **Redirect URLs** of the Casdoor application. On the first login, DolphinScheduler creates a local user with the Casdoor user name and email. The user named in `security.authentication.casdoor.user.admin` gets the admin type; everyone else becomes a general user. Our [DolphinScheduler guide](/docs/integration/java/dolphinscheduler) has the screenshots.\\n\\n**OIDC** arrived in 3.4.0. It is generic, so Casdoor works as an ordinary issuer. Spring Security\'s discovery requires the configured issuer to match the `issuer` in the discovery document, and Casdoor\'s issuer is the origin of its backend, so `issuer-uri` is just the Casdoor URL:\\n\\n```yaml\\napi:\\n  base-url: https://ds.example.com/dolphinscheduler\\n  ui-url: https://ds.example.com\\nsecurity:\\n  authentication:\\n    type: OIDC\\n    oidc:\\n      enable: true\\n      providers:\\n        casdoor:\\n          display-name: \\"Login with Casdoor\\"\\n          issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n          client-id: <client id>\\n          client-secret: <client secret>\\n          scope: openid, profile, email\\n          user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n          groups-claim: groups\\n      user:\\n        auto-create: true\\n        default-tenant-code: \\"default\\"\\n        default-queue: \\"default\\"\\n        admin-group-mapping:\\n          - my-org/ds-admins\\n```\\n\\nThe redirect URL to register in Casdoor is `https://ds.example.com/dolphinscheduler/login/oauth2/code/casdoor`, that is, `api.base-url` plus `/login/oauth2/code/` plus the provider key. With the `profile` scope, Casdoor\'s UserInfo endpoint returns `preferred_username` and `groups`. By default groups arrive as `my-org/ds-admins`. Set **Token group format** to `Name (group)` in the application if you would rather write `ds-admins` in `admin-group-mapping`. If `auto-create` is `false` (the upstream default), only users that already exist in DolphinScheduler can sign in. The [standard OIDC client guide](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) lists what the discovery document and UserInfo return.\\n\\n## Apache ShenYu\\n\\nThe Casdoor plugin has shipped with the ShenYu gateway since 2.5.1. You turn it on in the ShenYu admin console and fill in the plugin data. Two details come from the source rather than the docs. First, the secret key is spelled `client_secrect`. Second, the certificate field is a single line, so you write `\\\\n` where each line break goes. The plugin accepts a JWT from the `Authorization` header, or a `code` and `state` on the query string. It then passes the user downstream in the headers `name`, `id` and `organization`. Requests that carry neither are rejected with a token error, not redirected. On ShenYu\'s master branch (October 2026) the plugin moved out of the main repository into the ShenYu plugin store. The plugin name, fields and headers stayed the same, and you add the starter `shenyu-spring-boot-starter-plugin-casdoor` yourself. See [Using Casdoor in ShenYu](/docs/integration/java/shenyu).\\n\\n## Apache Pulsar Manager and ShardingSphere ElasticJob UI\\n\\nPulsar Manager added Casdoor in 0.3.0. The back end reads `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.clientId`, `casdoor.clientSecret`, `casdoor.certificate`, `casdoor.organizationName` and `casdoor.applicationName` from `src/main/resources/application.properties`. Since 0.4.0 the front end uses `casdoor-vue-sdk`, configured in `front-end/src/main.js` with `serverUrl`, `clientId`, `organizationName`, `appName` and `redirectPath`. A first-time Casdoor user gets a local Pulsar Manager account. See [Pulsar Manager](/docs/integration/java/Pulsar-manager).\\n\\nElasticJob UI, the console of Apache ShardingSphere ElasticJob, merged Casdoor login in November 2022. That was after its last release, 3.0.2, so you need to build master. The keys use dashes: `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.client-id`, `casdoor.client-secret`, `casdoor.certificate`, `casdoor.organization-name`, `casdoor.application-name`. See [ShardingSphere](/docs/integration/java/ShardingSphere).\\n\\nFor Apache APISIX, both plugins are covered in [APISIX](/docs/integration/lua/apisix).\\n\\n## Step by step user authentication setup in Apache IoTDB\\n\\nIoTDB authenticates every session (CLI, JDBC, Session API) with a user name and password stored by the database. Since there is no Casdoor login on the server side, the setup is IoTDB\'s native user model. The statements below are for the tree model. The table model of IoTDB 2.x has its own syntax in the [table model guide](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest-Table/User-Manual/Authority-Management_apache.html).\\n\\n**Step 1. Change the root password.** `root` is the only administrator and cannot be deleted. Log in with the CLI and set a strong password:\\n\\n```sql\\nALTER USER root SET PASSWORD \'Str0ng_Passw0rd!\';\\n```\\n\\n**Step 2. Create users.** User names and passwords are 4 to 32 characters long. They can contain letters, digits and `!@#$%^&*()_+-=`, and from V2.0.7 a password cannot equal the user name. TimechoDB, Timecho\'s commercial IoTDB distribution, requires 12 to 32 characters with upper and lower case, a digit and a symbol, and it has a different default root password. Change it the same way.\\n\\n```sql\\nCREATE USER ln_reader \'Read_Passw0rd!\';\\nCREATE USER ops_admin \'Ops_Passw0rd!\';\\n```\\n\\n**Step 3. Group privileges in roles.** New users have no privileges. Series privileges (`READ_DATA`, `WRITE_DATA`, `READ_SCHEMA`, `WRITE_SCHEMA`, or the aliases `READ`, `WRITE`, `ALL`) are granted on path patterns:\\n\\n```sql\\nCREATE ROLE ln_readers;\\nGRANT READ ON root.ln.** TO ROLE ln_readers;\\nGRANT ROLE ln_readers TO ln_reader;\\n```\\n\\n**Step 4. Delegate user management.** This is where versions differ. From V2.0.7, global privileges are `SYSTEM` and `SECURITY`. Before that, they were split into `MANAGE_USER`, `MANAGE_ROLE`, `MANAGE_DATABASE` and others. Global privileges are always granted on `root.**`:\\n\\n```sql\\n-- IoTDB V2.0.7 and later, TimechoDB\\nGRANT SECURITY ON root.** TO USER ops_admin;\\n-- before V2.0.7\\nGRANT MANAGE_USER, MANAGE_ROLE ON root.** TO USER ops_admin;\\n```\\n\\n**Step 5. Connect and verify.**\\n\\n```bash\\nsbin/start-cli.sh -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6667 -u ln_reader -pw \'Read_Passw0rd!\'\\n```\\n\\nThen run `LIST PRIVILEGES OF USER ln_reader;`. A user\'s effective privileges are the union of their own and those of their roles. To take a privilege away, revoke it from both the user and every role that grants it.\\n\\n### What about OIDC in IoTDB?\\n\\nOlder IoTDB versions had `authorizer_provider_class=org.apache.iotdb.commons.auth.authorizer.OpenIdAuthorizer` with `openID_url`. The client passed an access token as the user name with an empty password. That authorizer was built around Keycloak. It treats a user as an administrator only if the token\'s `realm_access.roles` contains `iotdb_admin`, and every privilege check reduces to that test. Its token parser also sets a near-infinite clock skew, which in practice ignores token expiry. Casdoor does not emit `realm_access`, so this was never a fit. IoTDB removed it in March 2026 ([apache/iotdb#17320](https://github.com/apache/iotdb/pull/17320)), and it is absent from 2.0.10 and 2.0.11.\\n\\nIf you want Casdoor accounts in front of IoTDB, the option that exists is the [IoTDB Web Workbench](/docs/integration/java/iotdb) integration. Its repository is archived on GitHub, so treat it as a starting point rather than a maintained product. The database connection itself still uses an IoTDB user from the steps above.\\n\\n## RuoYi\\n\\nRuoYi-Cloud has no Casdoor switch. Our [RuoYi guide](/docs/integration/java/RuoYi) replaces its password login with the Casdoor authorization code flow through the Casdoor Spring Boot SDK. It syncs the existing RuoYi users into Casdoor with a syncer, using bcrypt so their passwords keep working. For a plain Spring Boot app, start with [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc).\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- Apache DolphinScheduler, [authentication types](https://github.com/apache/dolphinscheduler/blob/dev/docs/docs/en/guide/security/authentication-type.md) and [`application.yaml`](https://github.com/apache/dolphinscheduler/blob/dev/dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/resources/application.yaml)\\n- Apache IoTDB, [Authority Management (from V2.0.7)](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest/User-Manual/Authority-Management-Upgrade_apache.html), [Authority Management (before V2.0.7)](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest/User-Manual/Authority-Management_apache.html), [CLI](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest/Tools-System/CLI_apache.html)\\n- Timecho, [TimechoDB Authority Management](https://www.timecho.com/docs/UserGuide/latest/User-Manual/Authority-Management-Upgrade_timecho.html)\\n- [apache/iotdb-web-workbench](https://github.com/apache/iotdb-web-workbench)\\n- Apache ShenYu, [Casdoor plugin](https://shenyu.apache.org/docs/plugin-center/security/casdoor/) and [plugin store cutover](https://github.com/apache/shenyu/blob/master/docs/plugin-store-casdoor.md)\\n- [apache/pulsar-manager](https://github.com/apache/pulsar-manager) and [apache/shardingsphere-elasticjob-ui](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere-elasticjob-ui)\\n- Apache APISIX, [authz-casdoor](https://apisix.apache.org/docs/apisix/plugins/authz-casdoor/)"},{"id":"steam-telegram-login","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/steam-telegram-login","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-steam-telegram-login.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-steam-telegram-login.mdx","title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","description":"Steam uses OpenID 2.0 and Telegram signs widget data with HMAC. Here is how Casdoor adds Steam, Telegram, X and Facebook login and hands your app plain OIDC.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Steam","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/steam"},{"inline":true,"label":"Telegram","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/telegram"},{"inline":true,"label":"Social Login","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/social-login"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":9.21,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"steam-telegram-login","title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","description":"Steam uses OpenID 2.0 and Telegram signs widget data with HMAC. Here is how Casdoor adds Steam, Telegram, X and Facebook login and hands your app plain OIDC.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","OAuth","OIDC","Steam","Telegram","Social Login","SSO"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/sso-for-apache-data-platforms"},"nextItem":{"title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","permalink":"/fr/blog/test-payments-without-a-gateway"}},"content":"There is no \\"Steam OAuth\\": Steam signs users in with OpenID 2.0 and gives you a 64-bit SteamID, nothing more. Telegram\'s classic Login Widget isn\'t OAuth either: it hands your page a set of user fields plus an HMAC-SHA256 hash that you check with your bot token. Telegram now also runs a real OpenID Connect provider at `oauth.telegram.org`. If you put Casdoor between your app and these providers, Casdoor does the protocol-specific checks and your app only ever speaks OpenID Connect to Casdoor, the same way it would for Google or GitHub.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The providers at a glance\\n\\n| Provider | Protocol | What identifies the user | Email available? | Casdoor provider type |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Steam | OpenID 2.0 at `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/login`, plus the Steam Web API for the profile | SteamID64, taken from the claimed ID `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/id/<steamid>` | No | `Steam` |\\n| Telegram (Login Widget) | Signed widget data, HMAC-SHA256 keyed with the SHA-256 of the bot token | Telegram user `id` | No | `Telegram` |\\n| Telegram (OpenID Connect) | OIDC authorization code flow with PKCE at `https://oauth.telegram.org` | `sub` claim of the ID token | No email claim; phone with the `phone` scope | `Custom` |\\n| X (Twitter) | OAuth 2.0 authorization code with PKCE | X user ID from `/2/users/me` | Not with Casdoor\'s default scopes | `Twitter` |\\n| Facebook | OAuth 2.0 authorization code | App-scoped user ID | Only if the profile has a valid primary email | `Facebook` |\\n\\nThe rest of this post goes through what each protocol actually returns and what Casdoor does with it.\\n\\n## Steam: OpenID 2.0, no OAuth\\n\\nSteam\'s developer pages describe Steam as an OpenID provider: your site sends the user to Steam, and Steam sends back a claimed ID of the form `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/id/<steamid>`. The number at the end is the user\'s 64-bit SteamID. There is no authorization code, no access token, no scopes, and no email: neither the OpenID response nor the Web API profile contains one.\\n\\nThat is why searches for \\"steam oauth\\" lead nowhere useful: the protocol is OpenID 2.0, the predecessor of OpenID Connect, so an OAuth 2.0 or OIDC client library can\'t talk to Steam directly.\\n\\nWhat Casdoor does for a Steam sign-in:\\n\\n1. The Casdoor sign-in page builds an OpenID 2.0 `checkid_setup` request to `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/login`, with `openid.realm` set to the Casdoor origin and `openid.return_to` set to Casdoor\'s `/callback` page.\\n2. When Steam redirects back, Casdoor posts the signed fields back to Steam with `openid.mode=check_authentication`. Only if Steam answers `is_valid:true` does it continue. It also checks that `openid.claimed_id` matches the `steamcommunity.com/openid/id/<digits>` pattern and extracts the SteamID from it.\\n3. With the Steam Web API key that you enter as the provider\'s **Client secret**, Casdoor calls `ISteamUser/GetPlayerSummaries` to get the persona name and the avatar.\\n\\nThe resulting Casdoor user has the SteamID64 as its username, the Steam persona name as its display name and the Steam avatar. The email field stays empty. Casdoor deliberately drops the placeholder text that the underlying library puts there. If your app needs an email address, collect it after sign-up.\\n\\nSetup takes two minutes: register a Web API key for your Casdoor domain on Steam\'s key page, create a provider of type `Steam` and paste the key as the client secret. The [Steam provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/Steam) has the steps.\\n\\n## Telegram: the Login Widget and its HMAC check\\n\\nThe classic Telegram login is a JavaScript widget. You create a bot with @BotFather, link your domain to it with `/setdomain`, and embed the widget. When the user confirms in Telegram, the widget either redirects to the URL in `data-auth-url` or calls a JavaScript function, passing `id`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `username`, `photo_url`, `auth_date` and `hash`.\\n\\nNothing in that payload is a token. Anyone can type those fields into a URL, so the server must check the `hash`. Telegram\'s documented check works like this:\\n\\n```text\\ndata_check_string = all received fields except hash,\\n                    as key=value, sorted by key, joined with \\"\\\\n\\"\\nsecret_key        = SHA256(bot_token)\\nvalid             = hex(HMAC_SHA256(data_check_string, secret_key)) == hash\\n```\\n\\nTelegram also tells you to check `auth_date` so that old data can\'t be replayed, but it leaves the window up to you.\\n\\nIn Casdoor, the `Telegram` provider type takes the bot username (without `@`) as the **Client ID** and the bot token as the **Client secret**. When a user clicks the Telegram button, Casdoor opens its own `/telegram-login` page, which loads `telegram-widget.js` from telegram.org with your bot username and points `data-auth-url` back at Casdoor\'s `/callback`. The callback collects the fields and the Casdoor backend then:\\n\\n- rebuilds the data-check string from every field except `hash`, sorts it, and compares the HMAC-SHA256 against `hash` using `SHA256(bot token)` as the key;\\n- rejects the data if `auth_date` is more than 24 hours old;\\n- uses the numeric Telegram `id` as the identity, the `username` as the username, the first and last name as the display name, and `photo_url` as the avatar.\\n\\nTelegram usernames are optional. When a user has none, Casdoor generates `telegram_<id>`, which stays the same at every sign-in. The widget never sends an email address or a phone number, so a Telegram-only user in Casdoor has neither. See the [Telegram provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/telegram) for the BotFather steps.\\n\\n## Telegram now offers OpenID Connect too\\n\\nTelegram\'s \\"Log In With Telegram\\" page now documents a standard OpenID Connect flow, and the old iframe widget documentation has moved to an archive page. The facts from Telegram\'s docs:\\n\\n- Discovery document: `https://oauth.telegram.org/.well-known/openid-configuration`. Authorization endpoint `https://oauth.telegram.org/auth`, token endpoint `https://oauth.telegram.org/token`, keys at `https://oauth.telegram.org/.well-known/jwks.json`.\\n- In @BotFather you open your bot\'s **Login Widget** settings, register the allowed URLs (origins and exact redirect URIs), and get a client ID and client secret.\\n- Authorization code flow, PKCE with `S256` recommended. The token request uses HTTP Basic authentication with the client ID and secret.\\n- Scopes: `openid` is required; `profile` adds the name, `preferred_username` and `picture`; `phone` adds `phone_number` with the user\'s consent. There is no email claim.\\n- There is no UserInfo endpoint. All user data is in the ID token, whose `iss` is `https://oauth.telegram.org` and whose `aud` is your bot ID.\\n\\nYou can connect this flow with a Casdoor [custom OAuth provider](/docs/provider/oauth/CustomProvider). Set the **Auth URL** and **Token URL** to the endpoints above, set **Scope** to `openid profile` (add `phone` if you need it), turn on **Enable PKCE**, and leave **UserInfo URL** empty. Register Casdoor\'s `https://<your-casdoor-host>/callback` as an allowed URL in BotFather. With no UserInfo URL, Casdoor takes the user from the ID token in the token response: it requires `sub`, rejects an expired token, and checks that `aud` contains the client ID. Without a user mapping, `sub` becomes the user ID, `preferred_username` the username, `name` the display name and `picture` the avatar, and `phone_number` fills the phone. The Casdoor sign-in page recognizes a custom provider whose auth URL points at `oauth.telegram.org` and keeps the OAuth `state` short for it.\\n\\nNote that a Telegram user signed in through the widget and the same person signed in through OIDC are not automatically the same Casdoor user. The widget identity is the numeric `id`, while the OIDC identity is `sub`, and Telegram\'s own examples show different values for the two. Pick one method per application.\\n\\n## X (Twitter) and Facebook\\n\\nThese two are ordinary OAuth 2.0, but each has a detail worth knowing.\\n\\n**X** requires a PKCE challenge on its OAuth 2.0 authorize URL. Casdoor\'s `Twitter` type generates a new code verifier for every sign-in, sends the `S256` challenge, and passes the verifier in the token exchange, so there is nothing to configure beyond the client ID and secret. Casdoor requests the `users.read tweet.read` scopes and reads the user from `/2/users/me`, which gives the user ID, the handle and the display name. It doesn\'t request an email field, so plan for X users without an email address. The [Twitter provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/Twitter) covers the app settings.\\n\\n**Facebook** returns an app-scoped user ID: the same person has a different ID in each Facebook app, so moving to a new Facebook app means your users won\'t match their old records unless you link them by email. Casdoor asks for `email,public_profile` and reads `id`, `name`, `picture` and `email` from the Graph API. Facebook omits `email` when the profile has no valid address. See the [Facebook provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/facebook).\\n\\n## How your app sees all of this: plain OIDC\\n\\nYour application never talks to Steam, Telegram, X or Facebook. It is an OIDC client of Casdoor, configured once with the Casdoor issuer, `https://<your-casdoor-host>/.well-known/openid-configuration`. You add the social providers to the Casdoor application on its **Providers** tab, and their buttons appear on the Casdoor sign-in page.\\n\\nAfter any of these sign-ins, Casdoor finds or creates a Casdoor user and finishes the normal authorization code flow with your app. The ID token your app receives is signed by Casdoor, and its `sub` is the Casdoor user ID, not the SteamID or the Telegram ID. So your app needs no OpenID 2.0 library, no HMAC code, no PKCE quirks per provider, and no special case for users without an email. The original provider ID is stored on the Casdoor user, for example in the `steam` or `telegram` field, if you ever need it.\\n\\nA few things to plan for:\\n\\n- **Account linking.** Casdoor links a social sign-in to an existing user by verified email, verified phone, or username, in the order the application\'s binding rule sets. Steam and widget-based Telegram provide neither email nor phone, so they create a separate user unless the user links the provider from an account that is already signed in. See [how Casdoor links accounts](/docs/provider/oauth/overview#automatic-account-linking).\\n- **Extra profile fields.** Providers that pass on raw data, such as the Goth-based Steam type and custom providers, can fill more user fields through [user mapping](/docs/provider/oauth/user-mapping).\\n- **The client side.** Any standard library works against Casdoor. [Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) lists the endpoints, and the [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc) post shows a complete app.\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- Steam Web API and OpenID: [steamcommunity.com/dev](https://steamcommunity.com/dev)\\n- Steamworks, User Authentication and Ownership: [partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/auth](https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/auth)\\n- Telegram, Log In With Telegram (OpenID Connect): [core.telegram.org/bots/telegram-login](https://core.telegram.org/bots/telegram-login)\\n- Telegram, Login Widget (legacy): [core.telegram.org/widgets/login-legacy](https://core.telegram.org/widgets/login-legacy)\\n- X, OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow with PKCE: [docs.x.com](https://docs.x.com/fundamentals/authentication/oauth-2-0/authorization-code)\\n- Meta, Graph API User reference: [developers.facebook.com/docs/graph-api/reference/user](https://developers.facebook.com/docs/graph-api/reference/user/)\\n- Casdoor source: [`idp/telegram.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/idp/telegram.go), [`idp/goth.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/idp/goth.go), [`idp/custom.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/idp/custom.go)"},{"id":"test-payments-without-a-gateway","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/test-payments-without-a-gateway","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-test-payments-without-a-gateway.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-test-payments-without-a-gateway.mdx","title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","description":"A dummy payment gateway completes checkout without moving money. How the Casdoor Dummy provider works, and when to switch to Stripe test mode or PayPal sandbox.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Payment","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/payment"},{"inline":true,"label":"Testing","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/testing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Stripe","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/stripe"},{"inline":true,"label":"PayPal","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/pay-pal"},{"inline":true,"label":"Subscription","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/subscription"}],"readingTime":8.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"test-payments-without-a-gateway","title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","description":"A dummy payment gateway completes checkout without moving money. How the Casdoor Dummy provider works, and when to switch to Stripe test mode or PayPal sandbox.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Payment","Testing","Stripe","PayPal","Subscription"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","permalink":"/fr/blog/steam-telegram-login"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4 : une nouvelle console, un Marketplace et OAuth pour les serveurs MCP","permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-v4"}},"content":"A dummy payment gateway is a payment provider that accepts every checkout as paid without contacting a bank or card network, so you can test orders, subscriptions and fulfillment with no money and no gateway account. Casdoor ships one: the [Dummy payment provider](/docs/provider/payment/dummy). Add it to a product, buy the product, and Casdoor records the payment and the order as `Paid`, starts the subscription and writes the transaction, all without leaving your server. Use Dummy in development, and switch to a vendor sandbox (Stripe test mode, PayPal sandbox, Paddle sandbox) in staging, where you also need declined cards, 3-D Secure and real redirects.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Dummy, Balance and vendor sandboxes at a glance\\n\\n| Provider | Test environment | How Casdoor picks test or live | Casdoor provider type |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Dummy | None needed, every payment succeeds | Always simulated | `Dummy` |\\n| Balance | Internal, funded by a recharge product | Always internal | `Balance` |\\n| Stripe | Sandbox with `sk_test_` / `pk_test_` keys | By the keys you enter | `Stripe` |\\n| PayPal | Sandbox apps and sandbox accounts | `runmode` other than `prod` uses the sandbox | `PayPal` |\\n| Paddle | Separate sandbox account at `sandbox-vendors.paddle.com` | `runmode` other than `prod` uses `sandbox-api.paddle.com` | `Paddle` |\\n| Adyen | Test environment (`checkout-test.adyen.com`) | `runmode` other than `prod` uses the test environment | `Adyen` |\\n| AirWallex | Separate sandbox account | `runmode` other than `prod` calls `api-demo.airwallex.com` | `AirWallex` |\\n| Lemon Squeezy | Store test mode with test-mode API keys | By the API key you enter | `Lemon Squeezy` |\\n| FastSpring | Test storefront with `.test.` in the host | By the **Host** you enter | `FastSpring` |\\n| Polar | Sandbox at `sandbox.polar.sh` | Not supported, Casdoor always calls `api.polar.sh` | `Polar` |\\n\\n`runmode` is the option in `conf/app.conf` (or the `runmode` environment variable) described in the [configuration reference](/docs/basic/configuration). Its default is `dev`, so a fresh Casdoor talks to the sandboxes of PayPal, Paddle, Adyen and AirWallex until you set it to `prod`.\\n\\n## What a dummy transaction means\\n\\nA dummy transaction runs through the same records as a real purchase (order, payment, subscription, ledger entry) but moves no money. It is different from a sandbox transaction. A sandbox still calls the vendor\'s API, shows the vendor\'s checkout page and sends webhooks; only the money is fake. A dummy transaction never leaves your system.\\n\\nThat difference decides what each one can test. A dummy gateway is good for your own logic: does the plan grant the right role, does the order show up, does the coupon count as used, does the user land on the right page. A sandbox is good for the integration: credentials, redirects, card declines, authentication challenges and webhook delivery. You need both, at different stages.\\n\\n## How the Casdoor Dummy provider works\\n\\nThe Dummy provider has no settings and no payment page. Here is the full flow on Casdoor master:\\n\\n1. When the user pays, the provider\'s `Pay` call returns the Casdoor result page as the payment URL. It packs the price and currency into the order ID instead of creating anything at a vendor.\\n1. Casdoor stores the payment in state `Created` and sends the browser straight to the result page. There is no card form, fake or otherwise.\\n1. The result page sees a `Created` payment and calls `POST /api/notify-payment` about a second later.\\n1. The provider\'s `Notify` call always answers `Paid`, with the price it packed earlier, so the price check passes.\\n1. Casdoor then runs the normal success path: the payment and the order become `Paid`, the subscriptions created for the payment are updated, a `Purchase` transaction is written, product stock is reduced and a coupon on the order is marked as used. If the product is a recharge product, a `Recharge` transaction also adds the amount to the user\'s balance.\\n\\nSo yes, Dummy marks the order as paid right away, and the user sees the success page with no step in between.\\n\\nTwo limits follow from that design. Dummy can\'t fail: there is no way to make it return `Canceled`, `Timeout` or `Error`, so failure paths need a sandbox. And Dummy can\'t renew: Casdoor only allows auto renewal for Balance and Stripe, so a subscription bought with Dummy always has auto renewal off.\\n\\nOn a new database, Casdoor creates a built-in Dummy provider named `provider_payment_dummy` in the `admin` organization, next to a built-in Balance provider. You can also add your own: in **Identity** > **Providers**, set **Category** to `Payment` and **Type** to `Dummy`.\\n\\n### Keep Dummy out of production\\n\\n`/api/notify-payment` is open to anonymous callers, because real gateways call it without a Casdoor session. For a real provider that is safe, since `Notify` checks the payment with the vendor. Dummy checks nothing and reports `Paid`. Any product that lists Dummy in its **Payment providers** shows it at checkout, and anyone who picks it gets the product for free. Remove Dummy from every product before you go live, and do not put it on products in an organization that real users can reach.\\n\\n## Balance: the internal wallet you can fund with Dummy\\n\\nThe [Balance provider](/docs/provider/payment/Balance) lets users pay from their account balance. Casdoor checks the balance first, deducts the price, and marks the payment and order `Paid` in the same request. Balance is a real production feature, not a test tool, but it pairs well with Dummy: put Dummy on a recharge product, \\"top up\\" a test user, and the balance is credited with no money involved.\\n\\nThis matters for subscriptions, because Balance is one of the two providers that support auto renewal. With Dummy plus Balance you can test the whole renewal loop on a laptop with no gateway account.\\n\\n## Vendor sandboxes for staging\\n\\n**Stripe.** Stripe separates test and live data by keys: sandbox keys start with `pk_test_` and `sk_test_`, live keys with `pk_live_` and `sk_live_`, and objects in one mode are not visible in the other. Casdoor uses whatever keys you put in the [Stripe provider](/docs/provider/payment/stripe); `runmode` does not change anything for Stripe. Stripe\'s test cards cover the paths Dummy can\'t: `4242 4242 4242 4242` succeeds, `4000 0000 0000 0002` is declined, and `4000 0000 0000 3220` requires 3-D Secure. Use any future expiry and any CVC. Stripe\'s terms forbid testing in live mode with real card details.\\n\\n**PayPal.** The PayPal sandbox is a separate environment with its own apps and test accounts. PayPal creates a default personal and a default business sandbox account when you first sign in to the developer dashboard; you pay with the personal one. Create the app in sandbox mode, put its credentials in the [PayPal provider](/docs/provider/payment/paypal), and keep `runmode` at `dev`.\\n\\n**Paddle.** The sandbox is a separate account at `sandbox-vendors.paddle.com` with its own keys and products; nothing is shared with live. Paddle\'s test cards include `4242 4242 4242 4242` (succeeds) and `4000 0000 0000 0002` (declined).\\n\\n**Lemon Squeezy** has a test mode per store, and API keys created in test mode only see test data. **FastSpring** test orders go through a test storefront: insert `.test.` into the store host, for example `yourstore.test.onfastspring.com`, and enter that as the provider\'s **Host**. **Polar** has a sandbox with its own tokens, but the Casdoor Polar provider does not select it, so a Polar provider in Casdoor always works against live Polar. **Alipay** in Casdoor is also hard-wired to production.\\n\\n## A test plan for Casdoor products, plans and subscriptions\\n\\n### Development, with Dummy\\n\\n1. Create a [product](/docs/products/product) and add Dummy to its **Payment providers**.\\n1. Create a [plan](/docs/pricing/plan) for that product and a pricing page that lists it (see the [pricing overview](/docs/pricing/overview)).\\n1. As a test user, open the pricing page and buy the plan. Check that the payment and the order are `Paid`, the subscription is `Active`, and a `Purchase` transaction appears on the user.\\n1. Sign in as the user and check that the plan\'s role took effect in your application.\\n1. If the plan has **Is exclusive** on, buy it again and expect the error that the user already has an active subscription.\\n1. Apply a coupon at checkout and check that its usage is recorded.\\n1. Create a recharge product with Dummy, top up the test user, then buy the plan with Balance and **Auto renew** on.\\n1. To test renewal without waiting a month, edit the subscription\'s **End time** on its edit page so it ends within 24 hours. Casdoor checks subscriptions once an hour; expect **Renewal state** `Renewed` and a new subscription for the next period. Then buy the plan again with a balance too low for the next period, move its end time the same way, and expect `Failed` with the reason in **Renewal message**. The [subscription docs](/docs/pricing/subscription#auto-renewal) describe the exact rules.\\n\\n### Staging, with a sandbox\\n\\n1. Run staging with `runmode = dev` and sandbox or test credentials only.\\n1. Replace Dummy with the provider you will use in production, configured with its sandbox keys.\\n1. Repeat the purchase and check that the browser goes to the vendor\'s checkout and comes back to the Casdoor result page.\\n1. With Stripe, pay once with the decline card and once with the 3-D Secure card, and check the payment and order states for each.\\n1. With Stripe and **Auto renew** on, Casdoor saves the card at checkout and charges it itself at renewal, so the end-time trick above works in the sandbox too.\\n\\n### Going live\\n\\nRemove Dummy from all products, set `runmode = prod` if you use PayPal, Paddle, Adyen or AirWallex, and swap in live keys. Check the [payment records](/docs/products/payment) of the first real orders.\\n\\nIf you also test sign-up email in the same environment, a local mail catcher keeps those messages off real inboxes; see [MailHog vs Mailpit](/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit).\\n\\n## R\xe9f\xe9rences {#references}\\n\\n- Casdoor source: [`pp/dummy.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/pp/dummy.go), [`pp/balance.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/pp/balance.go), [`object/order_pay.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/order_pay.go), [`object/payment.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/payment.go), [`object/subscription_renew.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/subscription_renew.go)\\n- Casdoor docs: [Payment providers](/docs/provider/payment/overview), [Dummy](/docs/provider/payment/dummy), [Balance](/docs/provider/payment/Balance), [Subscription](/docs/pricing/subscription)\\n- Stripe: [Testing](https://docs.stripe.com/testing), [API keys](https://docs.stripe.com/keys)\\n- PayPal: [Sandbox](https://developer.paypal.com/tools/sandbox/), [Sandbox accounts](https://developer.paypal.com/tools/sandbox/accounts/)\\n- Paddle: [Sandbox](https://developer.paddle.com/build/tools/sandbox)\\n- Lemon Squeezy: [Test mode](https://docs.lemonsqueezy.com/help/getting-started/test-mode)\\n- FastSpring: [Test orders](https://developer.fastspring.com/docs/test-orders)\\n- Polar: [Sandbox](https://polar.sh/docs/integrate/sandbox)\\n- Adyen: [Live endpoints](https://docs.adyen.com/development-resources/live-endpoints)"},{"id":"casdoor-v4","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-v4","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","title":"Casdoor v4 : une nouvelle console, un Marketplace et OAuth pour les serveurs MCP","description":"Casdoor v4.0 est sorti le 1er septembre. Deux mois et 25 versions plus tard, nous en sommes \xe0 la v4.25 ; c\'est donc le bon moment pour revenir sur ce qui a chang\xe9. En r\xe9sum\xe9 : la console d\'administration a \xe9t\xe9 reconstruite, l\'auto-h\xe9bergement est devenu beaucoup moins fastidieux, il existe d\xe9sormais un Marketplace pour les int\xe9grations et les th\xe8mes de connexion, et Casdoor peut agir comme serveur d\'autorisation pour les serveurs MCP et les agents IA.","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"Self-hosting","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/self-hosting"}],"readingTime":5.64,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-v4","title":"Casdoor v4 : une nouvelle console, un Marketplace et OAuth pour les serveurs MCP","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Release","IAM","SSO","OAuth","MCP","Self-hosting"],"date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","permalink":"/fr/blog/test-payments-without-a-gateway"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ex\xe9cution de Casdoor sur Kubernetes : Helm Chart officiel et Configuration as Code","permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"}},"content":"Casdoor v4.0 est sorti le 1er septembre. Deux mois et 25 versions plus tard, nous en sommes \xe0 la v4.25 ; c\'est donc le bon moment pour revenir sur ce qui a chang\xe9. En r\xe9sum\xe9 : la console d\'administration a \xe9t\xe9 reconstruite, l\'auto-h\xe9bergement est devenu beaucoup moins fastidieux, il existe d\xe9sormais un Marketplace pour les int\xe9grations et les th\xe8mes de connexion, et Casdoor peut agir comme serveur d\'autorisation pour les serveurs MCP et les agents IA.\\n\\nSi vous ne l\'avez jamais utilis\xe9 : Casdoor est une plateforme open-source de gestion des identit\xe9s et des acc\xe8s (IAM) \xe9crite en Go. C\'est un fournisseur d\'identit\xe9 complet, pas une biblioth\xe8que ni un proxy de connexion. Les utilisateurs r\xe9sident dans Casdoor, Casdoor \xe9met les jetons, et vos applications communiquent avec lui via OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, SAML 2.0, CAS, LDAP ou SCIM. L\'autorisation est g\xe9r\xe9e par [Apache Casbin](https://casbin.apache.org), de sorte que les mod\xe8les ACL, RBAC et ABAC fonctionnent tous. Il est sous licence Apache-2.0 et figure dans le CNCF Landscape.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Une console et des pages de connexion reconstruites {#a-rebuilt-console-and-sign-in-pages}\\n\\nLa v4.0 a remplac\xe9 la console d\'administration Ant Design par une nouvelle construite avec shadcn/ui, Tailwind et Vite (actuellement sur React 19). Elle dispose d\'une palette de commandes, de la s\xe9lection multiple de lignes, de la validation de formulaire en ligne, de formulaires et tableaux \xe0 deux colonnes o\xf9 vous pouvez masquer des colonnes. Le changement de langue ne recharge plus la page.\\n\\nLes pages de connexion, d\'inscription et de r\xe9initialisation du mot de passe ont \xe9galement \xe9t\xe9 redessin\xe9es. Le mode sombre fonctionne correctement, et les pages sont utilisables sur un t\xe9l\xe9phone.\\n\\nSi vous effectuez une mise \xe0 niveau depuis la v3, consultez d\'abord le guide de mise \xe0 niveau li\xe9 depuis le README. La principale chose \xe0 v\xe9rifier est le chemin de la compilation frontend si vous servez des fichiers statiques via un CDN ou un reverse proxy.\\n\\n## Un auto-h\xe9bergement simplifi\xe9 {#easier-to-self-host}\\n\\nUne grande partie du travail de la v4 provient des tickets et des fils de forum de personnes h\xe9bergeant elles-m\xeames Casdoor :\\n\\n- **Pas de mot de passe par d\xe9faut.** Lors de la premi\xe8re visite, Casdoor vous demande de d\xe9finir le mot de passe `admin`, ou vous pouvez le d\xe9finir via `initAdminPassword`.\\n- **D\xe9ploiement sur un sous-chemin.** D\xe9finissez `basePath` pour servir Casdoor \xe0 `https://example.com/auth/` au lieu de son propre h\xf4te.\\n- **Forward auth.** Casdoor dispose d\'un point de terminaison forward auth pour Traefik, Caddy et Nginx, ce qui vous permet de placer derri\xe8re lui des applications n\'ayant pas leur propre syst\xe8me de connexion, avec des chemins publics et des r\xe8gles par site.\\n- **Tags Docker.** Les images sont d\xe9sormais \xe9galement tagu\xe9es `X`, `X.Y` et `X.Y.Z`, afin que vous puissiez suivre une version majeure ou mineure.\\n- **Helm.** Le chart a \xe9t\xe9 d\xe9plac\xe9 dans le d\xe9p\xf4t principal, est publi\xe9 sur GHCR et [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) \xe0 chaque release, et peut conserver les organisations, les applications et les utilisateurs comme configuration. Voir [Ex\xe9cution de Casdoor sur Kubernetes](/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes).\\n- **Service Windows.** Le binaire peut s\'ex\xe9cuter en tant que service Windows.\\n- **Mises \xe0 jour.** La page d\'informations syst\xe8me affiche la derni\xe8re release et peut mettre \xe0 jour un binaire officiel en un clic.\\n- **Op\xe9rations.** V\xe9rifications de sant\xe9 des conteneurs, compteurs Prometheus pour les succ\xe8s et \xe9checs de connexion, logs d\'audit transmis vers Syslog, et prise en charge de Redis Cluster.\\n\\n## Marketplace\\n\\nLe nouveau [Marketplace](https://marketplace.casdoor.com) permet \xe0 un administrateur d\'installer des int\xe9grations directement depuis Casdoor : mod\xe8les d\'applications, fournisseurs d\'identit\xe9 et th\xe8mes de connexion. L\'installation pr\xe9remplit la configuration pour vous, et la d\xe9sinstallation restaure la configuration pr\xe9c\xe9dente.\\n\\nIl y a actuellement plus de 20 th\xe8mes de connexion. Vous pouvez pr\xe9visualiser un th\xe8me sur votre propre page de connexion avant de l\'installer. Les administrateurs d\'organisation peuvent \xe9galement personnaliser les pages de connexion avec leurs propres polices, images et HTML, dans le cadre d\'une liste blanche qui bloque les formulaires et les scripts. La d\xe9mo en lecture seule sur [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) fait d\xe9filer les th\xe8mes si vous souhaitez les voir.\\n\\n## OAuth pour les serveurs MCP et les agents IA {#oauth-for-mcp-servers-and-ai-agents}\\n\\nLa sp\xe9cification MCP demande aux serveurs MCP distants d\'utiliser OAuth, et les clients MCP ne connaissent g\xe9n\xe9ralement pas \xe0 l\'avance quel serveur d\'autorisation ils rencontreront. Casdoor couvre d\xe9sormais les \xe9l\xe9ments n\xe9cessaires :\\n\\n- Flux de code d\'autorisation OAuth 2.1 avec PKCE, et une page de consentement o\xf9 les utilisateurs peuvent n\'accorder qu\'une partie des scopes demand\xe9s\\n- Enregistrement dynamique des clients (RFC 7591) et gestion des clients (RFC 7592)\\n- Client ID Metadata Documents : un client MCP peut utiliser une URL comme `client_id` sans s\'enregistrer au pr\xe9alable\\n- Authentification du client `private_key_jwt` pour les clients enregistr\xe9s dynamiquement\\n- Indicateurs de ressource (RFC 8707) et m\xe9tadonn\xe9es des ressources prot\xe9g\xe9es (RFC 9728)\\n- \xc9change de jeton (RFC 8693), y compris les ID-JAGs pour l\'autorisation g\xe9r\xe9e par l\'entreprise, afin que l\'IdP d\'une organisation puisse d\xe9cider quels agents peuvent acc\xe9der \xe0 quels serveurs MCP\\n\\nPointez les m\xe9tadonn\xe9es de la ressource prot\xe9g\xe9e de votre serveur MCP vers Casdoor, et la connexion, le consentement, l\'\xe9mission et la validation des jetons sont g\xe9r\xe9s pour vous. Casdoor ex\xe9cute \xe9galement son propre serveur MCP, afin qu\'un assistant tel que Claude Code ou Cursor puisse g\xe9rer les utilisateurs, les applications et les autorisations via celui-ci.\\n\\n## Autres travaux sur les protocoles {#other-protocol-work}\\n\\n- SAML Single Logout et SSO initi\xe9 par l\'IdP\\n- OIDC `prompt=none`, meilleure conformit\xe9 pour le flux hybride, `prompt` et `max_age`, et le param\xe8tre `iss` de la RFC 9207\\n- R\xe9vocation de jeton (RFC 7009), DPoP et le flux d\'autorisation d\'appareil\\n- Connexion par lien magique, v\xe9rification de l\'historique des mots de passe, et passkeys partag\xe9s entre sous-domaines fr\xe8res\\n- Connexion exclusive avec un nombre maximum de sessions par utilisateur\\n\\n## Essayer {#try-it}\\n\\nLe moyen le plus rapide d\'explorer :\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 casbin/casdoor-all-in-one\\n```\\n\\nOuvrez ensuite http://localhost:8000. L\'image inclut SQLite et des donn\xe9es d\'exemple, utilisez-la donc pour essayer, pas pour la production. Le [guide d\'installation](/docs/basic/server-installation) couvre les d\xe9ploiements r\xe9els.\\n\\nIl existe deux d\xe9mos en ligne : [demo.casdoor.com](https://demo.casdoor.com) est modifiable et se r\xe9initialise toutes les cinq minutes, et [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) est en lecture seule.\\n\\nSi vous pr\xe9f\xe9rez ne pas l\'h\xe9berger vous-m\xeame, [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing) vous offre une instance d\xe9di\xe9e et manag\xe9e \xe0 prix forfaitaire sans frais par utilisateur. Les nouveaux comptes re\xe7oivent 10 $ de cr\xe9dit pour l\'essayer.\\n\\n## Liens {#links}\\n\\n- GitHub : https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor\\n- Notes de version : https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/releases\\n- Docs : https://casdoor.ai/docs/overview\\n- Discord : https://discord.gg/5rPsrAzK7S\\n\\nLes rapports de bugs et les demandes de fonctionnalit\xe9s sont bienvenus sur GitHub. Si Casdoor vous est utile, une \xe9toile aide d\'autres personnes \xe0 le trouver."},{"id":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","title":"Ex\xe9cution de Casdoor sur Kubernetes : Helm Chart officiel et Configuration as Code","description":"Casdoor dispose d\'un Helm chart officiel. Il se trouve dans le d\xe9p\xf4t principal sous manifests/casdoor, est publi\xe9 avec chaque version de Casdoor sur GHCR, et est r\xe9f\xe9renc\xe9 sur Artifact Hub. \xc0 partir de Casdoor v4.18.0, le chart peut \xe9galement contenir vos organisations, applications, utilisateurs et fournisseurs comme configuration d\xe9clarative : modifiez-les dans le fichier values, ex\xe9cutez helm upgrade, et les pods en cours appliquent la modification sans red\xe9marrage.","date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/kubernetes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Helm","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/helm"},{"inline":true,"label":"GitOps","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/git-ops"},{"inline":true,"label":"Configuration as Code","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/configuration-as-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":5.97,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","title":"Ex\xe9cution de Casdoor sur Kubernetes : Helm Chart officiel et Configuration as Code","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Kubernetes","Helm","GitOps","Configuration as Code","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4 : une nouvelle console, un Marketplace et OAuth pour les serveurs MCP","permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-v4"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor rejoint le CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"}},"content":"Casdoor dispose d\'un Helm chart officiel. Il se trouve dans le d\xe9p\xf4t principal sous [`manifests/casdoor`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor), est publi\xe9 avec chaque version de Casdoor sur GHCR, et est r\xe9f\xe9renc\xe9 sur [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor). \xc0 partir de Casdoor v4.18.0, le chart peut \xe9galement contenir vos organisations, applications, utilisateurs et fournisseurs comme configuration d\xe9clarative : modifiez-les dans le fichier values, ex\xe9cutez `helm upgrade`, et les pods en cours appliquent la modification sans red\xe9marrage.\\n\\nCet article d\xe9taille un d\xe9ploiement Kubernetes depuis le premier `helm install` jusqu\'\xe0 une configuration que vous pouvez conserver dans Git.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Installation {#install}\\n\\nVous avez besoin de Helm v3.8+ et d\'un cluster :\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nLa version du chart correspond \xe0 la version de Casdoor, donc `--version 4.18.0` installe Casdoor 4.18.0. Sans Ingress ou Gateway, le service n\'est accessible qu\'\xe0 l\'int\xe9rieur du cluster. Pour tester, transf\xe9rez un port :\\n\\n```shell\\nkubectl port-forward svc/casdoor 8000:8000\\n```\\n\\nPuis ouvrez `http://localhost:8000`. Lors de la premi\xe8re visite, Casdoor vous demande de d\xe9finir le mot de passe de `built-in/admin` ; connectez-vous ensuite avec celui-ci. Voir [Se connecter \xe0 la console d\'administration](/docs/basic/server-installation#sign-in).\\n\\n## Une configuration de production {#a-production-setup}\\n\\nLes param\xe8tres par d\xe9faut sont destin\xe9s \xe0 un essai rapide : Casdoor stocke ses donn\xe9es dans SQLite \xe0 l\'int\xe9rieur du conteneur, elles sont donc perdues lorsque le pod est remplac\xe9. Pour un d\xe9ploiement r\xe9el, placez les param\xe8tres ci-dessous dans un fichier `values.yaml` et installez avec `helm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor -f values.yaml`.\\n\\n### Base de donn\xe9es externe {#external-database}\\n\\n```yaml\\ndatabase:\\n  driver: postgres # or mysql, cockroachdb\\n  host: postgres.db.svc.cluster.local\\n  user: casdoor\\n  password: change-me\\n  databaseName: casdoor\\n  sslMode: require\\n```\\n\\nPour \xe9viter de placer le mot de passe de la base de donn\xe9es dans le fichier values, mettez tout le `app.conf` dans un Secret et d\xe9finissez `configFromSecret` sur son nom.\\n\\n### Exposer Casdoor {#exposing-casdoor}\\n\\nLe chart supporte \xe0 la fois un Ingress classique et l\'API Gateway. Avec un contr\xf4leur Ingress et cert-manager :\\n\\n```yaml\\ningress:\\n  enabled: true\\n  className: nginx\\n  annotations:\\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod\\n  hosts:\\n    - host: door.example.com\\n      paths:\\n        - path: /\\n          pathType: Prefix\\n  tls:\\n    - secretName: casdoor-tls\\n      hosts:\\n        - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nAvec l\'API Gateway (Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium, Kong, NGINX Gateway Fabric et autres), attachez une `HTTPRoute` \xe0 une Gateway existante :\\n\\n```yaml\\ngatewayApi:\\n  enabled: true\\n  parentRefs:\\n    - name: my-gateway\\n      namespace: gateway-system\\n  hostnames:\\n    - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nLe chart peut \xe9galement cr\xe9er la Gateway lui-m\xeame et ajouter une redirection HTTP vers HTTPS, voir le [guide Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm#exposing-casdoor).\\n\\n### Plusieurs r\xe9pliques {#more-than-one-replica}\\n\\nPar d\xe9faut, Casdoor conserve les sessions de connexion sur le disque local. Avant d\'ex\xe9cuter plusieurs r\xe9pliques, pointez toutes les instances vers le m\xeame Redis, afin qu\'un utilisateur reste connect\xe9 quel que soit le pod servant la requ\xeate. Chaque param\xe8tre `app.conf` peut \xeatre pass\xe9 comme variable d\'environnement, donc le point d\'acc\xe8s peut provenir d\'un Secret :\\n\\n```yaml\\nreplicaCount: 3\\n\\nenvFromSecret:\\n  - name: redisEndpoint\\n    secretName: casdoor-redis\\n    key: endpoint # host:port, or host:port,db,password\\n```\\n\\nLe m\xeame Redis est utilis\xe9 pour l\'\xe9tat du flux de p\xe9riph\xe9rique OAuth. Une fois Redis en place, `autoscaling.enabled: true` ajoute un HorizontalPodAutoscaler.\\n\\n## Configuration as code\\n\\nCliquer pour assembler les organisations et les applications dans l\'interface web convient pour un premier test, mais la plupart des \xe9quipes souhaitent que la configuration de leur fournisseur d\'identit\xe9 soit revue et versionn\xe9e comme le reste de leur infrastructure. Les valeurs `initData` permettent cela :\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  data:\\n    organizations:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: acme\\n        displayName: Acme\\n        passwordType: bcrypt\\n    applications:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: app-acme-portal\\n        organization: acme\\n        displayName: Acme Portal\\n        redirectUris:\\n          - https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\n    users:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: alice\\n        displayName: Alice\\n        password: change-me\\n        signupApplication: app-acme-portal\\n    roles:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: admins\\n        displayName: Admins\\n        users: [acme/alice]\\n        isEnabled: true\\n```\\n\\nLe chart stocke les donn\xe9es dans un Secret et les monte dans les pods. Casdoor les applique au d\xe9marrage, puis les v\xe9rifie toutes les 30 secondes (`initData.watchInterval`). Lorsque vous modifiez les donn\xe9es et ex\xe9cutez `helm upgrade`, kubelet met \xe0 jour le Secret mont\xe9 et les pods en cours l\'appliquent sans red\xe9marrage.\\n\\nQuelques r\xe8gles rendent cette utilisation s\xfbre aux c\xf4t\xe9s de l\'interface web :\\n\\n- **Seuls les champs que vous \xe9crivez sont g\xe9r\xe9s.** Un objet existant re\xe7oit les champs d\xe9finis dans le fichier ; ses autres champs conservent les valeurs \xe9dit\xe9es dans l\'interface. Vous pouvez g\xe9rer uniquement les URI de redirection d\'une application, et laisser le reste \xe0 la console d\'administration.\\n- **Les mots de passe sont des mots de passe initiaux.** Le `password` d\'un utilisateur n\'est utilis\xe9 que lors de la cr\xe9ation de l\'utilisateur, ce qui permet aux utilisateurs de le modifier par la suite.\\n- **Rien n\'est supprim\xe9.** La suppression d\'un objet du fichier ne le supprime pas de Casdoor.\\n- **Les erreurs n\'arr\xeatent pas Casdoor.** Si l\'application d\'un objet \xe9choue, l\'erreur est journalis\xe9e et Casdoor continue de fonctionner ; les donn\xe9es sont r\xe9essay\xe9es \xe0 chaque v\xe9rification jusqu\'\xe0 leur application r\xe9ussie.\\n\\nLe format du fichier est celui que Casdoor a toujours utilis\xe9 pour [l\'initialisation des donn\xe9es](/docs/deployment/data-initialization) : organisations, applications, utilisateurs, fournisseurs, certificats, r\xf4les, permissions, groupes, serveurs LDAP, webhooks, plans, tarifs et plus encore, avec les m\xeames noms de champs que l\'API REST. Une fa\xe7on rapide de d\xe9marrer consiste \xe0 exporter une instance existante avec `casdoor -export` et \xe0 conserver les objets que vous souhaitez g\xe9rer.\\n\\nPour garder les donn\xe9es hors du fichier values, par exemple lorsqu\'elles sont g\xe9r\xe9es par Sealed Secrets ou External Secrets, cr\xe9ez vous-m\xeame le Secret :\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  existingSecret: casdoor-init-data\\n  existingSecretKey: init_data.yaml\\n```\\n\\nLa m\xeame fonctionnalit\xe9 fonctionne sans Kubernetes. Montez un fichier JSON ou YAML et d\xe9finissez :\\n\\n```ini\\ninitDataFile = /init-data/init_data.yaml\\ninitDataMerge = true\\ninitDataWatchInterval = 30\\n```\\n\\n## Ou utilisez Terraform {#or-use-terraform}\\n\\nSi vous pr\xe9f\xe9rez `terraform plan`, l\'importation d\'objets existants et la d\xe9tection de d\xe9rives, le [provider Terraform](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/casdoor/casdoor) officiel g\xe8re les m\xeames objets via l\'API Casdoor :\\n\\n```terraform\\nresource \\"casdoor_application\\" \\"portal\\" {\\n  name          = \\"app-acme-portal\\"\\n  organization  = \\"acme\\"\\n  redirect_uris = [\\"https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\"]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nUtilisez les valeurs Helm lorsque la configuration doit \xeatre livr\xe9e avec le d\xe9ploiement, et Terraform lorsque Casdoor fait partie de nombreux syst\xe8mes que vous g\xe9rez d\xe9j\xe0 avec lui. Consultez le [guide Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform).\\n\\n## Migration depuis l\'ancien chart {#coming-from-the-old-chart}\\n\\nJusqu\'\xe0 la v4.15.0, le chart \xe9tait publi\xe9 sous `oci://registry-1.docker.io/casbin/casdoor-helm-charts`, et le d\xe9p\xf4t s\xe9par\xe9 `casdoor/casdoor-helm` est d\xe9sormais archiv\xe9. Le chart Docker Hub re\xe7oit toujours chaque nouvelle version, mais les nouvelles installations doivent utiliser celui h\xe9berg\xe9 sur GHCR. Migrer une release existante est une mise \xe0 niveau classique, et les noms des ressources restent inchang\xe9s :\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm upgrade casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor --version <version>\\n```\\n\\n## Liens {#links}\\n\\n- Chart Helm : [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [source](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor)\\n- Guides : [Essayer avec Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm) \xb7 [Initialisation des donn\xe9es et configuration as code](/docs/deployment/data-initialization#configuration-as-code) \xb7 [Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform)\\n- Vous ne souhaitez pas le g\xe9rer vous-m\xeame ? [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing?utm_source=casdoor.ai&utm_medium=blog&utm_content=kubernetes) vous offre une instance Casdoor d\xe9di\xe9e que nous h\xe9bergeons et maintenons \xe0 jour pour vous."},{"id":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","title":"Casdoor rejoint le CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","description":"Le 22 f\xe9vrier 2026, Casdoor a rejoint le CNCF Cloud Native Landscape, dans la cat\xe9gorie Security & Compliance sous Provisioning. Le Landscape regroupe des projets et produits cloud-natifs largement utilis\xe9s ; son inclusion aide les utilisateurs et les entreprises \xe0 d\xe9couvrir et \xe9valuer des solutions pour construire des syst\xe8mes cloud-natifs.","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CNCF","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/cncf"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cloud Native","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/cloud-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"Landscape","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/landscape"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":2.2,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","title":"Casdoor rejoint le CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CNCF","Cloud Native","Landscape","IAM","SSO","Security"],"date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ex\xe9cution de Casdoor sur Kubernetes : Helm Chart officiel et Configuration as Code","permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Am\xe9lioration de la s\xe9curit\xe9 des applications et de l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur avec l\'authentification faciale SSO de Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Le 22 f\xe9vrier 2026, **Casdoor** a rejoint le [CNCF Cloud Native Landscape](https://landscape.cncf.io/), dans la cat\xe9gorie **Security & Compliance** sous Provisioning. Le Landscape regroupe des projets et produits cloud-natifs largement utilis\xe9s ; son inclusion aide les utilisateurs et les entreprises \xe0 d\xe9couvrir et \xe9valuer des solutions pour construire des syst\xe8mes cloud-natifs.\\n\\n[https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<img src=\\"/img/blog/cncf-landscape-casdoor.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor on CNCF Landscape - Security and Compliance section\\" style={{maxWidth: \'100%\', height: \'auto\', border: \'1px solid #eee\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n\\n_Casdoor on the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape (Security & Compliance)_\\n\\n## \xc0 propos de Casdoor {#about-casdoor}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/casdoor-logo_1185x256.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nCasdoor est une plateforme open-source, orient\xe9e interface utilisateur, de gestion des identit\xe9s et des acc\xe8s (IAM) / d\'authentification unique (SSO) avec une interface web. Elle prend en charge OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML et CAS, ainsi que de nombreuses connexions sociales (Google, GitHub, WeChat, etc.), et vous aide \xe0 mettre en place la gestion des utilisateurs et le SSO en quelques \xe9tapes. Casdoor est d\xe9velopp\xe9 par [Casbin](https://casbin.org) en Golang et React. Il utilise une architecture s\xe9parant le front-end du back-end, supporte la haute concurrence et est activement maintenu par la communaut\xe9. Le projet a re\xe7u plus de 13k \xe9toiles GitHub.\\n\\n## \xc0 propos de CNCF et du Cloud Native Landscape {#about-cncf-and-the-cloud-native-landscape}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1.5rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://www.cncf.io/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/cncf-main-site-logo.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF - Cloud Native Computing Foundation\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n<img src=\\"https://landscape.cncf.io/images/logo_header.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF Cloud Native Landscape\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nLa **Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)** est un projet de la Linux Foundation qui promeut les technologies cloud-natives. Sa vision est de rendre le cloud natif omnipr\xe9sent. La CNCF maintient le **Cloud Native Landscape** ([https://landscape.cncf.io](https://landscape.cncf.io)), qui classe et suit les projets et produits dans l\'\xe9cosyst\xe8me cloud-native et sert de carte de r\xe9f\xe9rence pour les d\xe9veloppeurs et les entreprises.\\n\\n## Perspectives d\'avenir {#looking-ahead}\\n\\nRejoindre le CNCF Landscape refl\xe8te les progr\xe8s de la maturit\xe9 de Casdoor et de sa communaut\xe9. Nous continuerons \xe0 am\xe9liorer Casdoor et son int\xe9gration avec les workflows cloud-natifs, et nous accueillons avec plaisir davantage d\'utilisateurs et de contributeurs pour rejoindre la communaut\xe9.\\n\\nLiens connexes :\\n\\n- [Casdoor](https://casdoor.org) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [Documentation](https://casdoor.org/docs) \xb7 [CNCF Landscape \u2013 Casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?selected=casdoor)"},{"id":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","title":"Am\xe9lioration de la s\xe9curit\xe9 des applications et de l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur avec l\'authentification faciale SSO de Casdoor","description":"\xc0 une \xe9poque o\xf9 la gestion de l\'identit\xe9 num\xe9rique est cruciale, les solutions de Single Sign-On (SSO) comme Casdoor r\xe9volutionnent la mani\xe8re dont les utilisateurs interagissent avec plusieurs applications. Le SSO de Casdoor simplifie le processus de connexion en permettant aux utilisateurs de s\'authentifier une seule fois pour acc\xe9der \xe0 plusieurs services. Cela am\xe9liore non seulement la commodit\xe9 pour l\'utilisateur, mais augmente \xe9galement l\'engagement et la satisfaction.","date":"2023-12-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Single-Sign-On","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/single-sign-on"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face Recogniztion","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/face-recogniztion"},{"inline":true,"label":"FaceID","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/face-id"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/face"},{"inline":true,"label":"Machine Learning","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/machine-learning"}],"readingTime":2.07,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"James Lawson","title":"Product Manager","url":"https://casdoor.org","imageURL":"https://cdn.casbin.com/casdoor/static/admin.png","key":"James Lawson","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","title":"Am\xe9lioration de la s\xe9curit\xe9 des applications et de l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur avec l\'authentification faciale SSO de Casdoor","authors":["James Lawson"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Single-Sign-On","Face Recogniztion","FaceID","Face","Machine Learning"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor rejoint le CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/fr/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"},"nextItem":{"title":"Bienvenue","permalink":"/fr/blog/welcome"}},"content":"\xc0 une \xe9poque o\xf9 la gestion de l\'identit\xe9 num\xe9rique est cruciale, les solutions de Single Sign-On (SSO) comme Casdoor r\xe9volutionnent la mani\xe8re dont les utilisateurs interagissent avec plusieurs applications. Le SSO de Casdoor simplifie le processus de connexion en permettant aux utilisateurs de s\'authentifier une seule fois pour acc\xe9der \xe0 plusieurs services. Cela am\xe9liore non seulement la commodit\xe9 pour l\'utilisateur, mais augmente \xe9galement l\'engagement et la satisfaction.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nPour les entreprises, le SSO de Casdoor rationalise la gestion des identifiants des utilisateurs, am\xe9liorant la s\xe9curit\xe9 et simplifiant l\'int\xe9gration de nouveaux utilisateurs. Il contre le risque associ\xe9 aux mots de passe faibles ou recycl\xe9s en centralisant la gestion des utilisateurs.\\n\\nCependant, face \xe0 la croissance des menaces en ligne, int\xe9grer des mesures de s\xe9curit\xe9 robustes dans les syst\xe8mes SSO comme Casdoor est vital. C\'est l\xe0 que la technologie d\'authentification faciale entre en jeu, ajoutant une couche suppl\xe9mentaire de protection tout en maintenant des interactions conviviales avec l\'utilisateur.\\n\\nL\'authentification faciale utilise des caract\xe9ristiques faciales uniques pour confirmer les identit\xe9s des utilisateurs. Elle compare les images en direct avec des mod\xe8les faciaux stock\xe9s, offrant un moyen s\xe9curis\xe9 mais simple d\'authentifier les utilisateurs. Cette m\xe9thode pr\xe9sente une grande pr\xe9cision, m\xeame dans des conditions vari\xe9es, et est largement adopt\xe9e dans les appareils grand public modernes.\\n\\nL\'int\xe9gration de l\'authentification faciale dans le syst\xe8me SSO de Casdoor promet une exp\xe9rience utilisateur s\xe9curis\xe9e, efficace et fluide. Pour garantir des performances optimales, il est crucial de se concentrer sur :\\n\\n1. _Mesures anti-spoofing_ : La mise en \u0153uvre de la d\xe9tection de vivacit\xe9, comme le suivi des mouvements des yeux ou des gestes al\xe9atoires, est essentielle pour contrer les tentatives de spoofing et maintenir l\'int\xe9grit\xe9 du syst\xe8me.\\n\\n2. _Mises \xe0 jour r\xe9guli\xe8res pour une pr\xe9cision am\xe9lior\xe9e_ : Garder la technologie d\'authentification faciale \xe0 jour avec les derni\xe8res avanc\xe9es en IA et en biom\xe9trie assure la pr\xe9cision et am\xe9liore l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur. Il est vital d\'utiliser des ensembles de donn\xe9es diversifi\xe9s pour la formation des algorithmes afin de pr\xe9venir les biais.\\n\\n3. _\xc9quilibrer la s\xe9curit\xe9 et la commodit\xe9_ : Les meilleurs syst\xe8mes trouvent un \xe9quilibre parfait entre des mesures de s\xe9curit\xe9 strictes et la commodit\xe9 pour l\'utilisateur. Cette approche encourage l\'adoption par les utilisateurs en garantissant un processus d\'authentification simple sans compromettre la s\xe9curit\xe9.\\n\\nEn conclusion, en int\xe9grant l\'authentification faciale avec le SSO de Casdoor, les entreprises peuvent offrir une exp\xe9rience de connexion s\xe9curis\xe9e, efficace et conviviale. Cela non seulement renforce la s\xe9curit\xe9, mais s\'aligne \xe9galement avec les attentes des utilisateurs modernes dans le monde num\xe9rique."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/fr/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/fr/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","title":"Bienvenue","description":"Bienvenue sur le site officiel de Casdoor !\u{1F44B}","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Authentication","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/authentication"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth2.0","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"Casbin","permalink":"/fr/blog/tags/casbin"}],"readingTime":1.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"ErikQQY","title":"Casbin Member","url":"https://github.com/ErikQQY","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52615090?s=120&v=4","key":"ErikQQY","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Bienvenue","authors":["ErikQQY"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Authentication","OAuth2.0","Casbin"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Am\xe9lioration de la s\xe9curit\xe9 des applications et de l\'exp\xe9rience utilisateur avec l\'authentification faciale SSO de Casdoor","permalink":"/fr/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"Bienvenue sur le site officiel de Casdoor !\u{1F44B}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Qu\'est-ce que Casdoor ? {#what-is-casdoor}\\n\\nCasdoor is a UI-first [Identity Access Management (IAM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_management) / [Single-Sign-On (SSO)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_sign-on) platform based on OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML and CAS.\\n\\nCasdoor est d\xe9velopp\xe9 par [Casbin](https://casbin.org) en utilisant Golang et React.\\n\\nCasdoor vise \xe0 fournir une plateforme d\'authentification puissante pour g\xe9rer la gestion des utilisateurs.\\n\\n## Pourquoi utiliser Casdoor ? {#why-use-casdoor}\\n\\n* \u26A1\uFE0F Fonctionnalit\xe9 puissante et configuration simple. With support for  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_google.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_github.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_facebook.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_twitter.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_linkedin.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_weibo.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wechat.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_qq.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_dingtalk.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>   <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitee.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wecom.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img><img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitlab.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> sign in and traditional username/password sign in, Casdoor helps you implement your users management system just in a few steps.\\n\\n* \u{1F4A5} Architecture de s\xe9paration front-end back-end. Casdoor fournit une interface utilisateur web man\u0153uvrable et supporte une haute concurrence.\\n\\n* \u{1F942} Communaut\xe9 open source active. Casdoor est open source et activement maintenu.\\n\\n* \u{1F310} Support multilingue. Casdoor prend en charge plusieurs langues, y compris le chinois, l\'anglais, le fran\xe7ais et bien d\'autres.\\n\\n\\n## Comment utiliser Casdoor ? {#how-to-use-casdoor}\\n\\nVous pouvez suivre la documentation d\xe9taill\xe9e pour en savoir plus sur l\'[API Casdoor](https://door.casdoor.com/swagger/) et [installer votre propre instance Casdoor](/docs/basic/server-installation). Ou utilisez les [SDK Casdoor](/docs/how-to-connect/sdk) pour impl\xe9menter votre propre application."}]}}')}}]);